      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The New ' Fascinatiobe—Pata where Mr. Wilbur Wright has been practising aeroplane flights, is but a short journey from Biarritz, and thus altered an easy opportunity for King Edward to witness the American aviator's feats. When King Alfonso visited the aero- drome at Pan, he was anxious to go with Mr. Wright, but was prevented by a promise to his wife not to do so. The whole force of Spanish royal etiquette has frequently to be set in motton to prevent the young King from risking his life; either in racing motors, or going tip in balloons, or under the sea in a submarine, but 'it was only the promise to Queen Victoria that restrained him from going up with Mr. Wright. There will soon he other wives exacting similar promises from husbands, for the opening of the Aero Exhibition at Olympia reminds us that, makers are now prepared to supply flying machines from £l5O upwards. It is how- ever, left to the purchasers to demonstrate their powers of flight, and there is, as yet, no great rush to emulate the feats of Mr. Wright, who is expected to give an exhibition of his iiereplane flight in this country. The Spanish Wife.—There is no country in Europe, not excepting France, in which the wife has mote influence than in Spain. The ore idea of a husband is to consider his wire on all occasions, and when King Alfonso deterred to his young Queen's wish that he should not go up in Mr. Wright's aeroplane, his acquiescence was typical of the Spanish husband. There is little evidence as yet of the feminist movement in Spain, and women there would scarcely understand what is meant by such expressions as that "home life is played out," which one often hears in this country. To the Spanish wife home is everything and no one ques- tions her authority. She reigns supreme and is invari- ably adored by husband and children. "Madrid."—Yet another eadditis n to the 'Spanish series (Mr. John Lane) has made its apeeinance, namely "Mad' id " by Mr. A:F. Calvert. The volume is a most attractive inAructure, and interesting one, and it is good to learn that yet another half a dozen volumes have to be published, before this delightful sissies is complete. Toe Silent Sufferers.—While all sorts and conditions of men are endeavouring to get as much as they can out of the Government and especially from the Chancellor of the Exchequer, there is one class whose interests are always in danger ef being oveilooked. It is that of those people who are obliged to maintain a respectable appearance upon small means, the silent class, whom the Medical Aid Society for Necessitous gentlewomen seeks and help. At the annual meeting of the Society it was stated by Mrs. isiaurice Glyn, that some of the ladies who applied for medical treatment were incapacitated not by disease, but by starvation, and Canon E. Holmes told a pathetic story of a Girton girl, an orphan, who, while he was talking to her in the hull, fainted through want of food. There are many such people, whose sufferings never became known to the world. Because of their reserve, it ig difiloult to do anything to help but wherever the opportunity offers, it should not be permitted to pass unregarded. " Domestic Arts Institutions.—The 'mere man, who probably knows nothing whatever about the subject, is apt to think that there is plenty of work for women, if they like to undist take it. For example,they may be able to write a very, good, or a very bad novel ; for either of these there is a demand. Then it seems to him that his women folk experience great difficulty in getting their dresses made, that when they are completed they do not fit care not what the owner wanted. He also regards with ra melancholy interest, the shirts and collars which return from the laundry, and wonder whether it is really necessary to tear them to pieces in order to make them clean. All this is mere speculation, which generally proves nothing but his ignorance but one is glad nevertheless tcewitness the growth Of such enter- prises as the Shoreditch Technical Institute, where there has bet n a two days' exhibition of work done by the girls in the day trade school. The school has been in existence for three years, and like many similar institutions in the provinces, it seems to be doing a valuable work in instructing girls in dressmaking told other arts. Another American Invasiona—London shoppers have now at their service a new West End Establishment, under American management. Everyone has heard of the mammoth " stores" of New York and Chicago, and Mr. Selfridge, who has been connected with one of the largest of these, has now opened a similar establish- ment in the metropolis. What effect this American invasion will have ori established houses, remains to he seen. 'the American plan is to interest arid entertain the potential customer without asking him or her to buy. and this is nothing new in London, as most of the large houses no longer harass their customers with the atten- tions of a too officious shopwalker. It is more than likely that British energy will hold its own, and at any rate the added stimulus of American competition in London shopland means the advantage of the public. Larger Waists —Leadiog dressmakers are allowing considesably mole latitude in the matter of waist measurements, and in the London show-rooms the stock waist now averages twenty-three or twenty-four inches. Nature never intended theie should be a fashion in waists,any more than she intended a fashion in noses. The flowing costumes of classic tin es did not require any manipillat ion of waist and hips, which the modern world of fashion has made a fine art. The waist in fact is the pivot around which all the main features of dress range themselves, and the happy medium in waist measurement is a desideratum in these days when it is the almost invariable rule for fashions to run to extremes. Curious Headgear.—The uninitiated, looking round at a fashionable afternoon assembly, could scarcely be blamed for coming to the conclusion that every woman present was we .ring some one else's hat. Nothing in the shape of a bandeau is permitted, and to be quite correct one needs to secure headgear seveial sizes too big in the crown, put it on, cram, it down—and dis- appear. The smartest women are showing no hair and very little face, and although one can scarcely admire the idea, it has at least proved to be a warm and cosy one during the arctic weather we have lately been called upon to plod through. - Matching Colours.—The tiresome rind wearisome task of matching colours is ours no longer; when a com- plete toilette is, required we find every detail has been carefully provided. Many large emporiums now devote a separate showrooni or window to one colour in divers shades, and from the lett and veil to the shoes and stockings, the scheme is complete. Of late we have been somewhat " patchy," permittine ourselves a different coloured coat or wrap to the gown and a decided contrast in hats, but one colour--in different tones of course—and with just a touch of black, may generally he relied upon to make the better effect. , An Excellent Exaruple.—lt has often been said, an,l with some cause,that we need a much more elastic dress allowance than that apportioned the Parisienne, and even then fail in the effect that should be ours. One reason for this state of affairs is, that we provide our- selves with too many coats sled skirts, and gowns. Where, for example the Parisienne is content with one costume, we desire three, the result being very indifferent garments, and no margin for details. The Parisienne possesses herself of one carefully thought- out suit or goaen, well cut and well made, then rings the changes by means of extra linings, vests, bleuses,shirts, neckwear, etc. The same with hats. Our sisters on the other side of the Channel secure at infinite pains very often. A shape that is really good and suits them.— With this they are content, ringing the changes by means of different trimmings.—Needless to say the effect is much to be preferred to that obtained by half- a-dozen chomp mid nasty specimen. .. A Revival.—Satin cord piping, a relic of mid- Victorian times, has appeared again. The mode is ,rt pleasing one and especially so when carried out in black upon pale-coloured toilet tea. A touch of black' is a matter of considerable importance this Spring, and few fashionable women are content to do without it. MADCs'E.</p>
        <p>A man called at an tindertakgr't shop in Lauenberg, Germany, and asked the proprietor to take back at half price a coffin he had ordered. He explained that be had intended to commit suicide, but had changed his mind. "All our knives and forks at home are stamped with names just as they are in the hotels" a little boy named Lesin boasted to his schoolfellows at Ragusa, in Dalmatia. His father has now been arrested cn a charge of stealing them.</p>
      </div>
