      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>A PRETTY DRESSING-GOWN.</p>
        <p>Every dainty woman loves a pretty becom- fry, dressing-gown, and considers her ward- robe incomplete without such a ;garment. Nor .need a dressing-gown be elaborate and eversive to be dainty and attractive, for simple clelaines, lawns, and muslins, plain or patterned, look just as pretty as and infinitely fresher than the most costly silks and laces. The very charming gown pictured in our sketch is of quite a simple and inexpensive type. The original was carried out in pale blue delaine of good winlity, which was guaranteed to wash excellently. A short bodioe of—the Empire type formed ,the upper</p>
        <p>part of the gown, the bodice beisg finished at the bottom by. a band of slotted- embroidery threaded with blue satin ribbon, which was tie•d into a bow in front, with long, hanging loops and ends. A small square yoke of lace insertion finished, the neck of the gown, and below it came a deep shaped collar of the material trimmed with .narrow lace and in- sertion. The sleeves were of the wide, hang- ing type, and were adorned with lace and in- sertion to . match the collar. The fulness cf the skirt was ,gailered on to fae lower edge of the yoke, failing thence in soft folds to the ground. This 'dressing - gown was cut very long all round, and slightly. trained at the back.</p>
        <p>. _,H,,,,,,„,,,,/,..,;',,'-',is,-,..,•-i,,,,,,,,,,,“.,,,t, .90-, , ~,,,,,E.s • ii rt 1. itt- i 1 I .'!•,,--- , ~-/-, ii If .....,:, (f. . 51, ~,• i • 1,1, ' :••,.• . , f.,...........1,.. . ir-7---- , ' 4; 1.. ' •-- il ,V;•"-,-,17-7.7",,, Z.' f ! ./1 it'•',Fl / II it'i I , ;IJ • ~ •- • !„, f •P. '''., V i!, jai f',/ ~ ////1 i . it," 11 . •1. ' . , 11 f , - !!: t• , ~ 1, • ' • . 1//11,1' sr II it I/ il„! ?..1 I , 111 I 1 / ) . ...,,.. _ lII' 'll'</p>
