      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WORD AS TO FROCKS.</head>
        <p>A WORD AS TO FROCKS.</p>
        <p>Black satin, skirts and long Directoire coats, with just a touch of colour somewhere, if it is. only in the embroidery, are likely to be greatly worn, Says the Queen. Barbaric Ornament lend themselves to black 'dresses, and -they are certainly the mode. Black low bodices, short-waisted, often have a Mut of brilliant green tulle across the bust and short 'sleeves ,to match. Iridescent colourings find a place in trimmings peacock-green, copper tones, dull red, and the hue /of the sapphire. We are looking kindly on the mediteval cut; with straight bands over the shoulders hack and front. Satin buttons' find a place on satin frocks. Black coats have waistcoats, snthll and short. Dull Silver buttons with huge cabochon stones have a good following. and pyramid-shaped buttons of +brilliants. Some of the Parisian houses are attempting • to bring us back to paniers. But we shall still, depend on it, preserve the straight up-and- down appearance for some time to come. The mediceval gown hanging in two pieces from the shoulder is the dominant notion of the 'hour; the front and back must be straight, the sides curved to shew the desired outline of bust and wai,;t. Long % and tiplit sleeves prevail; the opening of the sleeves is beneath, and successfully concealed. There is always a ruffle at the wrist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>USEFUL PINAFbRE.</head>
        <p>A USEFUL PINAFbRE.</p>
        <p>T.' ct u red in our illustration is a particu- larly neat and useful little pinafore, Just the thing for regular every-day wear for either schoolroom or nursery. This little pinafore is cut square at the neck, and finished with a plain stitched band of the material. On to the lower edge of this band, the fulness of. the</p>
        <p>pinafore is set in a wide box-pleat in the middle, and a succession of tucks on either side. These tucks are quite short, the re- leased fulness pouching a little at the waist. There are no sleeves, and the armholes are finished by .stitched bands which match the neck. A neat stitched band holds the pinafore in at the waist. THE SINS OF SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Lady Violet Greville Utters some home truths in an article, "Society and its Morals," in the March 'number of Cassell's Magazine. "Our present society," she says, "has only one desire in common, the desire td get on; only one love, the love of money; only one vice, selfishness. The. piquant, sparkling, agrees.ble, tolerant, vividly living society of the early nineteenth century, small in extent, sure of itself, possessed a cultiva- tion, an originality, a charm and an individu- ality which we have lost. The great feature of the age is indifferentism, which proclaims itself in a laxity of principle, oa disregard of duty, a desire for liberty verging on licence, and a lazy contempt for religion. Society is not immoral. It has not the strength or the ardour to be ; it is simply a shifting, strug.Qlina mass of unexcited. jaded. restless</p>
        <p>beings, voia or enaraorer arm aignity, naving flung away the attributes which once made it lordly and prosperous, grasping at shifty shadows—crazy, faddy, discordant, and en- tirely without power and prestige."</p>
        <p>‘4,!'"o &lt;`,,t, )74A 4 ''''f•r'•l • • k'•'\ ------- r. 5.411 L .• • . critl* —724 "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>MACARONI CHEESE A LA FIRENZE.—BoiI 8 quarter of a pound of macaroni in salted water until tender. Then thicken half a pint of milk with flour and a small piece of butter. Add two tablespoonfuls of cream, a little made mustard, pepper, salt, and cayenne, and work till smooth over the fire. Grate two ounces of Gruyere cheese and one ounce of Parmesan. Stir into the milk and boil for a few minutes. Then acid the macaroni, and boil up once .more. Have ready a buttered .fireproof dish, pour in the mixture, sprinkle with fine breaderumEs, lay small pieces of butter on the top, and brown in a quick oven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>IRISH DEMONSTRATION.</head>
        <p>IRISH DEMONSTRATION.</p>
        <p>THE DEMAND FOR HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>" Freedom for our Fatherland " was the cause, blazoned • forth in letters of green, which locarlrish- men were asked to 'support by" coming in their thousands " to a' demonstration held in the Royal Theatre of .Varieties on Sunday afternoon; under the auspices of the Warrington branch of the United Irish League. The building was crowded with an audience which lacked nothing in . enthusiasm and the national colour 'ivas freely displayed. Two repre- sentatives of the Irish Parliamentary Party • had promised to attend but the meeting had to be satisfied with one, as Mr. J. P. Boland M.P., the party Whip, was taking the place of Mr. John Redmond, M.P., who ilalisposed, at Manchester.. Mr. Richard Hazleton, P. for North Galway, was present and gave an eloquent and stirring address. Dr. Liston presided and amongst others present on-the platform were ; Airs. Liston. Mi*s Roddy, Mr. R. HaZleton, Coimcillor Dicketh4on, Professor Ainsley (Widnes), Mr. and Mrs. Byrne (Earlestown),. Messrs. P. Conway (President of tip branch), Felix Maginn; Charles Roddy, Kelly, Peter Magill'', Cosgrove (Earlestown), P. Quinn, (Widnes), J. Elwell, jun., W. Egan; W. Rogerson, James Fay, AV. Kilduff, T. Farrington. (secretary), E. Handley, P. and G. Kenny, and A. NI Crowe.</p>
        <p>Before the speech-making began, Miss Eastens sang "Come back to Erin'" and her sweet rendering of the familiar Melody was well received.</p>
        <p>The CHAIEMAN declared that the principal object of the League was to help to bring to an end the quarrel which for so many years had gone on between Great Britain and Ireland. That quarrel which had had such disastrous consequences, was not the fault of the Irish people. "The fault at the present time " said Dr. Liston, emphatically "lies with the Conservative party who in this matter persistently pursue a policy ungenerous and unjust. The fear that if we had self- government our people would seek to separate them- selves from the Enipire is entirely erroneous and under any circumstances they could ne-N'er be so foolish and so blind to their own interests, as to cut themselves off from the many privileges, of being citizens of the great British Empire. I believe that once themajority are convinced /hat we do not want separation, they will willingly grant us a measure of self-government similar to what the colonies enjoy, and so establish between the two peoples a firm and lasting friendship." (Loud applause). . Mr. FELD( MA G,INN moved: "That we, the Irish- men of Warrington, desire to express our unqualified confidence in the Irish Parliamentary party and in the lead4rship of Mr. John Redmond and we offer them our sincere gratitude for their work during the last session on behalf of Catholic education which has resulted in the Act establishing a National University in Ireland." He Ti aintained ti at the Irish Parliamentary party were discharging their duty fearlessly, loyally and, on the whole, with signal success. They were the emissaries of conciliation as . between the democracies of Great Britain and Ireland. "It is quite true " said Mr. Magian "that we have not leached the golden dawn when we shall see our Parliament established On College Green—(cheers)--but, short of that great triumph, the Irish Parliamentary party with your assist- ance have been instrumental in procuring many great reforms which have gone far to advance the people on the road of self-government. . . . I know Irishmen who are cowardly and craven enough to he ashamed of the persistent obstructive tactics of the Parliamentary Party in the House of Commons. I should like to call attention to the fact that within recent years the strategy which has been-brought to a point of perfection by the Irish party in times gone by hus now become one of the leading and perhaps most important methods by which the Labour party and other parties in rho House of Commons are enf,rcing their claims. We Irishmen have, out of our poverty and lack of oppoftunity, provided the other parties a model which they are glad to follow now—indeed I claim that the Irish Parliamentary party has conferred a boon upon the British democracy,as well as upon us." (Applause.) Mr. C. Roupv, who seconded, waxed so enthusiastic that in a moment of forgetfulness he addresse i the Chaiiman as " Mr. Speaker," evidently imagining that he was fighting Ireland's battles on the floor of .our local House of Commons. He c,ntended it was . essential that the Irish language. should be made a , compulsory subject for matriculation at the National University for all the students taught in Irelaud,and'at his suggestion an addition was made to the resolution embody inn that view. "We claim national self-govern- ment raid Mr. Roddy" as a right. For us the Act of Union has no binding moral or legal force. We regard it as our fathers regarded it before us—as a great criminal act of usurpation, carried by violence and fraud, and no lapse of time, no mitigation of its details, can ever make it, binding on our honour and conscience." Mr. Roddy, claimed that by the decline of population Ireland had lostln capital £800,000,000. "That would buy a Dreadnought!" he exclaimed amid laughter and applause. hr. Roddy contended that not a single Bill had been brought into the House of Commons for the betterment of the workingclasses in England during the past twenty-five years. without receiving the whole- hearted support of the Irish party. (Applause). The resolution w as carried unanimously. Mr. R. HAZLETON, M.P., a young mAn with a sonorous voiee and an impressive style of oratory, said it was very necessary at the present juncture in politics for the Irish potty to know they had behind them the sup- port and sympathy of the Irish people, for without the united support of their people throughout the world the Dish party in Parliament would be a broken reed of little or no use whatever. Mr. Hazleton vigorously refuted the criticisms levelled at the party in cohnec- tion with the disposal of the Irish vote at Glasgow and he chntended that Catholic education could better he maintained by relying upon the eighty Irish votes in Parliament than upon men like the Duke of Norfolk. The Irish party had never for a moment abandoned the position they long since -took up that. they should be ahsoltitely free and indepenant of all parties in the United Kingdom. That independence was absolutely necessary. The Irish party had always been the friends \of the workingmen of Great Britain and had looked after their interests before there was a Labour party. They had no quarrel with the workingmen of England and Scotland who, if they could only get at the truth, would he unanimously on their side. Their quarrel was with the Government and with those who misrepresented the position and deceived the people of these 'countries as to what was the true demand of Ireland. The Irish members had been sent not to interfere or obstruct legislation affecting the people of this country,, but to win Home Rule for Ireland, and they would keep that object in view until it had been achieved. Their people asked for Home Rule as a right and as the only means of solving the evils, dangers and difficulties that surrounded Ireland at the present time. Taken by any test the Union had been an absolute failure. "The history of Ireland since the Act of Union " declared Mr. Hazle- ton " has t eon one long drawn-out trogedy—desnair, resentment, and hatred ; revolt, insurrection and, defeat; decay, famine, poverty, misery and depopulation ; mis- trust, unrest; discontent and insecurity ; coeroi, n, repression' over-taxation, extravagance and inefficiency —those have been the resnlts of the union between Great Britain and Ireland." Whilst .England s;nce the Union had been making a financial profit out of Ireland, this year, thanks to old-age pensions, that profit bad been atisolutely wiped out, and unless there was fresh taxation England would in future he running Ireland at a loss. " All we ask " concluded Mr. Hazleton "is to be allowed to work out our own destiny, to have charge and control of our own internal affairs. England has had the management df them for a century and has proved absolutely unable to manage them either to her own•satisfaction or to ours. We say that the only solu- tion and the one you must in the end accept, is to give to the people of Ireland the management of their own affairs, for which they have so long asked, and which when conceded will be not only to the advantage of Ireland„ but to the advantage of England." (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. COSGROVE, of Earlestown, Moved a .res'olution thanking Mr. Hazleton and pledging the meeting to continue unabated its demand for self-governme t for Ireland. He said that if ever the Irish party adopted an attitude that placed the question of Home Rule in a subordinate position to any other question, the Home Rule movement would collapse not only in this Icountry, but perhaps in Ireland as well. • Mr. BYRNE (Earlestown) seconded and the vote was heartily agreed to. Mr. HAZLETON, in responding, moved a similar com- pliment to the Chairman. Mr. A. M. CuoAYE, who seconded, laid stress Ou the fact that the Irish section ,of the community in War- rington was an important factor, consisting as it did of over 1,000 voters, and it would very probably be the balancing power in the next election. This vote was also agreed to and Dr. LISTON'S reply . concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>MR. A. J. KING, M.P.</head>
        <p>MR. A. J. KING, M.P.</p>
        <p>SPEECH ON ARMAMENTS AND</p>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM,</p>
        <p>Mr. Alfred J. King, M.P. for the Knutsford division, delivered an interesting address on current politics it the annual meeting of the Executive Council of the Liberal Association' held in the Manchester Reform Club on Saturday.- He said the Liberal party aas still healthy, in good heart and confident. For the next two or three weeks the interest of Parliament would be centred in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>BUDGET.</head>
        <p>THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>He was in no way alarmed at what was going to happen. The finances of the country were in the hands of Mr. Lloyd-George, in whom he had complete confidence, and whom at this particular moment was entitled to the sympathy of all right-thinking people. It was especially hard on a man on whoni so much responsi- bility rested that he should be subjected to slanderous attacks like thaf which was. exposed in the law courts the other day. (Cheers). Mr. Lloyd-George had shown in his whole career that be had sympathy with tt.e poor and needy'. One might be sure, therefore, that new revenue would be provided in such a Way as not to increase th6burdens of the poor ; and his conduct as President of the Board of Trade also gave assurance' that he would not do anything to cripple the industries of the nation. riming to the expenditure side of the nation's accounts, Mr. King spoke of the .cost of old- age pensions as quite justifiable. What he did not like was the ever-increasing</p>
        <p>• EXPENDITURE ON ARMAMENTS. To him the naval estimates, uhich only ;came into his hands on Friday night, were a source of grave anxiety. It was surprising in the fourth year of a Liberal Govern- ment to find them making propositions for an.expendi- ture on the navy greater than this country had seen before. At the same time, he does not think we ought to blame the Government too much. The thing to be blamed most was the public opinion which demanded this expenditure: " War, in my view," Mr. King went on," is a thing which ought to be relegated to past history altogether. It is unworthy of any nation that claims to be civilized. I therefore don't like these preparations for war—for preparations they really are. "People say these great armaments are a guarantee of peace, and they therefor r press them forward on every possible occasion. • I do not believe it. I regret that this great increase in our expenditure on a arr.hips should be proposed at the present time. •Again, people say a great navy is an insurance for our trade and com- merce. I have always understood that if yon reduced sour risk you reduced your premium. The very opposite seems to be the effect in this case. We have been reducing the risk of war for the last five years. We have an entente cordials with France, an.understanding with Russia, no cause of quarrel with Germany, and an alliance with Japan. Why then do we go on raising our expenditure on armaments—an expenditure which, to my mind, seems to have no end? This game of heggar-rny-neighbour, , can only end in national bank- ruptcy, Or the burden in time will become sc great that people will say, Better have war and get the thing over.' That is not a very cheerful outlook.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>INTERNATIONAL ANTI-WAR LEAG CE.</head>
        <p>INTERNATIONAL ANTI-WAR LEAG CE.</p>
        <p>"We hear of a confederacy in certain political quarters. I sometimes think there is a bigger'con- federacy. a greater conspiracy, at work—a confederacy composed of people who have money to lend, people who are interested in arrnament factories, people who are interested in gold mines, or who make money out of contracts for the supply of Stores and war materials. I should like to see a national, or an international league to fight these forces—Uß anti-war league.— (Cheers.) To die this is a painful subject. I cannot see bow Liberals in Opposition can oppose a large expenditure by a Tory Government if they support the present proposals."—(Hear. hear.) Mr. King next referred to the efforts of Tariff "Reformers" to spread their doctrines. Recently he had seen some TARIFF " REFORM " LEAFLETS, which had been distributed in his constituency. They were remarkable for lack of truth and tor half truths. For example, in one leaflet it was stated that Mr. J. Havelock Wilson, M.P., had said "there was little in the Merchant Shipping Act for which seamen need he thankful." H could not think such a thing was true, so he asked Mr. Wilson, and, as he expected, Mr. Wilson gave a complete denial to it. On the other hand Mr. Wilson declared that the Merchant, Shipping Act'passed by the Liberal Government in 1900 had completely revolutionised the position of the seafaring people of this country. (Cheers.) This - showed how mis- leading, how false, some of the leaflet statements were. (Cheers.) '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LATE SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. H. P. Greg) and Mr. King spoke highly Of the zealous work of Mr. Joseph Smith (who has just retired from the position of secretary to the Association) in the cause of Liberalism in the division for the past 14 years. As a token of the Council's appreciation of Mr. Smith's work there has been a sub- scription, and Mrs. King presented Mr. Smith with a cheque for £B3 4s. 6d., which will be made into a round sum of £lOO.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith, in acknowledging the gift, thanked the Association warmly for its kindness and confidence, and said he was glad to think he would remain a member of the Executive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Lord Stanley of Alderley was, with cheers, r.-elected President of the Association. Mr. A. L. Goodson was appointed chfrirman of the Executive, in succession to Mr. Greg. It was Mr. Goodson, Mr. Greg added, who taught their late member, Mr. Alan de Tattoo Egerton. how to fall by wresting from him the representation of Knutsford in the Cheshire County Council. Mr. Greg was appointed honorary treasurer in succession to Mr. Surtee3 Irwin. Mr. R. Holland Owen and Air. J. B. Thistlethwaite was re-elected honorary secretaries and Mr. J. Arnold Thompson was again eleeted auditor. Messrs. Greg, Goodson, Owen, and the Secretary were elected as delegates to the Cheshire Liberal Federal Council, which will in future work with the Laticashire and Cheshire Liberal Federation. Mr. Goodsion, Mr. Owen, and the Secretary were chosen to represent the Association on the General Committee of the National Liberal Federation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHEAPER ELECTRIC LIGHT.</head>
        <p>CHEAPER ELECTRIC LIGHT.</p>
        <p>Nine months ago in "The Daily Mail" attention was drawn to the revolutien in the cost of electric lighting then in progress as the result of the introduc- tion of the metallic filament lamp. In this lamps thread of one of the rare, metals—usually tantalum or tungsten—is -utilised instead of the carbon ulament, because it has been found that these metallic filaments give out a f: r greater light with a much reduced con- sumption of electricity. To-day we are able to print some actual figures of the saving effected by the change. For the darkest quarter of the yesr, that ending in December, by the use of tungsten lamps, with a trans- 'former. the bill has been reduced from 70 to 73 per cent. In other words, the same amount of light can now be obtained while the conditions are favourable for one-third the former cost, and even where the con- ditions are unfavourable, and where carbon lamps of low candle-power were previously in service, the saying should still be over 50 per cent. Such an advance in efficiency will interest .-eimry householder, for if electric light were known to be cheaper than the old-fashioned illuminants it would be almost universally preferred. To get the full saving out of the new lamp a low pressure or "voltage of electricity has hitherto had to be employed, and has only been found possible to lower the pressure without troublesome mechanism where the current is "alternating." The 'metallic lamps, however, are being rapidly improved, and only last week a new 16- candle-power pattern, which can be used on high pressure circuits without the intervention of a trans- former, was introduced. But as a very large number of electric lighting companies and municipalities through- out this country already supply "alternating " and not continuous current there is a great field for economy, and when the puhlic understands the possibilities of the new lamps their general introduction may be anticipated. It can be said with confidence that on "alternating " circuits electric light with the new lamps is now as cheap as gas, and on "continuous " circuits the same saving will shortly become possible, while electricity has further advantages- on the score of convenience and benefit to health, as with it there is no pollution of the atmosphere. The electric light companies, however, have not hitherto made the most of their opportunity. They have shokvn alarm rather than satisfaction at the introduction of the new lamps, apparently because they expect an immediate reduction in the quantity of current con- sumed and a loss of profit. But in the near future the demand for cheap electric light must grow enormously. and hundreds of houses which now burn gas or oil will change to the new lamp, which has achieved for elec. tricitv what the mantle accomplished for gas.—" Daily mail."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>,100SCarefil 'YTufied Sc Repaired</p>
