      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>OUR FOOTBALL PHOTO PRIZE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>OUR FOOTBALL PHOTO PRIZE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>[Tonge]</p>
        <p>[Photo.] A SECTION OF THE CROWD AT THE WARRINGTON BRADFORD MATCH The original of the portrait marked with a X will receive the "Examiner prize of 7s. 6d. on calling at the " Examiner " Office, Bewsey-street, before Saturday, March 27th." Last week's winner was John Valentine Wicherley, 20, Eustace-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Pad gate, 11h March, 1909.</p>
        <p>to maintain their reputation. But we lost; and the disappointment was 'atmost inexpressible in ordinary polite Knglish. Some hard things were said of those closely associated with the management and the ears of one player, at least, must have burned for an uncom- fortably lone time after' the event. Remembering another occasion, when through lack of judgment at the critical moment Warrington -lost the Cup, vengeance was vowed..vmd the poor Committee were the innocents to be duly presented for slaughter. Again the annual meeting is near at hand and if we could not beat Leeds we can surely wipe out the Committee. What sport for the Gods ! Now, Sir, it is ever so easy to be wise after the event, as we know, but to admit that if sp-and-so had been played instead of the one selected things might have been different may be gracious but it doetmot satisfy. Suppose that'another playei had taken the position and that he had failed.to fill it adequately—what then? The Cornmitttee would, doubtless, have been blamed just the same. The fact is that no matter whom We Com- mittee had selected to play, if he had proved a failure, they would have been blamed for not playing someone else. It is of no use crying over spilt milk, but it is of some importance to provide for emergencies, and to endeavour to prevent accidents in the future, and it is to that end that I wish to make two suggestions which, if carried out, will have a tendency to remedy the defect. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Football Clubs to-day, so far as their management is concerned, nd whether we like it or not, are very similar to commercial companies and must be con- ducted on strictly business lines. Nothing has to be left to chance. The "one man" ide-t is rapidly giving Way to departmental management, and individualism to combination. My ~first suggestion. then is—more first- class players. Recent events have proved to us that they are available and that their acquisition has been the means of making the treasurer happy, to say nothing of the secretary's sleep. ' My second suggestion is of a more delicate nature but .I hope I shall not be misunderstood. No one, for a single moment, doubts the whole-heartedness of the officials and Committee. The Hon. Treasurer and Hon. Secretary have not only been praised by the memhers of the Club but have been congratulated time and again by visitors on the efficient way ih witich they have dis- charged their onerous duties. All concerned have had a most. trying time during the season that is quickly drawing to a close. They have done their best, with the material at hand, and he would boa cleverbptimist who would flatter himself that he could have come out of the ordeal with flying colours. But it does seem to me, Sir, that the Committee have not been sufficiently strict in their methods of management. Probibly, they have been handicapped in not having a strong reserve of players, but the fact remains that the players appear to have had more latitude than was good either for theirselves orthe Club. It may be that the abolition of the working clause has added to the Committee's difficulties. But, because the team has not come up so our expectations it is unfair—and suicidal—to talk of ousting them as a body. What guarantee have we that the second six will be better than the first? If they have, for obvious rerefons, been weak, why not strengthen them ? There is a very large membership and it should not he impossible to select, say another four members to add .to their number. The Committee should be electcd not so much because they have been old players but more especially on account of their executive ability. My second suggestion then is that Rule 8 be amended and that the Committee should be increased front six to ten. Big Committees are not always advisable, but I am of opinion that, at the present time at any rate, new blood would be beneficial and would help to make the body politic strong and healthy, and would satisfy the members of the Club. I hope that rny letter may be read as it is written, frankly, and those who have adopted an unhappy, knack of reading between the lines for motives iyill for once curb their appetite and look upon it as an effusion of an old member anxious for the Club's welfare.—Yours truly, EDMUND OSBORNE.</p>
