      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Surprise at Runcorn Home Team Lose by a Goal-kick.</head>
        <p>A Surprise at Runcorn Home Team Lose by a Goal-kick.</p>
        <p>Between two and three thousand spectators witnessed the meeting of these teams at Runcorn in the second round for the Cup, there being a fair sprinkling of Hull . supporters present. Both sides were w(ell represented. Major commenced for Hull, and after it short attack on the Runcorn line the home forwards brought relief vith dribbling. The ball was sent to Watton who dallied too long, finally losing possession. E. Rogers wordy gathered, and opened the score with it fine try after five minutes' play. The same player failed at goal. Anothgr brilliant run by E. Rogers looked ominous for Runcorn, but Flanagan relieved with a long kick, which was followed by the home 'forwards taking the ball over the Hull line for .Padbury to score, thus bringing the teams equal, SA the latter player was unable to add a goal. Resuming, E. Rogers had a shot at goal without success, Padbury likewise failing with a similar kick. Jolley and Butterworth put Runcorn on the attack, but Wallace Anderson, and Cottrell soon. had Runcorn in diffictilties, and on three occasions Flanagan, Houghton, and Padbury respectively just kept the visitors out. From a scrummage the ball was kicked across to E. Rogers, who gave Hull the lead again by cleverly running over at the corner, only to fail with the gonl-kick. Half-time :—Hull 2 tries (6 points), Runcorn 1 try (3 points). kfter the interval a fine round of passing by Jolley, Watton, Butterworth, Moran and Hughes almost ended in a score. From a scrummage Jolley passed out to Wotton, who scrambled over, only to be ordered back, to the great disnppointment of the home supporters. A dribble by the,Hull forwards was just stopped in time by Kennedy. Wallace. however, got possession from the pack, and wormed his way through the Runcorn backs in a marvellous fashion to score a fine try, which he improved without difficulty. Matters now looked rosy for the Yorkshiremen, but on resumption a pack was formed near the Hull line, where Jolley secured and scored, Padbury failing to keep augment. Following this the game was keenly contested. A round of passing in which Jolley, Watton, Moran and Flanagan took part, saw the latter cross at the cOrner, Jolley however, being unable to add a goal. The closipg stages of the game were in Hull's favour, and although each side strove hard, there was no further score. Result 1 goal and 3 tries (11 points), Runcorn 3 tries (9 points).</p>
        <p>ORDINARY MATCHES. *Hnll\Kingston FL 4 4 20 York 3 2 12 *Warrington</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ENJOY A "FRIENDLY."</p>
        <p>Having no interest •in the Cup struggle and being ' without a League fixture Warrington arranged a " friendly " with Bradford Northern as visitors on Saturday. Fine weather prevailed, but the match did not arou.e much interest and only about 159 spectators assembled. Warrington were at full strength, while the visitors also brought a fair side. 'reams : Warrington—Tilley, back; Landor, Dickinson, Taylor, Treharne, three-quarter backs; Brooks and Jenkins, half backs ; Dowell, Neill, Thomas, Shugar-t, Belton, and Boardman, forwards. Bradfotd Northern—Ward, back ; Jones Griffin, ' Cluderay, Luke three-quarter backs ; Roberts and Gow land, half backs; Mann, Mitchell, Kitchen, Wallard, Haley, and Starncliffe, forwards Referee, Mr. E. H. Smirk, Wigan. Warrington were early to the fore with a strong attack, and after Jones had beaten off one invasion, Brooks broke through and passed to Taylor, who com- pleted the try. Another brilliant display by the home hacks led up to Treharne scoring on tbe right. Thomas failing at goal in both instances. Bradford made spasmodic bursts, and Mann was always to the fore in leading his forwards. There was little finish, and Tilley beating them off, Brooks again got well away, and though challenged by Ward succeeded in passirg to Thonnts, who crossed to also place a goal. Again War— rington attacked, and the issue of another hot raid tA as a try to Taylor, Thomas again goaling, Warrington leading at half-time by sixteen points to nil: Turning round, Warrington continued to play a strong game, and though Wird and Cludeny exhibited a good defence, O'Neill managed to break through, closely followed by Thomas. Bradford rallied well, and from a passing bout Jones exhibited a fine torn of speed and scored. at the' corner. Afterwards Warrington had matters as they pleased, and Brooks and Landor scored, Warrington winning by 3 goals, 8 tries (30 points), Bradford Northern 1 try (3 points). NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. *Batley 5 8 34 Rochdale H 2 0 4 St. Helens 5 10 40 *Aberdare 3 4 18 Bramley v. Dewsbury postponee.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS INDULGE IN HIGH SCORING. This League match was played at Merthyr, berore moderate attendance. Pope scored, Barton failing to convert. Manchester from a pm scored. Taylor (Aberdare) scored a try. Bowen from a race down added, Rees converting. Flanaghan scored a try. Bowen (Aberdare; scored, Rees converting. Brien scored a try. Prescott scored, Baton rnajorisinc,,. Flanaghan, Kelly add J. Greenwood added tries. The two latter converted. Trenwith and Manchester added tries, Lea converting the last. Result :—St. Helens 5 goals 10 tries (40 points), Aberdare 3 goals 4 tries (18 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>NOTES BY " WRIST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " WRIST."</p>
        <p>Warrington seems to be in the backwaters of foot- ball at the present time. There has not been a decent match or an item of interest since the fatal cup-tie on February 27th. This, in itself, still provides plenty of food for discussion, and there are shoals of wild rumours about the ultimate fate of the - Committee when a certain meeting comes round. The 'members had better put cold water bandages round their heads in order to avoid doing something rash. It wquld be the 'height of folly, in my opinion, to go in for a new Committee en bloc. The officials, and several members of the Committee, have gained knowledge and experience of an invaluable character, and which new men would entirely lack. Ido not say that there could not be changes in the personnel of the Committee with advantage, hut on the whole I think the wisest plan is that suggested by the writer of the letter below which favours the introduction of new blood by the enlargement of the Committee from 6to 10. There is certainly too much work for a small Committee nowla- clays, and four new men.. of the right type would strengthen the Club management and promote greater efficiency.</p>
        <p>Leeds did not enjoy their triumph over Warrington for long. The second round engagement on the Hunslet ground proved too much for. them, as everybody orpected. Luck did not favour them to the extent it did at Wilderspool. So far the most noticeable feature of the Cup Competition has . been the luck of Oldham--a fortunate side for some seasons past. In the first round they scraped home hy a single point. Last Saturday they were mor dly beaten at Swinton, but managed to equalise in the last few minutes and won the replay with a certain amount of ewe on their own pet pitch at WatersheddingA.- Now they are drawn at home again for Round 3, and unless, things go very seriously wrong they will qualify for the semi-final by beating thair second opponents from Hull The draw for the Third Round is as follows :</p>
        <p>Salford V. Halifax. Wakefield Trinity v. Hunslet. Huddersfield v,Wigan.</p>
        <p>Oldham v. Hull.</p>
        <p>My favourites for both Cup and League are Wigan, and they possess an excellent chance of carrying off the double event. They have never yet got their names inscribed on the Cim, hut the only serious obstacle now is at Huddersfield. On neutral ground the clever Wiganers would secnre the vote agairrt any club, and seeing the way in which they cut no Hunslet at Park. side on Wednesday evening they should prove fully equal to the demands made upon them at Fartoun.</p>
        <p>T don't often venture to prophecy, but I bave a vision th,t, the sami-finals will be contested by Wigan, Oldham, Halifax and Wakefield Trinity.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men had some good practice last Saturday at the expense of Bradford. who sent a moderate side to take part in a "friendly." There was some fine straight running by the home backs and a winning score of 30 points to 3. One of the best tries of the day was got by O'Neill, who ran from half way and when challenged on the line gave his tackler a real old-fashioned " hand-off " which placed him on his back 'and left the scorer free.</p>
        <p>Bradford .Were only a shadow of the team which used to Rive Warrington an occasional hiding at Wilder- spool. The chief attraction on their side was one Gowland, a half hack from Canada. His hair hung nearly to his shoulders, and the generally effeminate appearance of the man caused the crowd much merri- ment.. He was at once honoured with several nick- names, such as " the poet," " Hall Caine," and the like. I don't think Bradford's latest capture is likely to anse a run on the Rockies for football talent, though it is a curious fact that the" grizzly" made the open- ing for the only try which Bradford got.</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington resume League football,and if they have not got soft with idleness they should capture a rouple of points at Barrow. It is to be noted that the Committee have not yet forgiven Fish for the Karno crime, and Bradshaw gets the left wing position as the result of a dashing display at PernberCon.Team. : Tilley, full back; Bradshaw, Dickenson, Taylor and Tr,harne, three-quarters; Brooks and Jenkins, half-hacks ; O'Niell, Thomas, Dowell, Shugars, Belton and Board- man, fo,mards.</p>
        <p>. The League table up to date is as follows For Agst Per P. W. L. D. G. T. P 4 G. T. P. ceotage Wigan 27 25 2 0 101 147 6.1331 33 .161 92•46; Oldham 26 22 4 0 66 93 411 27 26 132 8461' Halifax ...26 21 .4 1 63 95 41122 31 137 8269 Batley .95 18 6 2 40 62 266 28 Si 149 7340 Salford 27 19 8 0 63 73 345 35 51 223 7020 , Merthyr Tl 7 11 5 1 28 41 179.34 25 143 6764 H'dd'rsfi'ld2B 17 .8 3 55 85 365 45 45 228 6607 Wak'frld T 25 16 S 1 57 80 354 35 43 199 66.00 Hunslet ...25.15 9 1 40 61 263 33 45 201 6200. Broughton 26 15 10 1 57 63 303 44 59 265 5961 Runcorn ...25 14 10 1 30 60 240 33 32 162 5800 Hull 98 15 12 1 61 87 383 48 56 294 5525 Ebbw Vale 23 12 10 1 25 61 235 41 55 259 5434 Warring'n 25 13 11 1. 51 59 279 33 50 215 54.00 Leeds 95 13 13 0 44 72 304 51 57 273 5000 Dewsbury..24 11 12 1 49 62 284 39 53 237 47'91 Unil K.R.23 10 12 1 57 63 .297 47 68 298. 4565 Keighley...24 10 13 1 48 59 273 40 61 263 4315 Leigh 93 10 13 0 31 43 188 44 55 253 43'47 St. Helens 25 913 3 38 -63 265 54 70 318 4200 York 94 914 1 49 62 281 53,-80 345 39'58 Bradford... 26 10 16 0 47 .66 292 56 74 -.334 3846 Swinton -.28 10 17 1 37 52 230 50 82 346 3750 Mid-Rh'da 17 5 11. 1 , 17 24 106 SO 44 192 3235 RocluialeH2s 716 2 22 40 164 49 .65 293 3200 Barrow TreherbertlB 413 1 15 17 81 22 55 212 .2560 Widnes ...24 417 3 20 32 135 39 61 261 2291 oßarry l7 314 0 11 18 76 52 95 389 1764 Bramley _22 220 0 29 28 14266 121 495 .909 , Aberdare 17 116 0 22 30 134 59 96 406 s'BB</p>
        <p>The Lancashire pombination results up-to-date are as follow : P. ,W. L. D. Pts. Warrington A 9l .. 16 ... 1 ... 4 .., 36 Wigan A Oldham A 2l ... 12 ... 5 ... 4 ... 28 Pemberton Leigh Shamrochs , 2O ... 9 .. 6 ... 6 ... 24 Inca Wigan Highfleld Runcorn A . , 22 ... 8 ... 10 "... 4 .„. 20 St. Helens A Broughton Rangers 21 ... 7 ... 12 ... 2 ... 16 Widnes 'A 22 ... 6 —l2 ... 4 ... 16 Salford A Rochdale Hornets A 21 ... 6 —l4 ... 1 ... 13</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" require another point to be absolutely sure of the championship, and with Ince as visitors to-day they should obtain acouplepretty easily. The selected team is Mclntyre, full back; Hockenhull, Cartwright, Jordan and Landor, three-quarters; Lloyd and Daintith, half backs; Harmer, Bradshaw, Harrison, Cheaters, P. Boardman and Hewitt, forwards. ,</p>
        <p>THE CUP-TIE DEFEAT.—ANOTHER LETTER. Dear " Ovalist,"—Perhaps I may be permitted to say a few words with reference to the letters lately appear- ing in the" Examiner "concerning Warrington's cup- tie defeat. What criticism '1 have to offer is in no carping spirit but as a member anxiow for the welfare of the club and desirous of furthering its interests. Of course, we were all sure of winning. Under ordinary conditions, not even the most enthusiastic follower of the Leeds Club would fancy his men leaving the Wilderspool ground victorious. We were a much improved team whilst our opponents had failed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATICN.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATICN.</p>
        <p>WARRINGToN " PEMBERTON..</p>
        <p>Warrington took a powerful side for this important return match at Pemberton on Saturday. The visitors early demonstrated their superiority, and following on some good work by the forwards the backs handlel smartly and Bradshaw early-raced over for a brilliant try. Mclntyre failed at the place-kick. The locals attacked, but found the defence of Lees, Mclntyre and Jordan very sound, and a renewal of the attack by the " Wirepullere " culminatel in Bradshaw scoring his ! second try in fine 111cIntyre improving, --Ralphs placed a goal by Pemberton, but Hookenhall placed the-visitors further ahead when he raced over, for Cartwright to improve. In the second half Pemberton were often dangerous and Warrington were handicapped by serious injuries to Bradshaw and Lees, who took no farther part in the game. The home forwards ruled matters and tries were scored by Latimer and Holding, one of which was converted by Ralphs. Result : Warrington "A," two goals three tries (13 points) ; Pemberton, two goals, two tries (10 points). • G. T. P. G. T. P. Warrington 2 3 13 Pemberton Oldham 5 9 37 Wigan Highfield 1 1 5 St. Helens</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ASSOCIATION GAME.</head>
        <p>THE ASSOCIATION GAME.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " OBSERVER, " CUP. SEM I- PISA L.-REPLAYED TIE. #</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ALBION V. SCTTO'N COMMERCIAL.— There was a large crowd to witness this encounter on Saturday, on the gronnd of the Walker's Brewei:%.• Club at Padgite•lane. Williams started for Sutton and after a few exchanges the Albions attacked, Gitlihrand raced away and Sutton had a spell of defending, Greg- son eventually shooting over. Play was being evenly fought out until Sutton again attacked, without result. Sutton kept up the pressure, but the AlbionV. defence was sound. Warrington had another turn, Mather shooting wide.. The game was keenly fought in the second half, and Sutton scoring twice against the Albions once qualified to meet Clockface in the final struggle for the trophy.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE: FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>For ag'st</p>
