      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Concert at Latchford</head>
        <p>Concert at Latchford</p>
        <p>A successful evening concert was held in Wash- lane Schoolroom, Latchford, on Monday evening in aid- of the Golden City Bazaar. There was a good attendance and a capital programme was submitted, each of the artistes having a fine reception. Miss Carrie Hastink was in good voice and sang" Bid Me discourse" -(Bishop) and " 0 haste ye birds (Juinb,ert) with taste and expression. Miss Emily, Taylor was heard to great advantage in " Waltz Song" (E. . German), and was warmly applauded. Mr. J. W. Willett sang very.creditably " The Sailor's grave " 4Sulliran) and" An evening song" (Blumenthal). IN4. W. Hughes also sang well. Miss Marion Beeley proved herself to be a true artiste and gave charming interpretations of" Flower song " (Gounod) " Rose in , the bud " (Foster) and "Open thy blue eyes" (Mannet).' Her phrasing was excellent and the purity and flexibility of her rich voice greatly impressed her hearers. The feature of the eveniUg was the delight- ful violin playing of Miss Annie Kirkham whose solos were " Fantasia Caprice" (rieuxtemps) " Serenade " (Pierne) and " Cyardas " (liztbey), This artiste has a perfect -command of her instrument, her staccato passages • being most praiseworthy. Mr. Jan Hurst was the pianist and accompanist. He gave brilliant performances of k* Scherzo in B flat minor" (Chopin) and fantasia "Mid-summer night's dream"(Meiidelssohn- Liszt) and his sympathetic playing of the accompani- ments drew from the artistes much eulogistic com- ment. Other pleasing items were the duets " When the ;wind blows in from the sea" by Miss Emily , Taylor and Miss Marion Beeley and ." Maying " by Miss Carrie Hastings and e.Nlr, W. Hughes. The con, cert was arranged by Mrs. E. Lloyd-Jones,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ABREAST WITH MODERN SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>ABREAST WITH MODERN SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>GREAT REMEDY FOR OVER-FATNESS.</p>
        <p>M ,/ • cdical science which has made such extraordinary progress within the last two decades, is further. enriched by a remarkable remedy for obesity. The prescription is now given to the world. and one can make it up without trouble after procuring the following harmless ingredients at any chemist's : —One . half-ounce of Marmola, one ounce of fluid extract of Glycyrrhiza 8.P., one ounce of pure Glycerine B.P. and Peppermint Water to make up six ounces in all Now take two teespoonfuls after each meal and at bedtime, at the same time enjoying the most wholesome food you itincy. This simple, scientific and entirely harmless home treatment will make you lose pounds of unneCessry fat-degenerated flesh in a week, and in. a very short while you will have felt the last of the discomforts of corpulency with its tram of inevitable ills. You will find your diges- tive powers much improved, and blood and skin will be purified. Both physically and . mentally you Will feel. much younger, and the wonderful decrease in bulk will leave you brighter and more energetic, as well as symmetrical in figure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Handsome</p>
        <p>Contributions.</p>
        <p>By the kind permission of the Mayor, the Working- men's Committee of the Infirmary held their annual meeting in the Council Chamber of the Town Hail, on Saturday last. There were a good number of delegates present, and before the meeting was brought to a close each man had decided to pot forth fresh energy, tact and zeal into the work of maintaining the Infirmary which is such a blessing to the town and neighbourhood. His Worship the MAYOR (Alderman W. J. Forshaw) presided, and amongst those present were the Revs. H. V. Pigot William Bracecamp and C. Harvey Cook, Drs. Joseph and Richmond. Colonel Buckton, Messrs. W. P. R).lands, Robert .Pierpoint, J. H. Smethurst, M. McMillan, and Councillor Broadhurst.—Letters explain- ing 'non-attendance were received from Sir Gilbert Greenall. Mr. F. Monks, Mr. Henry hoberts, Mr. W. H. Bleckly, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, Mr. Hedley Taylor, Captain George Cro,field, Mr. Thomas Wads- worth,and a telegram from Aldermen Thomas Burton. The CETAIRMAN said the committee was born on the sth April, 1878 and he was present at that reeetirg and he himself recorded the minutes, because at that time the secretarial work was in the hands of Mr. Joseph Davies. The committee had prove* very great bless- ing to the institution. (Hear, hear.) He congratulated the committee upon having obtained during the year an income of £1,064 12s. 11d., Which was the hest year the committee had had since its existence. There had recently been a considerable extension to the Warring- ton Infirmary and it would require further funds to keep it going. There had been an increase in the nurs- ing and the domestic Staff and they would need further income tc carry out their proposals. He was sure they would use all the energies in their power to raise means for supporting a very noble and valuable institution. Mr. J. H. J. HAMPSON in presenting the statements of accounts gave some notable comparisons between what had been received in 1907 and the amount received in 1908. He said the total received direct from the works was £939 .19s. Bd. Messrs. Armitage Rigby had gone down from £47 17s. Bd. to £27 12s. 6d. That was mainly owing to the cotton strike. They Ind had some good rsceipts from other works. Messrs. Cros- field's had gone up from £2l to £4O, Messrs. Pearson Knowles from £lOO to £l5O. and Messrs. Richmond's Gas Stove and Meter Co. Ltd., from £lB to. £3O. Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co. Ltd., were unable to give anything owing to the state of trade. The total Amount handed to the Treasurer of the Infirmary was ' £l,OBl 12s. 11d.</p>
        <p>:ill% GEO. WALKER expressed regret at. the inability Of Alderman Burton to be present and said the meeting showed, the very keen interest that some of them were taking in that very desirable institution. They had struggled for many years to attain the sum of £l,OOO, and now they had reached it. . The recently formed Charities Committee existed for the purpose of raising money for the betterment of that institution, and he frusted that the Charities Committee would render their services when they required them. Mr. W. P. RYLANDS moved the adoption of the balance sheet and congratulated the Workingmen's Committee upon the extremely satisfactory results of their efforts. That was a very critical time in the his- tory of the Infirmary, for the recent enlargement of the institution made it necessary that they must have an increard income of at least £1,500 to £2,000 a year. The workingmen bad a right to say they would bear their share of the burden, hut it was not right to ask them to bear it all themselves, and he trusted that the appeal which was being made would result in a large increase of subscriptions. Wigan Workin.mea's CO-mmittee raised £8,371 in a year, and Leicester £l2OO so that they bad plenty to strive for.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH WILLIAMS seconded and the balance sheet was cordially adopted. Mr. C. L. WATKINS proposed that the delegates present form General Committee with power to add to their .namher.—Alderman Thomas Burton was elected chair- man ; Mr. George Walker &amp;ratty chairman; Mr. H. .7. H. Hampson and Mr. C. J. Holmes, secretary and auditor respectively. Mr. GEORGE WALKER then read his report on the Education Committee in the course of which he said there was one phase •of the Echication Committee's work which did not progress as satisfactorily as they could wish and that was the building of additional elementary schools. There was a great lack of accom- modation in the schools in the district., and-there were many schools in the town which did not rnit their needs nor the requirements of the Board of Education. The vital queston was : Were they getting value for their money? He had on a previous occasion expressed his doubt of that and it had received confirmation by the Parliamentary Secretary of the Board of Education The curriculum was too crowded, too many subjects attempted and taught indifferently, in fact there was too mach instruction and too little education. They prided themselves on their efforts to construct an edacational ladder, which, they hoped would enable the child of the elementary school to find its way to the University, hut for the main body of workers that educatiMital ladder was a failure. The workers had not the means to avail themselves of the opportunity. The prediction that elucation would prove the royal road to success was a failure., If that were so, why did Germany and America, who boast of being the most highly educated technically of all the nations, suffer from unemployment in a worse degree than ourselves? He recognised that education must play an important pia in our social regenertt'on and Ili the building up of all that was best in our physical and moral nature. In conclusion he said the education in our schools should be less literary, more practicable and better calculated than at present to adapt the child to its future occupation. _it must aim at developing char- ac'er and intellience rather than imparting book knowledge.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERT PrERPOINT proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman, Deputy, Secretary, Hon. Auditor and to )Jr. Walker for his report end observed that ha aveed absolutely with a great deal of what. Mr. Walker had said. He thought very strongly indeed, and hlways had thought that the bbject of education should be the preparation for the life that WAS before the child. and that the mere training in the way of how.to read rind write and to do nothing else, was very apt to terminate in nothing hut the reading of penny novels and other things of no value. The girls should be tiught more of the domestic duties, so that when thy came to be women they would be nseful.women. (Applause.) _</p>
        <p>Mr. MAssET seconded and the motion- was c'arried ananitnonsly. _</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were accorded to the Pres., Chief Constable and Pie, Warrington Rugby Football Clubs, Warrington Works Bowling Association, Hon. Surgeons, Home Surgeons, Matron and Nurses, Warring.ton Charities Committee, and his Worship the Mayor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WHOOPING COUGH. Immed;ate Relief and certain Cure by VENO'S LIGHTNINO COUCili CURE.</head>
        <p>WHOOPING COUGH. Immed;ate Relief and certain Cure by VENO'S LIGHTNINO COUCili CURE.</p>
        <p>The extraordinary efficacy Veno's Lightning Cough Cure in children's cough troubles is once more to the fore. Mrs. Archer, of 127, Cann Hall Road, Leyton- st one, writes:— My little boy, just 3 years old, suffered terribly for over 9 months from whooping cough. He coughed continuously all that time, and we did•not know what it was to have a good night's rest with him, and we thought he would not live. I tried nearly everything you can mention, emulsions, lozenges, etc., and spent pounds, but found nothing to do him any good, until tried Vene's Lightning Cough Cure. T got a 94d. bottle and in a week he was quite cured, and I cannot tail you how grateful I am for your wonderful remedy." Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is a safe and certain remedy for coughs and colds, bronchitis,asthma,estarrb, and all chest and throat troubles. Price 9%d., ls. and 2s. 91. a bottle at all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CONCERT AT BOLD-STREET.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT BOLD-STREET.</p>
        <p>' Mr. Levi Richardson possesses a happy knack of mak- ing a concert a success. Although the audience was not as large as might have been expecteA, the concert in aid of the Ellesmere-street Mksion held at Bold-street on Thursday evening last week, had the patronage of the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Forshaw), Mr. and. Mrs. C. E. Parker, Alderman and Mrs. Bennett. and proved highly enjoyable. The Maajor presided and in the course of a few remarks said it certainly was a pleasure to him te renqw his acquaintance with Bold-st., Chanel. His wife had a great love and affection for Methodism, and it was a great pleasure to him to take the chair at that concert, which was in aid of the funds to clear off a debt of about £2OO in connection with the Ellesmere-Area Mission. He understood they were doing a very good and praiseworthy work there. (Applause.) The programme was an excellent one, and was headed by. Mr. Levi Richardson (tenor), who sang "My Lute" with taste and expression. During the evening he also rendered "The Message," and took part in a duet with Mr. J. C. Brien, entitled "Watchman, what of the night." Mr. J. C. Brien was heard to advantage in "Son of Mine," " The Rebel," and "Bedouin Love Song." Mrs. Haviour's violin solos " Romance " and "Souvenir de Sorrento" were cleverly given and received enthusiastically. Clarinet aolos were contributed by Mr. W. J. Ford, and were entitled" Long Ago" and "Adieu." Miss Prince, who has a good command of elocution, recited "Two loves and a life" and " Legend Beautiful." Miss Silcock, th 9 young soprano who carried off the challenge cup the previous Saturday at Earlesto\vn, was loudly encored for her song "Orpheus and his lute." The popular test piece for two voices " Mayine " was sung beautifully by Miss Avice Beswick aod Mr. William Dutton. Mr. Hipkiss' juvenile prize choir, which ' was successful at the Warrington Musical Festival, rendered in an admirable way " The Shepherds" and " Sailor's Lullaby." Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., was an efficient accompanist.—A vote of thanks to the Mayor concludei an enjoyable evening.</p>
      </div>
