        <head>Sales by Auction</head>
        <p>Sales by Auction</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. SALE OF SURPLUS LANDS. TO BE SOLD BY AUCTION (Subject to Conditions Which will be declared at. the time of the Sale) by --VIESSRS. THOMAS SUTTON AND SONS in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall on Wednesday next, the 24th March, at 5 o'clock in the ajternoon, the Valuable SITES in Bridge-street, 4-nutstord-road and Liverpool-road, Warrington. LOT I.—A Plot of Freehold Land on the West side L)f Bridge-street, between the premises belonging to i„l.•_,T. J. Lee and Mr. J. Shaw, having a frontage to "luge-street of about 21 feet 6 inches and containing about 262 square yards. 133TT 2.—A Plot of Freehold Land on the West side of “uge-street, between the " Feathers " Hotel and the Pternises occupied by Messrs. G. and J. Weaver, having a frontage to Bridge-street of about 26 feet 6 inches and containing about 223 square yrirds. gLOT 3.—A Plot of Freehold Land on the North-east ia --"e of Knutsford-road, formerly the site of the : Mersey Bedstead Works" containing in the whole o_34_l4.quare yards or thereabouts with a Wharfage on River Mersey of 136 feet 3 inches together with the now standing thereon subject to two rents of 44 28. 4d. and £4 16s. per annum respectively. LOT 4.—A Plot of Leasehold Land on the North-east deiof Knutsford-road formerly the site of "Edelsten's" f 01.482 having a frontage to Knutsford-road of 240 .eet, and a Wharfage on the River Mersey of 240 feet, "d containin in the whole 1,475 square yards or t hereabout . g illis lot will be sold subject to an annual ground rent of £l5 Hs. Bd. and to the covenants and conditions t'ctained in an Indenture dated the first day of eiCember 1836. OT S.—A Plot of Land partly Freehold and partly nes.chold situate on the North side of Liverpool-road, ekining Sankey Brook and formerly the site of the sr" "mica' Works" containing in the whole 5,359 11',4, re yards or thereabouts cis lot will be sold subject to the yearly rents of 6s. 6d. and £l4 6s. 6d. respectively and the covenants L conditions affecting the same. triP 6.—A Plot of Land situate on the West side of 4 f uge-street adjoining the Royal Court Hotel having r"tage to Bridge-street of 21 feet' 5 inches and conta* Thits rung about 209 square yards. 9g9 • lot will be let on a building lease for a term of aft Years and will be sold subject to rights of light eeting the same but with the benefit of the party 11,1,1 shown on the plan. o.,elans and particulats of Lots 1 to 5 can be obtained aPplication at the-Town Clerk's Office and as to Lot °ll aPPlication to Henry Greenall &amp; Co., Solicitors. J. LYON WHITTLE. Town Clerk.</p>
        <p>TIIE-EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MARCH 20TH, 1909.</p>
        <p>The effect of Mr. THE POLICY AsQuiTH's great speech APPROVED. on *Tuesday was par- ticularly visible in Wednesdays divisiOn, and in the withdrawal (24. Mr. HARVEY'S amendment,directed against !ncreased naval expenditure. There is a say- handed down from one generation to another, to the effect that speeches hardly ever influence divisions. It was once true, lio doubt, in the days when there were only t 0 Parties, when the working classes were 11,1rePresented, and when members went `4nwn to the House with their minds ftilly inade up on all questions. In these days tile conditions are wholly different. In the :tesent Parliament, to say nothing of its .12eent predecessors, the voting has without doubt been frequently influenced by speeches, that he Mr. ,ASQUITH it may be said, indeed, 'at 'he seldom speaks on any important °ceasion on which a division is to take place Nvithout carrying into the lobby with hiin nie members who had entertained a differ- ' intention. •What he said on Tuesday "c-ept the House' off its feet. We are never likely to see a House of Commons tha i 8 unanimous on any question in the leasi- (lehatable. But if the division had been takenls When the PRIME MINISTER sat down, the voting would have been as nearly unani- hTh.nns as it has ever been in our political 131siterY when great questions of national r were under discussion. • The majority of 23 means overwhelming 48.Pproval of the Government's proposals. It ,31,F1lines that, in the opinion of the great bulk dour representatives in Parliament, the „,°vernment is not only taking adequate i.'easures for the security of the nation, but 21. the maintenance of our supremacy at sea i(3ll,der such conditions as have never existed if:ll3re. This fact, it should be understood, net called in question by the minority. ust the contrary is the case. The minority an expression of the opinion that the than is proposing to do much more irn the circumstances call for, and its vote therefore, an endorsement of the main Zenr°Bl3:siftaic that the Administration is in no % Zhei.Pbuildinngg short in regard to the duties - t-b e responsibilities forced upon it by the programme of our German 14,1gilbeurs. There has been on the part of -AsquiTH and his colleagues, the fullest 't,uossible acknowledgement of a grave situa- ela:: There has been a quality of Ministeri a theaeur that is altogether exCeptional, and • emoeonclusion of the whole matter is the paiaitsenlent of a policy which is no more ilable to the Government than to Mr. A. 4-ItvEY and his friends, but which is —4e necessary by the position we hold in</p>
        <p>he world and by our determination to main- am n it.</p>
        <p>Opposition speeches like that of Mr. BALFOUR on Tuesday and that of Mr ARTHUR LEE of Wednesday are; of course the speeches of men who aim at convincing' the country that the Government pro- gramme falls far short of the 'actual and pressing requirements. The Opposition; said Mr. LEE, had reason to fear that the Govern- ment had not secured complete safety for the nation, and could only trust that it might not be responsible for the national ruin. This is, we venture to think, less the language of panic than of partisanihip. It is the well-understood proceeding Aescribed by Hamlet as "tearing a passion to tatters." It would be necessary to consider it very seriously if it were a case based on accurate information. Fortunately it has no such solid foundation. For instance Mr. ARTHUR LEE stated that in April, 1911, Germany will be in possession of thirteen Dreadnoughts, but he is confuted in a mostl timely manner by the Secretary of State forl the German Navy, who told the members of the Reichstag on Wednesday that Germany will not have 13 Dreadnoughts until 1912. As the Government calculation is that there will be s6venteen German Dreadnoughts in that time, the margin of safety will therefore be much broader than has been calculated upon by the framers of the Estimates. Meantime Germany has not a single Dreadnought on the seas. That, sutely, is a consideration that should serve to calm the most panicky breasts.</p>
        <p>Warrington has always</p>
        <p>• A TERRITORIAL been so conspicuously RALLY REQUIRED. loyal and forward in its volunteering that it comes as an unpleasant surprise to learn that the local Territorial battalion is the only one in the South Lancashire Brigade which has not yet raised its full com- plement of men under Mr. Haldane's scheme. A letter in our correspondence column ' from Colonel Edelsten, J.P., the Secretary to the: West Lancashire Association, gives the figures of the various battalions,and points out that the Warrington Corps is 200 men short of its proper establishment. We feel sure that the Colonel's appeal will not fall on deaf ears. If the St. Helens and Widnes battalion can raise its 980. men, Warring- ton and Newton will certainly not be content to lag in the rear. Before the Territorial Army Scheme was intro- duced the Warrington Volunteer Battalion was over 1000 strong, and the 'leakage due to the change has been difficult toonake up: A special effort is called for, but we feel con- vinced that when the young men of the town are made aware of the circumstances they wilf rally to the ranks for the honour of Old ngland and their native town.</p>
        <p>Earl Winterton may be EARL WINTERTON. very- expert at asking t awkward questiogs in the House of Commons, but he is not an orator on whose words the nation is ever likely to hang with breathless interest. On Tuesday he spoke to the Warrington Con- servatives• for about an hour on the navy estimates, Ireland, and Tariff Reform, and almost hushed them to sleep. If he had belaboured the Government in whole- hearted fashion instead of admitting so nicely that the ministers were obviously sinceret—if misguided—or if he had roundly asserted in true Tory style that Tariff Reform would Mean work for all, would raise wages, and would not increase the price of neces.- saries, he might have raised a cheer. But there seemed to be a strain of conscien- tiousness in this young scion of the Tory aristocracy, and he was unable to beat the big drum to anything like an inspiring strain. He appear s to have all the qualifications for making an admirable disciple of " philosophic doubt," but spurring the Tariff Reform nag to a gallop is beyond him. Frankly speaking the demonstration was a failure politically. It might have passed from our ken unhonoured and unsung but for Mr. W. H. Bleckly's story of the parrot which died say- ing "Free Trade," "Free Trade." 'nit was a really important contribution to the ques- tion of the hour and will doubtless be fully discussed by advanced thinkers on fiscal policy.</p>
        <p>It has so often been</p>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR understood that Mr.</p>
        <p>NAILS THE FLAG. Balfour was on the pohit of making a momentous and final deliverance upon the subject of Tariff Refortn, while after the final deliverance there has seemed to remain the old want of finality about his point of view, that his speech at the Tariff Reform lunch has perhaps been credited with too little significance. Scarcely any one seriously expected that he would give any indication of the practical shape in which, as a states- man, he would desire to embody his ideas about that change in the nation fiscal sys- tem which he declares to be necesgary. But there were three notable features about his speech which ought not to be overlooked It was his first formal recognition of the Tariff Reform League. It was manifestly intended to increase, rather than diminish, the difficulties felt by those members of his Party who find Tariff Reform a stumbling- block. And it demonstrated that, however reluctant he may still be to lay down the lines on which he would ' establish his "reformed tariff," he has made up his mind that Tariff Reform as a plan for creating employment shall be officially adopted by his Party as a platform manifesto. The fiscal controversy has become too insistent to be wholly lost sight of even for a few clays' truce. It is often puzzling to determine how far Mr. Balfour's economic arguments are inspired by dialectic subtlety, and how far</p>
        <p>by ignorance of the practical conditions of industrial life. He professes to have dis- covered an insuperable dilemma in the co-existence of an outflow of British capital from this country and an inflow of German capital into it. If it is good for German capital to come in, then,he contends,it cannot be good for British capital to go abroad. Does he know that capital is often specialised as well as labour? Can he not understand that it is a good thing for German capital to introduce into this country new or More highly organ- ized industries? . And that it is also a good thing for British contractors to find scope for the employment of their capital in build- ing harbours and railways in undeveloped countries, thus earning profits—remitted to England as imports—by which the neces- saries of life are cheapened for the working classes of this country—a result which increases employment by increasing the effective demand of the working classes for better clothes and houses and furniture!</p>
        <p>The claims ofs the THE INFIRMARY. Infirmary upon the sympathy anid support of the townspeople are once more being urged, and in another column we publish an encouraging list of new and increased annual subscriptions. The annual statement of accounts discloses the fact that the expendi- ture for the past year has exceeded the income of the institution to the extent of £1,200. The Board of Management are in no way dismayed by this result, as they are confident that the inhabitants of the district will measure their generosity by its needs. In a, special appeal it is pointed out that an aver- age of 12 accidents for every day of the year receive attention. Almost every day a surgical operation is performed, and in addi; tion thousands of other cases 'annually receive treatment either as in-patients or out-patients. "We cannot regard contribu- tions" the circular aptly puts it "as mere charity, but rather a provision to oui•less fortunate fejlow creatures of a share in those resources of science of which a claim by the rich to a monopoly would be repugnant to every right feeling we possess." Subscrip- tions may be paid to Mr. W. Peter Rylands, chairman of the Board of Management • Mr. George Dakin, hon. treasurer; Mr. J. H. J. Hampson, secretary, or into any of the local banks.</p>
        <p>The Education problem THE ANCIENT is ,as ancient as it is EDUCATION difficult. Two and a PROBLEM, half milleniums ago they were wrestling with it, and the situation even then was far from being a new one. There reigned in Assyria from B.C. 668 to 625, Assurbainpal, a monarch of literary instincts wild built up a huge library at Nineveh. The books were of baked clay and stone, and they have been dug up in vast numbers. The deciphering of these ancient records has been a fascinating task and has given many surprises. A tablet has lately been read which tells of the establishment of the library. It • seems that the king set himself to the task not from literary motives but from political. Formerly all who desired to hold State positions or religious offices were educated in the temple schools by the priests. But the priests took to politics and some of them became Home Rulers. Therodach- Baladan led a movement in favour of Home Rule for the Chaldean provinces and the priests began to teach •it in the schools. The king decided upon a National system of education and made Nineveh the seat of learning. In those far off times, even as to- day, it was difficult to free education from priestly domination, and if the king of Assyria had taken a bolder line he might have solved for all time one of the problems which has vexed educationists during th whole of the 2500 years which have since rolled into the past. But whatever we may think of his educational policy there is no scholar who is not grateful for the founding of the library which, has been the store- house from which we have gleaned immense treasures of knowledge of the old Empires of the East.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>N3TES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>N3TES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Gray's final reply to Mr. B. S. Townroe on the question of Compulsory Military Training—or National Service—will be published next week.</p>
        <p>* * * Drawings by Mr. Wilmot Lunt frequently find a place in Punch." In a picture which appeared in last week's issue, a doctor and. patient's wife are represented in consultation outside the bedroom door and the patient's wife is saying to the doctor, "I say it is influenza doctor, what is your humble opinion ?" . .</p>
        <p>* * * The Conservative demonstration on Tues- day evening was generally voted to be very dull affair. Earl Winterton talked for an hour, hut his criticisms of the Government were anything but destructive, or capable of arousing party enthusiasm. It is a solemn fact that mote than one member of the audi- ence was sound asleep, and there are grave suspicions that a well-known Tory leader, who occupied a seat on the platform, was guilty of 'nodding," if he did not actually indulge in forty winks.</p>
        <p>There is nothing like -seizing opportunity with both hands. When the result of Mr. Lloyd-George's libel action was heard in Carnarvonshire the secretaries of several chapels which are in debt forwarded appeals to him for a portiiin, of the £l,OOO damages. The secretaries of several coming bazaars also made similar applications. It is a pity the Chancellor did not impose a heavier tax upon his traducers. He might have turned a much larger amounC, of bad money into' good channels. * * * Mr. Rufus Isaacs is said to have had sixl hundred guineas marked on his brief for coming down to Liverpool on Saturday to defend Mr. Louis Henshall, at the Assizes. If this statement is correct, the big-fee K.C. has surely established another record. Mr.Henshall pleaded guilty, and Mr. Isaacs' duties were, in a comparative sense, exceed- ing light, his remuneration for the time he was actually occupied in pleading what extenuation he could on prisoner's behalf I working out at something like one hundred guineas a minute. * * A Manchester merchant has recently afforded an object lesson to Tariff Reformers. He obtained three loaves from, Germany of the kind of bread eaten there and the prices for a 41b loaf in English money were : s. d. ' Black bread 0 6 (nearly) Fine bread 0 71 Proben (a fancy bread) The "fine bread" is the nearest equivalent to our working man's loaf, and it is open to all readers to compare the price they 'are paying for a 41b loaf with the 7-Id. paid for the " fine " loaf in Hamburg on the Bth of this month. TheY difference is obviously due to Protection, and Praection only.</p>
        <p>Mr. Adolph -Lazarus Langdon, K.C., who was leading counsel for the prosecution in the important Warrington case at the Liverpool Assizes last Saturday, has this week been appointed Recorder of Burnley. Mr. Langdon is a son of the late H. M. Lazarus, of Man- chester. Educated at Manchester Grammar School and Pembroke College, Oxford, he afterwards devoted himself to the law, and soon earned a considerable reputation on the northern circuit. Before removing to London he acted for some time as hon. secretary of the Manchester Jewish Board of Guardians. * * *</p>
        <p>The Member for the Borough, while sympathising warmly i with some aspects of the National Service ideal—and particularly that relating to the improvement of. the national physique—dissents sharply from the main contentions advanced by the Leaguers in regard to the invasion scare. In the long and informative letter, the first instalment of which we publish in another column Mr. Crosfield says with significant emphasis: do not believe for a moment that any respon- sible German is at the present time seriously contemplating the invasion of this country. All this talk about 'imminent invasion, is, to my mind, a wild chimera." The many advocates of peace, arbitration, and ultimate disarmament, in War- rington, will welcome this outspoken opinion from Mr. Crosfield, who, by the way, has had the priirilege this. week of discussing the question of national defence with both Lord Brassey and Mr. Haldane. * * *</p>
        <p>As we anticipated, the Warrington Educa- tion Committee has sanctioned the dismissal at three months notice of the married women teachers in its employ, the only exception being in favour of those whose husbands are incapable of maintaining them. • In view of the overcrowded state of the profession and the difficulty which many young teachers have in finding places, we think the com- mittee took a wise course, the reasonable- ness of which can hardly be questioned. The present condition of things is unjust and anomalous—though, of course, something eould be said on ethical grounds for the married teachers—and Mr. T. P. Bradshaw is to be congratulated upon his boldness in tackling the question. The Committee's decision will, we think, be endorsed by the great majority of the townspeople. * * *</p>
        <p>Orford Ward has been much in the public eye and mind this last four months, but there should now be a period of welcome quietud6 for the inhabitants. Mr. Halliwell, a popular Liberal nominee, has succeeded to the vacancy caused by the unseating of Mr. Peter Peacock, and as far as we' know, the Socialist party are not going to petition against his ieturn. The Conservatives, with becoming modesty, lay low and did not risk a rebuff by running a candidate. Seeing that the Socialist doubled his previous poll it is conceivable that a number of "true blues" cast their votes on his side with the idea of reducing the Liberal majority. The most noticeable feature of the election was the fall in the total poll from 1185 in November to 783. The recent petition has left a feeling of nervousness in the electorate and many voters said they were not going to risk going to gaol in order to vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LETTER FROM . •MR. CROSFIELD, A PEN-PICTURE OF THE QUEEN'S</head>
        <p>LETTER FROM . •MR. CROSFIELD, A PEN-PICTURE OF THE QUEEN'S</p>
        <p>HALL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Sir,—You ask me to send you a few notes on recent political events. I am very glad to do so,, and I will refer to a few of those events of most Importance in the order in which they have occurred during the last fortnight. So let me take first what it is no exaggeration to describe as the</p>
        <p>VERY MEMORABLE AND STRIKING MEETING that was, held in the Queen's Hall, here last Tuesday week. I think the report of that meeting and the splendid 'demonstration there in favour of Free.Tracje, would be something in the nature of a cold douche the 'next morning for those Tariff Reformers who were wise enough to read it. True, they would not find much mention of it in their own press. . The Daily Mirror de- voted just three little lines to the most important public meeting held this year! Even The Times gave but a mere fraetion of the weighty and powerful speech delivered by Lord Balfour. of Burleigh. I only mention that in passing, as an illustration of the tactics of the Confederates, an illustration too of what one has urged publicly in Warrington, that in this great controversy we shall for the most .part have to fight the press with the platform, so much of the half-penny sensational press in this part of the. Kingdom at any rate has been nobbled by the friends of monopoly. Not that the Free Trade press is losing ground in the country: on the tontrary it is gaining ground, and the advance now being _made by our old friend the Warrington Exam- iner is a very happy illustration of that progress. I understand that the Free Trade Union either have published, or intend to publish, a full report of the Queen's Hall meeting in pamphlet form, and in that case I will take care that a number of copies are sent to Warrington. In the meantime, just a word or two about those who were present at the mcetihg, and the chief speeches made there. With Lord Avebury in the chair, I saw on the platform the Prime Minister, Lord Balfour of Burleigh, Mr. Vivian, Lord James of Hereford, and other notable people. I have quoted the above names just to show the</p>
        <p>WIDE RANGE OF POLITICAL OPINION represented at that meeting. Striking evidence, I think, of the supreme national importance of the great question that brought these men, differing deeply as they do on other questions, together on a common platform. The chicf speakers, after the chairman, were the first three I have named. They spoke in that order, and a happier and more representative selection could not, I venture to say, have been made. The Prime Minister speaking first received a splendid ovation, and the musical honours were given in rather more musical style than some- times happens, thanks to the judicious lead and guidance of the beautiful Queen's Hall organ. Mr. Asquith's speech was masterly from begin- ning to end, and worthy of the occas on, and he broke new ground in folloWing and tracking down the latest hare that has been started by the Protectionists. .</p>
        <p>As a rule, Mr. Asquith speaks wholly without verbal preparation. Mental preparation before- hand of course there must be, at least in the sense that the speaker is master of his subject; but given the indispensible information, Mr. Asquith's command of language is truly as- tonishing. There is no one in the House here who can rival him in power of condensed and forcible expression, without preparation before- hand. He will make in the course of a single day three or four speeches without, as I have put it, a sy.llable or verbal preparation before- hand, and yet without saying a word too much or a word too little, every sentence in its place, every argument unfolded with as much logic and precision as if it had been excogitated for a week. This very afternoon I heard him in this fashion deliver „a very important speech on topics of quite national importance to an im- mense deputation which waited on him at the Foreign Office; and, by the way, there was one point in that speech on the subject of education, where the Prime Minister endorsed emphatic- ally a promise already, I think, given last autumn by the Chancellct of the Exchequer,, which I am certain will be noted with very special interest in Warrington. ' I allude to the question of largely extending the time for the repayment of loans made to local authorities for the building of school?. There cannot, I feel certain, be two opinions as to the wisdom and justice of assisting localities in that way.</p>
        <p>But to return to the Queen's Hall meeting. There I noticed that Mr. Asquith had fortified himself with a quite unusual supply of notes, and with the aid of these his speech was built up like a splendid piece of masonry into a solid weight of fact and argument, the force of which has been felt throughout the whole country. To avoid disturbance it had been necessary to limit the meeting to men, but there were three of these who tried to interrupt the Prime Minister by their shouts on the suffrage question. Evidently the meeting was in no humor for disturbances of that kind, let alone the destruction of the meeting itself, so those gentlemen made their exit, an exit which, I may mention, was a good deal more expeditious and forcible than ceremonious! So the Prime Minister was able to make a good ending to a fine speech, the intellectual force displayed during which reminded me of what an American once said of John Stewart Mill, namely that "his mind worked like a splendid machine." There is a fine weighty alliterative flavour about Lord Balfour of Burleigh's name, and if a personal remark of the kind is not out of place, I am sure that his substantial and imposing presence is quite in keeping with it: but though .1 have listened to him once or twice in the House of Lords I really wasn't at all prepared for the remarkable weight and force of the speech itself. In that respect one cannot pay a higher compliment to his speech than to say it was well worthy to tank with Mr. Asquith's. I see in the Westminster Gazette this after- noon another of our friend " F.C.G.'s " incom- parable sketches. The Duke of Argyle, standing on the edge of the Tariff sea, says: Do let me . shove you in my dear Lord Balfour. You'll find it beautifully cleansing, and you can come out when you like ; " but Cousin Jonathan who is away immersed in the water intervenes and says: "Come out when you like? I guess you just can't! Once you get in here ,you'll have to stick!" The explanation of that picture is a letter which the Duke of Argyle has been writing to The Times, where he recommends Free Traders to try washing in the world's stream of Fiscal Reform.</p>
        <p>"We can come out again when we like," he says. "Washers are not hopelessly damned. The United States of America and Canada and Holland are all bathers who have proved this. The naked Wee Fiscal Frees, even with Lord Balfour of I3urleigh to give them weight, will not persuade a public that believes in their countrymen rather than in foreigners that • the British are not capable of using the fiscal stream in concert with their sons whose strong arms know the currents. Let us shove the shiverers in!" As to that, I think the Duke will find Lord Balfour of Burleigh and many another Free Trade Unionist will take a deal of shoving. Or to use a homely expression Lord Balfour himself used on Tuesday week, the Duke will find him a "dour devil" to deal with. From that remark you will gather that, serious as was the subject of Lord Balfour's speech, it was not unrelieved with humour. On the contrary, there was quite a delightful flavour of humour running all through it. Indeed, a certain combination of outspoken frankness and sturdiness and humour in the speech delighted the whole audience. A more intelligent audience no man could have wished to address. I take it five-sixths, if not nine-tenths of them, . would be Liberal Free Traders, but they gave this powerful Conserva- tive statesman a most cordial reception from beginning to end._</p>
        <p>Not an-easy thing you will say for OUR FRIEND VIVIAN to follow these two speakers. It must surely be a matter of legitimate pride to him that coming after them towards the close of the meeting he was none the less, able to hold that meeting every bit as well as they had done. And deservedly so. The work he has been doing with tireless energy to bring about the better housing of the people 13V inducing those who would otherwise live in overcrowded and comparatively unhealthy centres to pitch their tents in the open country where there is plenty of fresh air and sunshine and where families dwell in happy homes; that work is beyond. all praise, and its value is so well linowneand recognised up here that the moment Vivian rose he received a fine and stirring reception. He is known to Ns'icry many readers of the Examiner in Warrington where we have been proud to welcome him on more than one platform, and will be proud to welcome him again in no dim and distant future; so your readers will understand how the strong ' ringing sentences of this vigorous, courageous, and manly speaker told gs he pointed out with graphic force the disaster it would be to the British Commonwealth if the day ever came when a mass of the people feeling the pinch of high-priced food were clamouring for abolition of Protective duties to British ministers whose hands were tied by compacts with our Colonies. To my mind, that is a very real danger which we should have to face if we tried to assist well- to-do farmers in the Colonies with preferences given at the expense of the mass of our working people at home.. Not sane and wise Imperialism but danger to the Empire I submit lies that</p>
        <p>n(The conclusion of Mr. Crosfield's letter, dealing with the naval estimates, and the report on industrial conditions in France, .will be giren next week).</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS. P</p>
        <p>aoteicr Grammar School. H. Hands of the above school was, on Monday, elected to an Exhibition of £2O for three yoars at Downing College, Cambridge, in open competition for history.</p>
        <p>LINSERD COMPOUND 'for boughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. 91d. &amp;Is.lid. Of Chemists only.—Advt• Music License</p>
        <p>The magistrates at the Borough Police Court on Monday; granted a music licence to the managers of the Skating Rink, with permission to remain open until 12.30. midnight on March 20th, the occasion of a fancy dress carnival.</p>
        <p>Work for the Unemployed. At a meeting of the Sites and Building Committee of the Warrington Education Committee a letter was read from the Warrington Trades and Labour Council', asking this committee to receive a deputation from that body as to the building or improvement of schools with • a view to work being found for some of the unemployed, and it was decided to receive the deputa- tion in question at the next meeting of thk3 committee.</p>
        <p>GARDEN TOOLS, Wire Nettiwz, ROOfing Felt, S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>"Peter Pan." "Peter Pan" registered its 1,025 th performance on Monday night last at the. Shakespeare Theatre, Liver- pool. The famous comedy will continue next week st the same houses, with matinees this afternoon, and the following Wednesday and Saturday. Miss 'Pauline Chase, the charming young lady who now plays the title role has been in the cast of "Peter Pan ".since the production Of the play in London five years ago. In June the company proceeds to Paris for a season at the Theatre du Vaudeville.</p>
