      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Cast-off Clothing.</head>
        <p>Cast-off Clothing.</p>
        <p>_Miss F. Beswicl:,. 47, Bewsey-street and Mrs. E. Jackson, 118, Wilderspool-causeway, appeal for cast= off clothing for the girls of the Silver-street Club, many of whom have had neither hats nor jackets during the recent severe weather.</p>
        <p>-- Rylands' Recreation Club. A concert of exceptional interest and attractiveness was given at the .Pavilion of Rylands' Workmen's Recreation Club, Gorsey-lane, on Saturday evening. The major part of the programme was sustained by the Perfection Prize Band, under the conductorship of Mr. Bedforth, their selections being :—Overture "Bohemian Girl" (Balfe); cornet duet, " Badinage " (I?iminer), Messrs. Ryder and Fairhurst; selection "Songs of Seotland " (Round); solo (soprano) "The Lost Chord" (Sullivan), Mr. Allisen ; selection "Great Britain" (Round). The solo playing of Messrs. Ryder, Allinson, Bedforth and Fairhurst was splendid and they had a most enthusiastic reception—in fact each item on the programme met with well-merited applause. Mr. Harry Sutton of Bolton, was the comedian and for quite 45 minutes- he had the audi- ence roaring with laughter; h,is patter and songs being very original. He had to respond three times, each item being applauded to the echo. Mr. W. W. Whittle had charge of the stage and arranged. the programme, and the concert was a great success in every respect.</p>
        <p>Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; it never fails. is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. T. Starkey. We regret to record the death of Mr. Thomas Starkey, manager of the Central Grocery Department of the Warrington Co-operative Society, who passed away at his home in Crosfield-street at 3.15 on Thursday afternoon, after five weeks illness. Mr. Starkey, who was 57 years of age, had been in the employ of the Society for forty years and was a faithful and conscientious servant. The funeral will take place at the Cemetery on Sunday. WOOD MANTELs, Kitchen Ranges and Tiles. Large stock. Estimates free.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Dramatic Society's Profits. At the meeting of the Warrington Dramatic Society, held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Tuesday night last, it was unanimously decided that a cheque for £lO should- be sent by the Hon. Treasurer, Mr. Arthur Browne, to each of the three charities to which the proceeds of the late performances "A Marriage has been Arranged" and. =` The Lady of Ostend" were advertised to be devoted, and a cheque has accordingly ! been sent to the Hon. Treasurers respectively of the Warrington Infirmary, the Warrington Nursing Institution and the Warrington Ambulance Brigade. LINSEED COMPOUND ' for Colizh4 and C.l is. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists only. 91d.,15.1id.,25.94.-Adv Penny Popular Concerts. A crowded attendance and an excellent programme again characterised the. concert given in the Bold- street Wesleyan Schoolroom on Saturday evening. The Rev. T. Miller presided, and during an interval in the programme, delivered an address. Songs were creditably given by Miss Carrie Hastings, and Messrs. Ireland an Plumpton. Picturssque action songs pro.Ved popular. Humorous selections were contri- buted by Mr. S. Aukland an created much laughter. LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs &amp;Colds. Relieve than and difficult breathing. 9id., Is. lid.—Advt.. •</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENr.FIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRB.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Personal.. At a meeting of the Warrington Education Com- mittee on Mondtiy evening Miss- Mary Williams and Mr. A. J. Jolley were re-appointed as selected mem- bers of the. Education Committee for the next three • years. It was mentioned that Miss Williams would shortly be leaving Warrington, but it was the desire of the committee to retain her services as long as she remained in the town.</p>
        <p>Mr. 0. Silvers' tone so, long the popular manager at the Royal Court Theatre, has been vastly successful on tour with a drama from his own pen entitled" The Nihilist" and during. the 'last twelve months has visited all our principal cities drawing record business in London and g-lasgow. Mr. Silverstone hopes shortly to bring the play to Warrington where he will be see' playing Prince Alexander. ' LAWN MOWERS !should now be repaired for the season. Special Plant. Prompt attention.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Caledonian Association. . • • Under the auspices of the Warrington Caledonian Association, and in aid of its benevolent fund, a lantern lecture on "The Outer Hebrides of Scotland" was given by Mr. Thomas Max, F.S.A., in the Masonic Hall, Bold-st reet, on Tuesday evening, the chair being occupied by the President of the Association,Councillor J. Fulton-Smith.—Mr. May, who, when in the Revenue Service, spent some years in the Western Isles of Scotland, gave an interesting description of, the Hebrides. The lecture was illustrated by a large number of slides, giving views of the fine cliff scenerft and of interesting and historical places throughout the islands, including a number of pictures of lone St. Kilda and its inhabitants.—On the motion of Mr. J. L, Davenport, a hearty vote of thanks was given Mr. May for his lecture.—A vote of thanks was similarly accorded the Chairman, on the motion of the Secre- tary, who stated that Mr. Fulton-Smith had taken great pains to arrange de lecture and ensure its success.</p>
        <p>" PIMP:RID" FIRE • GRItTES are unegnalled for eco menu in fuel and great heating power. Inspection invited.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. The Golden City Bazaar. _ _ _</p>
        <p>We would remind our readers that the " Golden City ',' bazaar in aid of tke Warrington Baptist. Church Extension will be held Th the Parr Hall on Wednesday Thursday, Friday and Saturday next. As a result of representations made to the Baptist Union concerning the position of the church in Warrington, a. grant of £5OO was allocated by the Twentieth' Century Fund towards the building of a new church. By the terms of this grant, the promise expires in June, 1909. The object of the bazaar is to raise the sum of at least £l,OOO to put the icongregation in a position to secure the grant and to enable them to commence building not later .than the spring of 1909. Particular interest centres in the bazaar as it will be opened on the first day by Mrs. D. Lloyd George, wife' of the Chancellor of the Exchequer. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, MP.' will. preside. The other opening ceremonies will be per- formed as follows :—Thursday, by Mr. .James R. Slater, of Liverpool, chairman Mr. Henry Roberts; Friday; by Mr. James Dodds, Chairman, Mr. Norman Balmford ; Saturday, children's day. The Hall will be charmingly decorated and splendid entertainments and attractions are announced.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' (Aniseed, Senega, Squil ""&amp;o.), for Coughs. Of Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
        <p>LINSEED CONIPOUND with warm water is an cxeel lent gargle for sore throat.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street Band of Hope. A large number of children and adults were present in the schoolroom last Monday evening when a service of song entitled " Driven from home " (illustrated with limelight views) was rendered by the members of the choir.—Mrs. Simpson presided, and gave a brief but useful address, after which she introduced Mrs. James Dodds, • who had kindly consented to give the connective readings.—The musical portion . included solos, which were creditably rendered by Misses Mary A. Rothwell, Nellie Lomax, Ada Whalley, Flossie Adams and Irene Cun.nington (the latter being a little girl with a sweet voice, which bespeaks a very promising 'future. Messrs. Thomas Allen and Percy Keen also sang; while Mr. I'. Knowles filled the posi- tion of conductor, and Mr. A. Taylor as organist.—The lantern was manipulated by Messrs. Fred Simpson and J. Oultram.—Replying to a vote of thanks, Mrs. Dodds expressed pleasure at being able to attend and assist in a work of such importance as was Band of Hope work, an after complimenting the children upon their ,splendid conduct during the service, and giving a few words of kindly helpful advice for the future, a highly successful gathering, concluded with the Benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>COAGULTNE," KLINX," TENASITINE.'</head>
        <p>COAGULTNE," KLINX," TENASITINE.'</p>
        <p>Cements for mending all thins.—Advt. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. "The Stitch in Time." The Lancashire County Branch of the National Service League is about to present this invasion play, by Miss T. L Crosfield and Mr. B. S. Townroe, in various parts of the county for propaganda purposes, and on Wednesday evening there was a special per- formance for the local Territorials and their friends in the dining hall at Messrs. Crosfield's works. A large audience assembled and appeared to find the play highly interesting and entertaining. There have been one or two alterations and improvements since the first-production. Captain G. R. Crosfield, as Colonel Allison, V.D. now takes the part of an explanatory " chorus " and to him falls the task of pointing the moral of the story. This, of course, allows of the pruning or omission of certain speeches originally allotted to other characters, and there is certainly a gain from the artistic point of view. Then a diversion is introduced in the form of the thrilling capture of a spy. Mr. William Atkins, ,the village cycle repairer, is found to be in league with the enemy and despite his protestations of innocence and the hurling of maledictions at hiss accusers, he ,is ordered to be shot within five minutes—much to the delight of the audience. Pluffles, the pet dog, has no longer to pay the penalty Of martyrdom for England's lethargy,an adequate supply of tinned meat having bed. secured before the invaders appear. There were several changes in the cast,Mrs.J.Becke portraying Hilda Watson Captain J. Becke, Reginald Melcham' • M.P.; Captain Crosfield, Colonel Allison; Captain Avery, Private Mulvaney. A satisfactory performance was given and the principals were rewarded with hearty applause at the close. During the evening selections were given by Messrs. Crosfield's orchestra and a contingent of the Apollo Male Choir. COlonel J. D. Fairclongh, commander of the local Territorials, thanked the League for tying to give a fillip to thebattalion by arranging the entertainment. They all hated war and the best way to prevent war was to be Prepared against it. If such an event did happen, thousands of young men would come forward and rally round the flag, but throngh lack of training they would not know how to shoot . and to act together and they would probably be mown down by hundreds. We must be prepared and not wait until war happened before we bestirred ourselves.</p>
