      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE</p>
        <p>Special Report of the Manchester Meeting.</p>
        <p>A meeting of industrial and professional women took place in the Free Trade Hall in Manchester on Wednes- day, to support Women's Suffrage. The ball was crowded, and the speakers had a good reception. One of the most interesting people on the platform was Dr Garret Anderson, the first, and as yet, the only Lady ISla,yor. Dr. Anderson is also interesting as being one of our first lady doctors and the sister of Mrs. Fawcett,. Another -particularly interesting figure was Miss Hornirnan, the manageress of the Gaity Theatre at Mancbester,where plays are acted that appeal chiefly in the cultured classes. Miss Horniman, when she rose to second a vote of thanks to the Chairman, met with a storm of prolonged applause. Mrs. Garret Anderson, in her introductory speech, pointed out that 74,000 married women worked in the hancashire cotton mills, and it had been proposed to take their work away from them, This, she said, would be a serious check to marriage, and a blow to the health of the country, which dependei largely on women's work. Further, womeia are now not permitted by law to make bouquets after 8 p.m. As it is between 8 and 10 that they are chiefly in request, many women have lost their employment. Why does not Government go further, and extend these laws to their logical conclusion and say that nurses and lady doctors should not work after 8 p.a.. or even say that women should not feed their babies after a certain hour. It has been proposed to put a stop to the barmaid profession. What are these women to turn to ? Miss Murby, representing the Civil Servants, moved a resolution in favour of Women's Suffrage. She showed that women in the Civil Service for the same work as men, got a half and a third of what men got, and the upper branches of the service *ere closed to them. It is said that this is because men have wives and children to support. but Government does not take into con- sideration whether this is so or not, nor does it consider whether women woikers have relatives to support or not. Asa matter of fact an astonishing proportion of women are supporting relations,—in some instances, the wives and children of deceased brothers. Yet Govern- ment is called the model employer. It may be that to men, it is not to women. Finally, she told how women Civil Sercants had been forbidden to take a prominent part in the Women's Suffrage movement, though many of them had wished to do so.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Grant Richards, the editor of the English- woman, the new woman's monthly,seconded the motion. She said that it was prejudice that stood in the way of granting the Suffrage. Women, as well aq men paid for the dockyards and armies of England. Why should they not vote for the uses they are put to? She said that nowadays Parliament was to a great extent a court of .arbitration between employers and employed. By representation the interests of both were safeguarded, except where women were concerned—mid their interests were absolutely unprotected. •Miss Roper spoke next. She gave statistics concern- ing many manufactures and showed how in certain trades, such as the cotton trade, upon which England depended largely for its wealth, the majority of workers were women. In the cotton trade 628 out of a thou- sand were women.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Haworth spoke from tbe educational point of view. Girls' schools ought tlo be inspected by women she said, but as a matter of, face there were hardly any women inspectors. A short time ago six women inspectors were appointed by Government to inspect schools for little children. They brought to light so many abpses that they were withdrawn, and the inspect- ing was/left to men who knew nothing of the needs of little children. With thit vote, she said, would come new opportunities for women in school, medical, and university work. Other speakers. were, Miss Chrystal McMillan, who told of her experiences in the else of appeal before the House of Lords, in which she pleaded the right to vote, as ber name stood on the register of her university. She pointed out that woman, according to the law, is not a person where rights and privileges are concerned, but only in cases of duties and obligations. She told bow she had visited the harems of Tanjier, and seen soulless women more like animals than human beings. They had been stunted into this state. The anti-suffrogists, she said, like those who believed in harems, wished to stunt the moral growth of women. Other speakers were Miss Gore-Booth, Miss Reddish, and Mrs. Sargent Florence. The meeting was a most impressive one. It was a case of the anti-suffragists' theories being upset by bard facts, coming from the representatives of work- people themselves. It was remarkable, pots° much for its eloquence, as for its unvarnished truth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Warrington and District Canine Society.</head>
        <p>Warrington and District Canine Society.</p>
        <p>A Committee meeting of the above Society was held on March 11th at the Lion. Hotel when the schedule for the members' show to be held on May Bth was drawn up, for 38 classes, At this show four cups will be competed for. Several classes have been guaranteed, spedial prizes and medals. Judge: Mr. Lloyd Lewis. At this meeting Mr. F. Townley, 9, Windsor-street, Warrington was appointed assistant hou. secretary.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the New Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.—WHlTE &amp; WRIGHT' Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw st., opposite top of Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PASSIVE RESISTERS SUMMONED AGAIN.</head>
        <p>PASSIVE RESISTERS SUMMONED AGAIN.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police on Thursday before Messrs Charles White (chairman), W. Roberts and J. Rymer. Young, William Clatworthy, 21 Lovely-lane, appeared for non-payment of a portion of the poor rates, amount- ing to 9s. 4d. The Chairman: Why don't you pay it? Defendant : It is the education rate I object to pay. There are a good many things I submit to till we "get to a certain point. Chairman: The law sass you must pay. Defendent : My idea is to put the law always first,and that the law is higher than the man who makes the law. Chairman: Why don't you pay it under protest instead of being summoned. Defendant: We do not like going back on the stand we have taken. An orden was made.</p>
        <p>Thomas Dale, 7, Priestley-street, was summoned for non-payment of poor rate amounting to is. 4d. Defendant: I object on these lines. Ido not believe in paying any money for denepiinational purposes. It is a matter of conscience with me.</p>
        <p>Chairman: You are carrying it to a point of absurdity. t is ridiculous.</p>
        <p>Defendant: ft may appear so in your opinion, hut not in mine. I disapprove paying for denominational ,teaching.</p>
        <p>An order was made.</p>
        <p>the Q ss• oukTs =</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. J. W. Hayward (chairman) end A. J. Jolley.</p>
        <p>FALSE ASTESTATION.—Frederick Chesters, a private of the King's Liverpool Regiment, was sentenced to one month's imprisonment for making a false astesta- tion whilst snPsting _</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before ihe Mayor (in the chair),the Deputy Mayor (Alderman • Smethurst), Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. W. Roberts, W. Furness, H. Thornton, W. Richardson, T. Locker and G. Pilling.</p>
        <p>SHOP THEFT.—Cbarle‘ Campbell, Ship Yard, was charged with having been drunk on Saturday night, and further with stealing 2 caps from outside the shop of Messrs. Unsworth, Lime-street, about 8 o'clock the same night. He pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable, stated that the prisoner was loitering outside the shop, when he was seen by the son of the manager to take a cap from the door, the lad followed the prisoner into HorsemarkA-street, and gave them in charge of Sergeant Barlow. The prisoner was also seen to take a cap from the rail outside the shop about three weeks ago. Mr. T. Shaw. the manager of the shop followed prisoner and took the cap horn him, but let him go as there was no policemen about. In reply to the charge prisoner said that he only " broke teetotal " that night. (Laughter).—He was ordered to pay 20s. and costs (22), or one months' imprisonment in default.</p>
        <p>SLOT METER THEFTS.—Henrick Taylor or: JoSep)) Beatty, a private of the Ist Kings Regiment stationed at Kinsale, was charged on remand with (1) robbing a slot meter at the dwelline house 11, Fountain-street, and stealing the sum of ss. Bd. and on March 3rd (2) with committing the similar offence and stealing the sum of ss. Bd., from 53, Old-road. Prisoner pleaded guilty to both charges. The Chief Constable stated that prisoner was before the court on Tuesday last and was remanded in order, that particulars could 'be obtained. He (the Chief Constable) had communicated with prisoner's commanding officer, who had stated that prisoner'had a good character. Prisoner had been lodg- ing at 53, Old;road, and on March sth the landlady found the rnetor broken open and the money gone. She reported it to the Corporation. The same circnrnstances occurred with reference to the dwelling house 53 Old- road. Gas meter thefts bad been very prevalent of late there being as many as seven or eight in a week, and considering the circumstances of the case, the tebants of the houses in which the thefts are comtnttted having to make the money good be wished, the magistrates would make an example of the prisoner. Prisoner was sentenced to One month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. C. White (chairman), Alder- men Smethurst, and Ellison, and Mr. J. W. Hayward. THEFT OF BRASS.—Joseph Booth, labourer, 24, Cyril- street, was charged 7ith stealing on the 16th March from a warehouse at' the Mersey-street Gas Works, two brass unions and a quantity of scrap brws, value 25.6 d. the property of the Corporation.—Prisoner pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that about, 2.30 the previous afternoon Mr. Barker, the stores keeper, was told to secrete himself and keep the prisoner under observation. He saw the prisoner teke the scrap brass from a barrel and as he was coming out he took the two unions from the warehouse. Barker immediately followed prisoner and took him to the timekeeper's lodge where, he emptied his pockets, disclosing the stolen brass. ~The police were communicated with and prisoner was taken to the Police Station. The prisoner had worked at the works for 11 or 12 years and there was nothing against his character.—The Chairman said be was very sorry to see prisoner there and the Bench would try to deal as leniently as possible with him.— He was fined 20s. and costs. or 14 days' imprisonment. DRUNK on FALL?—Charles Webb, Priestley-street, was charged with having been drunk on Monday, 16th March. He was represented by Mr. T. SA Steel, solicitor. Before evidence was given the Chief Coif- stable said that if any suspicion arose as to the man being drunk be would like the case adjourned until the following day in order that medical evidence could be secured. P Turner stated that about 7.10 on Monday night he was on duty at Market-gate, when he was called to the man who had fallen down the steps of the public convenience opposite the Market Place Post Office. Tftere was no question about defendant being drunk.—Mr. Steel's defence was that defendant, hal fallen down the steps accidentally and rendered himself unconscious.—The case was dismissed on payment on costs. •</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. Charles White (in the chair), W. Roberts and J. Rymer Young. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—W. Peplow, of Eccles, and formerly of King-street, Warrington was summoned for cruelty to a horse on October 9t1,1907' by working it in an unfit state.—Defendant pleaded guilty.— Inspector Nye said that he was on duty at 9.0 a.m. on the day in question in Knutsford-rd., when he saw the defendant in charge of a black mare, attached to a spring cart laden with empty bags. On examining the animal he found a raw wound underneath the saddle 2iin. long and I.3in. wide. The animal was in a shock- ing condition and very weak, and waS • quite unfit for work.—Defendant said be had only bouuht the horse the day previously for £5 to which VI r.' Nye replied : I would not give him ss. for it.—Fined 10s. and costs. FRlDAY.—Before the Mayo t(AI derman Forshaw),(in he _chair), Deputy Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Mess'rs. A. J. Jolley, W. Furness, H. Thornton, G. Pilling and J. Hepherd. BOOT THEFT.—James Gibson, a stranger, was charged with stealing a. pair of boots from outside the shop of Messrs. Brown and Co., Ltd., Horsemarket-street, on Saturday evening last.—The Chief Constable stated that he had circulated prisoner's linger prints, description, &amp;c., all over the country in order to obtain particulars of his antecedents and had received information from Scotland Yard that prisoner was a well-known man. He started his criminal career in October 190-7, and•had been in prison most of the time since, serving sentences received at Liverpool and Manchester.—Prisoner was committed for two months with hard laLour.</p>
        <p>UNHAPPY MARRIED LlFE.—William Mather, Brook- land-street, was summoned at the instance of his wife for being an habitual drunkard, also for persistent cruelty, and she applied for a- separation order.—Mr. Steel, solicitor, represented the complainant, and stated that the couple hnd been married for 25 years and had seven children. Defendant was a wiredrawer, and earned on an average £1 10s. per week. He sometimes finished work in the morning at 6 o'clock but did not land home until about 10 o'clock, generally in a drunken condition. He had assaulted his wife, and but for the interference of the children he would have dealt her serious injury. On one occasion he came home, threw her on the floor, lacked her and gave her two black eyes, which swelled immediately. .He had also threatened her with a poker. He had boasted that he could knock the wall dogn as he was so strong. He had also threatened to knock defendant's head through the wall.—Complainant corroborated these statements. and said her husband acted like a madman, but he had given her 30s. per week regularly since Christmas.— Sarah Martindale, a married daughter residing at Poulton, near Blackpool, said that she had been living with her mother since Christmas,and she wasnow afraid to leave her.—A separation order was granted, allowing complainant 17s. Gd. per week and the custody of the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. :John White (in the chair), C. White, R. C. Parr and C. E. Parker. MINOR OFFENCES.—CharIes Beale was charged with allowing his horse and cart to stand unattended in Warrington-road, Padgate, on 3rd March. Fined ss. and costs.—Thonias Stubbs, of no settled ,abode, was charged with being a deserter from the 4th Manchester Regt. since 15th June, 1907. P.C. 1327 (Wi:kes) of Newton, spoke to arresting prisoner. He was handed over to an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Bold-street Weslesan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Weslesan Church.</p>
        <p>At the Evangelistic meeting held, in the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, On Sunday evening at the close of the ordinary service, the Rev. E. B. Keeling presided, and Mr. R. J. Peake gave an aldress on the subject of "The Young,ltuler." There was a fair attendance. • Bewsey-road Wesley Guild. -</p>
        <p>Novel episodes are alnays happening at the various Wesley Guilds. The Bewsey-road Wesley Guild, like the rest of the Guilds,enjoys having something " out of the ordinary," so, on the suggestion of Messrs. Grice and Massey. two of the" Guilders," a curio exhibition took place in the Lecture Hall adjoining the Church last Monday evening. This exhibition had been looked forward to by the members, and all contributed some- thing to make it a success. A special collection of shells, kindly lent by Mr.J. W. Hayward, was exhibited. Captain Bailey's curs were a centre of interest, for they were various implements of war,which he collected during the late wpr with the Boers. Mr. W. Stretton's model of a steamship and a sailing vessel also claimed much attention. Two hundred foreign coins were shown by Mr. Once. A letter penned by John Wesley in March of 1782, and which the Rev. E. B. Reeling exhibited, drew quite a small crowd. Relics from all the quarters of the globe were to be seen, and the exhibition was voted agreat success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at this meeting on Sun- day, presided over by the . Secretary. Mr. Drewitt of Stockton Heath gave the address upon John's letter from the Isle of Patmos, laying stress on the words "I write to you young men"—young men of.thought and intelli-</p>
        <p>genre, to prepare for greater service and usefulness. What an army it would be if all would consecrate them- selves to the service of evangelism of the world. The Chairman said next Sunday would be the. National P.S.A. Federation Sunday. when hymns would be sune by over half a million P.S.A.'s and Brotherhoods all over the Kingdom, and at four o'clock, Greenwich time, one special hymni would be sung by all the Federation. After that a penny collection would he taken for rhe Lancashire Federation as well_ as the National Federation Fond: The Chairman next referred to death °Sone of the first officers of the Movement— Mr. Joseph Podmore. sen. A short time ago they had the pleasure of conpratulatiug Mr. and Mrs. Podmore on reaching their golden wedding day. Mr. Podmore had always taken -a very lively and loving interest in their work, and helped them in many ways. They felt his loss greatly, and he would ask the meeting to agree that a vote of condolence be sent to Mrs. Podmore the widow, and the members of the family, who were now passing under a great cloud of trouble and bereavement. Mr. R. Lewis proposed and Mr. Joseph Owen seconded, the audience standing in their places as the vote was quietly taken. Mr. A. Healey and D. McEacher took part in the meeting. Mr. J. Wallington presided.at the organ.</p>
        <p>• Cairo-street Chapel. Special Services. Special sermons were preached last Sunday by the Rev. R. Roberts, of Bradford, in the Cairo-street Unitarian Chapel: In the morning the rev, gentleman addressed a good congregation on "The ever-living religion." He considered that man's religion was the highest expression of his experience in life. NI that expression they were prepared to find the ever-living religion, which had abided throughout the ages. A man was not a full individual who did not realise the place of religion,-and the play of its forces in his individual life. They discerned men in incalcuable ages making rude attempts to find in this world some hone or sanctuary. That wrestle for religion went back for millirns of years. By that man was united into one great brotherhood. The thing that created a passion for social service was religion. It was religion that helped man on his toilsome upward journey. "Humanity outgrows itself." he went on, and "creates new Christs; for to us the Saviour of the past. has but a pathetic message for the need of to-day." The .apostles were but symbols of ancient beauty. Had they then no apostles of their own ? They were face to face with position, the like of which had never been seen in the recorded story of man. They had enormous possi- bilities of power of production and of wealth. They had called forces into being, which had never before 'played upon the earth. Was the age to be a Godless or a materialistic age ? There was more room' for usefulness and serviceableness now than ever before, and it was for them to do it.</p>
        <p>In the evening the rev. gentleman again preached to a good congregation and took for his text "A social Gospel." ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church. Memorial Service to Mr. Podmore.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church. Memorial Service to Mr. Podmore.</p>
        <p>A Memorial service to the bite Mr. Joseph Podmore was cohducted by the Rev. John Yonp.e, at Wycliffe Congregational Church, last Sunday evening. Special hymns were sung by the congregation including" Lord Thou hasp been our dwelling-place," ",Lord, of Life</p>
        <p>the Source and Giver" (written by Mr. Alfred Corney: and " We are but strangers here." • The choir render&amp; the anthem "No shadows yonder."</p>
        <p>The rev, gentleman based his address on" They that be planted in the House of the Lord shall flourish in the couris of our God; they shall still trrin,,' forth fruit in old 'age " (Psalm xcii. 13th and part of 14th verse). He thought the text fitted the life and described the character of the beloved Christian brother whose memory they honoured. Jo,eph Podmore had verified the text by living it. He (the preacher) had known him upwards of 32 years. The first time he saw him' was on August Bank Holiday Sunday in 1976, the day when I e (Mr. Yonge) preached in that pulpit for the first time, is the " supply" for the .day. He remem- bered how his face attracted him. That Sunday he looked down from the pulpit for a sympathetic face and he found one-when he looked upon Joseph Podmore. " Father" said one of their little Sunday school lads not long ago, "Who is that old gentleman who teaches the infanta?" ," Oh," saifi his father "That is Mr. Podmore !" " Father " added the boy "Isn't. he like a sunbeam ? " That father might be proud of that boy for he was a discerner cf spirits. A sunbeam was a true description of their friend, and he %as glad that the likeness of him prirlied in the " Examiner" of last Saturday conveyed that identical impression. That was just the face which had met his continual gaze, during all those long years of his ministry to that Church. Mr. Podmore was as good, as true, as sympatbetic,and as devoted as he looked. He lived up to his face, and his whole life was worthy of remembrance, as his portrait was worthy of a conspicuous place in the homes of his wide circle of friends. They ,knew and appreciated the interest he shewed and the work he devoted to Friendly Societies, and they rejoiced in the honours that had been accorded him. They knew of his loyalty to political reform, and of his sympathy with the amelioration of social conditions, hut he (the preacher) would speak of him in connection with his religious life, and espeeially of his connection with Wycliffe. Mr. Podmore formally joined that Church in the earlier part of 1876, in the November of which year, he (Mr. Yonge) became pastor. From he age of 20 to 21, he had been connected with Dallam-lane Chapel which bad sprung from the Primitive Methodist Church at that kime located in Old-road, Latchford. In those days Mr. Podinore was connected with a vigorous Band of Hope, in which he took a principal part, and formed a Brass Band, which did useful work in advancing the, cause of temperance in the town. He spent a vast, amount of time in compiling and arranging the music for the different instruments ; for in those days band music was not so readily obtainable as it was now. It was a labour of love ' with him, just,as the same kind of work had been in his later years for the infant scholars. Circumstances ekentually arose at Dallam-lane which led to many leaving that place and at that time Mr. Podmore and some others came and settled down at Wycliffe. That was in the days of Mr. Jessop's pastorate, so that his connection with Wycliffe must really date hack more than forty years. In Band of Hope work at Wycliffe he was ever a willing and diligent worker, and was the means of gathering a good choir. Later on, when Mrs. Reaney arranged for popular services in the old Public Hall, Mr. Podmore rendered her much practical musical assistance, which she acknowledged by the gift of some valuable books. He was also one of the originators and founders of the British Workmen Public-house, along with Mrs. Reaney And Mrs. Nield, and he was also a hard worker in the "Good Templar" movement. As long as strength enabled him, he took his full share in the arrangements for the Infants' Treat on Walking Day, and collected money so that each of the little people should get some prize or gift. His greatest yearly delight had i been n the annual Infants' Tea Meeting. None of those who had seen him on those occasions were likely to forget how his face beamed with delight, and how' his clear silvery voice made itself heard above all the merriment of the children's voices. Of his services to that church as deacon, treasurer and administrator of the Sacramental offerings for the sick and poor; as for many years a day school manager; as a visitor of those in trouble; as an advisor and counsellor in times of care and difficulty; as an occasional leader of week evening services, and as a preacher at the Cockhedge Mission, time would fail him to speak. 04 the cordial interest he displayed in all the welfare of Wycliffe; of his sympathy with the teaching the pulpit; of his hive for what he deemed a great theme and a,good discourse: he need not linger to note. We lived unhappily, in times when the old firm adhesion to Sabbaths and Sanctuaries bad become sadly relaxed, and in too many cases altogether abandoned. It used to be said that in "place of the fathers shall be the children," but it was far less so now than in years gone by. Should not the present day generation con- alder whether their looser loyalty to Sabbath and Santuary was as good and profltable,and even pleasurable las the stricter loyalty of those who bad gone before</p>
        <p>them? Might it not be well for all to seek to become more faithful "followers of them who through faith and patience were now inheriting the promise?" The vacancies in the life and service of Christ's Church created by the elevation of godly parents to the Upper Heavenly Temple, should be eagerly filled up by the cheerful accession ,of such as "inherit the priceless legacy" of such true Christian example.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. I'. A. Turney gave an addresq on" Personal experience of the romance of a Central Mission "on Wednesday before a fair attendance in the Lecture Hall adjoining the church. Mr. E. .1. Gerrard presided and the Rev. E. B. Keeling supported. The rev, gentle- man gave a description of .the home mission works at the Leeds Central Mission.. He emphasized the neces- sity of going to the people and not waiting till the people came to them. He gave a great many examples of the way in which people had been uplifted and blessed by going to Christ. He mentioned that a com- plete change in the lives of men who had ,been victims of drink and folly had been effected by the mission. He had been working in the noble cause of Christ for three years along with Samuel Chadwick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</head>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</p>
        <p>Appropriate sermons for Hospital Sunday were preached by the Pastor (Rev. R. E. Lethern). In the morning he took for his subject "Prisoners of Hope" (Zechariah ix. 12). In the evening "A bruised reed shall he not break " (Isaiah xlii. 3), formed the title of a powerful and interesting • sermon. In the course of his sermon the rev. gentlen.an said that a reed, a bruised reed, was man. With many a fair feature in his manifold nature that still showed that it was God's hand that fashioned him, how fickle and inconstant, swayed now, this and now that 'way with every varying mind, not master of circumstances hut at their mercy, trembling like a reed in the presence of the mighty forces of, the'universe ; crippled by the competition of creatures stronger than himself; crushed under the heels of herds of evil passions that hurled their wild fury against him-and withdrew ; smitten by sin that like a blighting tempest had swept through the moral universe and left him bruised and broken. Did they not know and feel th.tt the facts or life, the prophets emblem was only too sadly trite? Winn as some,.ody had said was the r*ed of the sentient universe. Man was but a reed, said Pascal. the feeblest thing in nature, but he was a reed that thought,. It needed not that the universe arm to crush him. An exhalation or a drop of water. sufficed to crus him. Man was yet nobler than the ir universe for he ew that he died and the universe even in prevailing ag nst hint knew not its power. In con- clusion Mr. Letheren said : "'Has sin or sorrow or the conflict of circumstances left you a bruised reed? Christ stoops to you. 'Trust him. Let him restore you. Let Him tealize in you and thus join the possibilities of holy living for which God created you."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Latchford Wesleyan Church. ' Opening of New Organ.</head>
        <p>Latchford Wesleyan Church. ' Opening of New Organ.</p>
        <p>A two manual organ kindly presented by Miss Hartley wa§ opened on Thursday evening at the Latchford Wesleyan Church. Mr. Frank Hickman presided at the organ' and submitted an admirable selection of pieces which brought out to advantage the sweet rich tones of the instrument. Through the generosity of Mrs. Hat tley, the motto "Enter into His gales with thanksgiving and into His courts with praise," had been nainteil over the rostrum.and produced a pleasing effect. Mr. James Dodds presided and was supported by the Revs. E. B. Keeling and John Gunnell. Mrs. A. Booth was the vocalist and rendered " My heart ewer faithful " and " I will extol Thee ! 0 God" very expressively. The following are the items which Mr. Hickman played on the new instru- ment :—Overture in B (Ambroise Thomas), " Question and Answer " (Wolstenholme)," Sonata III" (Mendels- sohn), " Autumn Song; " (Schumann), "Song of Melody" (Clegg),' " Cantelene Pastorale " (Mr. H. Higgs). " Hallelujah! for the Lord God omnipotent reigneth " (Handel).. The CHATRM AN, in the course of a very brief address, said he feared that anything in the way of a speech upon such an occasion would he out of harmony with the character of the service, the introduction of a discord- ant interlude. But he did desire to say hoW deeply he appreciated the honour which Miss Hartley.the generous ponor of the organ, had done him in asking him to pre- side on that occasion. An organ was an aid to reverence and a great help to worship, and in Northern Britain where half a century ago the Presbyterians had abjured the use of the organ in their Churches, its absence was now the exception and not the rule. He hoped they would not rest till they got rid of the debt upon their now coimpleted premises. He was not a member of the Wesleyan Church personally but he would help them so far as he could especially as he was an employer of labour in Latchford. He asked fora liberal collection.</p>
        <p>Mr. WI I LIAM PEARSON proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. and Miss Hartley, Mr. Dodds, Mr. Binns (the donor of the organ stool), Mr. Hickman and Mrs. Booth. (Applause.) The Rev. E. B. KEELING in si;conding observed that Mr. Dodds had given them £25 towards the debt of the school and had promised to give them, the last- £25, if they wiped out the deficiency speedily. (Applause.) He hoped it would be cleared before 1910. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Feeding of School Children.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, the Rev. T. R. Matthews presiding. There were also present :—Miss Broadbent,. Mrs. Simpson, Rev. M. Ryan, Dr. Murray, Messrs. G. Mather, A. J. Jolley. J. Billinge, W. Gill, R. Lee, W.,Fuiness, W. C. Brooks, W. Flannagan, J. Cook, W. Houghton, M. W. Seton, A. Brooks, P. Longton, A. Bottomley (clerk), R. W. Peacock (assistant clerk), and J. F. Awbery (master).</p>
        <p>ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE. The Boara approved the recommendation of the Assessment Cammittee that £3l ss. to be paid to Mr. J. C. Sutton for his services as clerk to the committee from 26th March to 24th June, 1908, and £125 to Mr. A. Bottomley for his services as clerk from 25th June 1908 to 25th March, 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>CONTRACTS.</head>
        <p>CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>The folio wing contracts were accepted for the ensuing half year. Workhouse: male officers' uniform, E. A, Jones, Warrington; coal and coke, Wigan Coal and Iron Co. ; clogs, W. Jones; boots, shoes and slippers, T. E. and H. -Porter, Liverpool; coffins, Albert E. Mason, Warrington; brooms and brushes, Geoffrey Crank; butte., lecon, cheese, lard and margarine, H. Milling, and Co. Ltd.•, tobacco and snuff, Sndlow and Co.; fish and ice, J. M. Porter, Ltd.; butcher's meat, H. Singleton; officers' joints, H. Singletrin ; flour and oat meal, Carter and Son; draper 7., E. A. Jones; wines and spirits, R. Rowson . and ~Sons; sdrugs, Burgoyne, Flurbidge and Co., London; dressings, &amp;c., Evan, Sons, Lescker and Webb, Liverpool; soda water, Harold Smith, Warrington • tailor's goods, T. J. Lee; leather Pocock Bros., London. Cottnge Homes: butter, bacon, cheese, lard, H. Milling and Co. Ltd.; brushes, &amp;c, G• Fern; coal and slack, R. Evans, Ltd., Haydock ; tailor's goods, T.. 7. Lee; bread, \V. P. Strange; groceries and necessaries, W. Owen and Sons; flour, Carter and Bon; meat, Thomas Baldwin; drapery, T. J. Lee; drugs Burgoyne, Burbidge and Co., London; leather, Thomas Samuel, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>THE FEEDING OF SCHOOL CHILDREN. It will be remembered that the Director of Educa- tion recently wrote enclosing a list of children who were alleged to he going to school under-fed and whose parents were in receipt of relief. _</p>
        <p>Father RYAN, .on behalf of the Warrington Relief Committee, said they had bad 22 parents betore • them, and 9 declared that their children were properly fed. When they were questioned very many said they some- times had to stint themselves in order that their children imight be fed. Five of the 22 did not appetir but the relieving officer reported that the children ought to be well fed and that they ought not to suffer in their educatron for want of food. Six declared they had not sufficient food to give their children and on the advice of the clerk the committee increased the out- door relief to an e.tent that they considered wonlil be sufficient to enaftle them to teed their children properly. Mr. A. BROOKS, onbehalf of the country relief com- mittee, said they had 10 cases before them, and in none did the parents allege that there was any stint of food. Certainly the parents had not stinted themselves, for they showed evidence of very goosl feeding. They were very respectable widows who could be fully trusted with the care of their children. The CLERK said that in a few cases the women were well able to do work but so long as they could get relief hew the Education Authority or the Guardians they were not prepared to do it. All the same the children were short of food, but if the mothers tried to do some- thing be dill not think there would be any reason for their being uhderfed. Some parents whose circum- stances were Poorer than others who declared that their children were under-fed, said they had no complaint whatever to make and that their children were well fed. In each instance the question was put " Are your children well 'fed ?" and in nearly all the cases it was admitted that they .did get sufficient to eat.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : Some of the cases were serious enough. but not as serious as some people would havt3 us think.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said that in all cases where the parents said their children were underfed, the Guardians increased the relief by a sufficient amount. He sug- gested that the reply to the Education authority be to the effect that in most cases the children at the present time were well fed and in those 'cases where the mothers complained that they could not feed their children owing to insufficient income the Gusrdians had incceased the relief to enable them to do so. 131LLINGE: Were all the people who came before the committee in receipt of relief? The CLERK: Yes.</p>
        <p>Mr. BILLINGE : The probabilities are that this is another way of receiving increased relief? Mr. MATHER That is it,exactly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>FLYING MATINEE. DOLLY REFORMING HERSELF."</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Macnaghten is to be congratulated, upon providing his patrons with such a high-class attraction as Henry Arthur Jones' comedy "Dolly Reforming Herself " which was presented at a flying matinee on Monday afternoon. The company, under the direction. of Jerrard Grant Allen and Geoffrey Besant, is touring the country in a motor saloon, and thus opportunity is afforded to dwellers in the provinces to witness one of the latest West End productions. Mr.Macnaghten's enterprise in booking the company for a visit to the Palace 'met with an encouraging measure of support, and so favourable was the impression created that we have 'little doubt that future matinees will be even more successful. The performance was adequate in ever Y respect; the characters were so well chosen that there was not a weak link in the chain, and the bright and. witty dialogue and briskness of action. which characterise the comedy afforded a delightful afternoon's entertainment. In " Dolly Reforming Herself " the " versatile author has given of his best, and the eagerness and facility v *th which New Year resolutions are made and brol -</p>
        <p>the pretty • domestic • entanglements and. El scenes which ensue caused laughter loud and long. Charming and irresistible Dolly has a weakness for running up huge bills to gratify her tastes in the matter of artistic millinery and the like. The long- suffering husband is driven to rebellion, apd quite the tit-bit of the play is witnessed when he rates Dolly in no mild terms for her extravagance and declines to pay the piper. There are tears and coaxing, temper and hysterics, but Dolly's blandishments conquer in the end, and all is peace. Miss Aimiee de Burgh as Dolly proved herself a true artiste, and acted with a freshness and unaffected charm that soon placed her on good terms with the audience. Mr. P. Clayton 'Greene did admirably as Harry Telfer;Dolly's husband, and this-talented pair certainly carried off the chief honours of the afternoon. Mr. Reginald Besant took the part of Matthew Barron Dolly's indulgent, good- humoured father, with real skill and Mr. Donald Calthrop Was a bright and persistent Captain Went- worth. • 2lr. Edward Sterling portrayed the fessilised Prof Sturgess to the life and Miss ,Gertrude Bibby as Mrs. Sturgess played a .difficult eictional part in brilliant style. The other characters were capably sustained and the production throughout was a credit to the company and to the Palace management. _ _</p>
        <p>This wee- entertainment at the- Palace and Hippodrome is of an excellent character, . and • anyone in need of a pleasant change from the hum-drum of every-day, affairs, may find it by a visit there. The tirincipal event of the evening, and one which claims the attention of all who are fond excitement—and of course, possibly others—is a re , -entation of the Johnstown Flood, Nothing in a s e production, except the inrushing of water, could better depict how that terrible catastrophe happened. On the morning of the disaster the sunithone out brightly in the East, fulfilling, as the inhabitants thought, a promise of a fine day. None dreamt of the awful storm and catastrophe that was approachtng. Suddenly "the whole atmosphere seemed •to change, and the rain descended in torrents and the lightning flashed. Never: was such a storm heard of before in the annals of our history, The reservoir, which, was not far away, and whose dimensions were abmit three miles long and LI miles wide was filled to The brim, and the 'pressure on the earthen walls was so great that they gave way, and the mass of water rushed upon the town brushing all obstacles aside, excepting the Roman Catholic Church and the Ironworks, and-carryingin its train a death roll of 2,500. Those who . were only injured were taken to the Church, but hardly had the torrent spent its force, when fire/broke out and ,adaed to-the unutter- able horrors of the situation. The models were so realistic that one's horror were fully roused. Ba-Be, or a picture postcard album, was as the pr gramme stated, a distinct artistic novelty. This set of Views was of the tableaux style, but appeared to be more realistic than is •usuat. It is rather peculiar, and yet not to be wondered at, that the fellow feeling of an audience always goes out to the homeless and the distressed. This was so, when a young lady was . posing as "Homeless," on the Thames Embankment, for the onlookers seemed at flrst - horrified and then, coming as it were to terra-firma, applauded the little girl as she lay there, the„picture of object misery. Menotti - was quite at home when he • was balancing himself upon a steel wire quite 15 feet.from the ground. He ,was - not, at all concerned, for he lit-a cigarette, "as cool as you ,please.". But when on a bicycle resting on .t wire, which was travelling at the 'rate of 40 miles an hour, he was . more con- cerned, for he realised this was the most difficult of , his feats. He gained deserved applause again and ,again. Um-ma-Morgan, refined American 'comedienne, dressed in a Territorial Costume proudly Claimed in a song that "no man shall be bolder" to the delight of the andience. Miss Elenriquette Maude, who is work- ingler way round the world alone made a distinct success when she told of her experience since she left New Zealand in 1904. Messrs. Morton and Elliott provided 'amusement and wonder in a varied turn. Mr. Percival caused great merriment by his caperings at the piano.. The orchestra played Very appropriate music, and' the biograph pictures were excellent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</p>
        <p>'Next week's bill is headed by Bellew and Stock's company of juvenile entertainers, The Eleven Romps, in their refined musical , scena. The other artistes will be :—Ted Karno and Co., in the screaming pantomime absurdity" Dinner hour" ; ,Maudie Forde, comedienne and dancer; Chas. Woodward, the popular typical vocalst ; The Cromwells, in their unique and novel juggling act; the' Czar Troupe of Russian Dancers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WIDOWERS' HOUSES.</head>
        <p>WIDOWERS' HOUSES.</p>
        <p>An_event of great interest will be the first visit to the Palace and Hippodrome of Miss Horniinan's Com- pany, under the direction of Mr. B. Iden , Payne, the Gaiety Theatre, Manchester. The visit will take the form of a flying matinee of Bernard Shaw's. play in there acts, " Widowers'. Houses," which Mr. Macnaughten has arranged- for Thursday afternoon. The Combination of attractions, that is the first visit of Miss Horniman's Company, a Flying Matinee and the performanqe of a play by Bernard Shaw who is admittedly one of the finest of living dramatists, might to result in a crowded house, and if • Warrington's -Previous record is to be considered that will undoubtedly be the case. To those Who intend going there is only one word of advice and that is to book early.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>"AT HOME"</p>
        <p>TO KINGS.</p>
        <p>• King Edward's interest in Mr. Wilbur Wright's aerial achievements is likely lc give the spur that is needed to the progress of aeronautics in Britain. Many regrets have been expressed that the inventors, sportsmen and financiers of this country have been so slow to profit by the lessons of the American genius, and it may not be long before we take the lead in aerial navigation, as we have done in so many other sciences and inventions. Whatever country ultimately becomes ruler of the air, it will never be forgotten that Wilbur Wright, like Marconi, Stephenson, and Watt, was the pioneer. Each of them met first the incredulity and ridicule that have broken smaller men, and each has triumphed by the most laborious and painstaking experiment. Our photograph sb.ews the Chateau at Pau where Mr. Wilbur Wright has been entertaining the King With an exhibition of his machine.</p>
