      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MR. WELLMAN IN HIS AIRSHIP.</head>
        <p>MR. WELLMAN IN HIS AIRSHIP.</p>
        <p>Undeterred by the tragic failure of Andree, who tried to reach the North Pole by balloon, Mr. Walter Wellman has for many months been making active preparations for his great attempt. He will start with the advantage of much improved apparatus, and of greater aeronautic knowledge than was possible for the covageous Frenchman. A most attractive opportunity of inspecting the Wellman airship is now being offered at Olympia, London, by the International Aero and Motor Boat Exhibition. The ship IS not so large as the German Zeppelin, but is more than double the size of the British Nufli Secundus. It can carry a ton and a half of provisions, sufficient to last the expedition eight months, as well as a boat, and dogs and sledges. From Danes Island, Spitzbergen, where the expedition will make its start, to the North Pole is seven hundred miles, and the adventurers hope to make the journey and return in three days.</p>
        <p>The Vlliitechapel Murder is one of the most sordid tragedies which have ever occurred even in this neighbourhood of ,evil reputation. A marine engineer named William Sprowell, or Sproull, is the victim. Nothing of any value wats found on the 'Jody, and the pockets had been turned inside out. The police followed a trail of blood- stains from the spot where the body was found, to a large house which is let out in tenements Bloodstained fingermarks were on the door, and in a room on the ground floor, two women and a man were found in an excited condition. Another man was 'aught by the police as he was endeavouring, furtively, to leave the premises. All four, Were detained. Deceased belonged to the steamship "Dorset. '</p>
        <p>FEEDING POULTRY.</p>
        <p>Many of the complaints from which fowls suffer, as well as poor 153 ing, era directly due to over-feeding, says " E.T.B. " in" Farm and Home," and yet it is an extremely dicult matter, particularly for the beginner, to know just how much be should give to his fowls. Unfortunately, there is no hard, and fast rule that can be laid down in this respect. Sometimes stated quantities are given, but it is very risky advice, for the requirements of fowls vary so enormously. Some experiments that were conducted in France some time ago proved that a Brahma required about 17oz. of food per day to keep it in good condition, whereas a Leghorn kept amid similar surroundings throve well upon 4ioz. of food daily. This thaws how much the breed basto do with it, apart from any other considerations. But there are other factors that determine the correct quantity that should be supplied to a fowl to maintain it in good condition.</p>
        <p>The Association of St. Petersburg Lawyers has decided that in the event of a member of the profession taking part in a game of chance in a club, d:sciplinary action shall be taken against him, and, if necessary, he shall be struck off the rolls.</p>
