      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>'1</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. I</p>
        <p>EARLY ROMAN CATHOLIC WORSHIP IN</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>We do not know whether the history of Roman Catholic Worship in Warrington has been carefully traced. Our own efforts have resulted in the discovery of a few fugitive references and one or two original papers, and in the absence of a complete history ,we are constrained to set forth the few facts which these papers contain. If any of our readers can help us to enlarge the narrative they will earn our thanks. Setting aside any discussion of pre- Reformation religion,Roman Catholic worship appears to have been established about the middle of the 18th century.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DAYS OF CONCEALMENT.</head>
        <p>DAYS OF CONCEALMENT.</p>
        <p>' The earliest place of meeting of which we have been able to find mention was an old room over the Feathers Inn, near Friars Gate. Most of the old Inns had open yards attached to them and spacious rooms for stOrage, and it appears to have been in one of the spare rooms approached from the yard that Mass was celebrated by visiting priests. The date we have not been able to ascertain but it may be approximately fixed at 1765, a period when memories of the Pretender were very fresh in the town. There is a memor- andum relating to this preaching room made by Peter Caldwell—an ancestor of the present family. He states that he recorded fiom the lips of Thomas Gee a description of the building next to the Feathers Inn. - A trap door was said to have existed through which the priests could escape when mir- suivants entered the house. The narrator described the-room an 4 its accessories, the secret celebration of Mass, the watchmen placed to give warning, and the worshippers who carried jug to convey the suggestion that they were going to the Inn for beer. The late Dr. Kendrick held conversations with Peter Caldwell and took notes of his -statements. As these had been received from Thomas Gee who had taken part in the services we think they may be accepted in proof of the existence of the Feathers Inn meeting. From the upper rooni in Bridge-street the congregation migrated to another upper room this time behind the Cooper's Arms in Bewsey-street. This extemporised chapel was situate in the loft or top storey of a build- ing occupied by a ropemaker. We have not been able to glean any paraculars of the events of these early years nor do we know how long the congregation remained in these temporary and not very convenient meeting places. The next removal would seem to indicate that the congregation was increasing in number -for a room of large proportions was hired in Dallam-lane. This large room had served as a meeting place of the Methodists and had echoed tb the voice of John Wesley on more than one occasion. But this only sheltered them for a short space—four years perhaps—and then for the first time the congregation entered a building in which they could feel quite at home. THE FIRST CHAPEL AND RESIDENT PRIEST. The first resident priest was the Rev. Barnaby BradshaW, who came„to Warring4on in 1766. The date is fixed by an inscription on a; picture in the possession of Mr. Peter Caldwell. It represents" Our Lord's agony in the garden" and bears on the back the inscription: "Brought from Lamspring College in Germany about the year 1766 by the Rev. B. Bradshaw, priest of Warrington." It was to the energy of this minister that</p>
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>the congregation owed its first home, the old chapel in Bewsey-street built in 1778 and now used as the Hall of /he League of the Cross. Father Bradshaw was a gentleman of unusual culture, of charming humour and broad charity. In the Rector of Warrington of that period the town had another cultured and kindly ecclesiastic, and between the two a very close friendship was formed. At a time when a deplorable intolerance prevailed this like-minded pair lived in close and happy intercourse. Only once did the theological spectre appear at the feast and Rector Owen offer remonstrance. The subject was Purgatory and the Rector refused to be taken there. "But after all my dear fellow" said Father Bradshaw, "don't you think you might go further and fare worse." Like the Bishop who • tried to curb the famous Father O'Flyn, the Rector had to laugh with, the rest, and the priest fluttered to a more genial 'topic. When Father Bradshaw died he was laid to rest within the walls of the Parish Church and the Rector read the ser- vice doubtless with much secret regret and heart ache at the loss of a revered and worthy friend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>ST. ALBAN'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. ALBAN'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>For 47 years the old chapel served its purpose, and in 1823 under the energetic supervision of Dr. Molyneux, St. 'Alban's Church was built. This earnest priest brought to his task . an indomitable energy, and inspired the congregation with his zeal. He is said to have , been a man of some personal . eccentricity, but like Goldsmith's , "Man of Ross" ", his failings leaqed to virtue's side." ,It was a large task which he outlined, but he showed them the way to its realization, and lived to see the reward of his courage. During the progress •of the building he doffed his coat and spent the mornings in mixing and carrying mortar to the bricklayers, and in many other ways showed his deep concern to provide for his congregation a worthy meeting place. The joy of the congregation in 'leaving the poor damp buildings for the spacious chapel of St. Alban is marked in 'the report's which have survived of the opening services, and of the elaborate Preparations which were made for the great event. There was :no Warrington newspaper to record the circumstances of the opening, but the " Catholic Miscellany" of November 1823 gave an interesting account. By the incredible exertions of their pastor the building has been completed in a space of time scarcely exceeding six Months, and now instead of ,an obvure dark and damp place of worship, the Catholics in Warrington have a most capacious and elegant chapel, which will be an ornament to the town, and it is hoped will serve ro raise a little the Catholics in .the estimation of their neighbours. .N 9 fewer than thirty-tour priests hail assembled to take part in the ceremony, a greater number than were ever assembled before in this country on such an occasion. On the Elevation of the 1-lost -instead of the- little .bell which is usually tinkled, a large bell outside tlk Chapel was tolled. This had a particularly grand eflect—all within being silence and adoration," - The collections made that day exceeded .4:,140, and of the sermon the writer says : "'We despair of Conveying to the' reader even a faint idea of its eloquence. ' _ _ _ -</p>
        <p>This dedicatory sermon was preached by Dr. Peter Augustus Baines, Bishop of the diocese, and was afterwards published. From the printed copy we infer that the publica- tion was due to Father Mollineux. It is entitled "The Advantage and Consolation of the Christian Religion, a sermon preached on the dedication of the Catholic Chapel, War- rington November the 13th 1823." A prepatory note says: "Mr. Mollineux begs</p>
        <p>leave to inform the reader that the following Sermon assuredly was not composed with the view of meeting the p'ublic eye. It was extorted from the amiable author by the influence of private friendship, with the hope of doing good in deference to the opinion of many who had the pleasure of hearing it, as well as of many who only heard it spoken of."</p>
        <p>With the erection of St. Alban's, Roman Catholicism became an accepted and visible part of the life of the town and the passing of the Catholic Emancipation Act six years later removed the last excuse for persecution. St. Alban's is eclipsed in beauty by the more spacious and modern Catholic Churches, but it remains a memorial to the zeal of its builder, and a connecting link between the Catholicism of the present and the "day of small things" that lay before it. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>r6\6R MISCELLANY.</head>
        <p>r6\6R MISCELLANY.</p>
        <p>In a pleasant and appreciative reference to our last week's "Warrington Chapter" a correspondent calls attention to the noble English in which Lord Strange's statement is' framed "Let my master be happy though I am miserable " etc. The passage is certainly a beautiful one and observes some rules of language which are now almost invariably disregarded. Our correspondent's request that we should compare it with the English of a modern military man—sent by telegraph or dictated to a clerk—is one that had perhaps better be disregarded.</p>
        <p>* * *-*</p>
        <p>Students of old manuscripts must often be conscious of the difference between the old and the new in the manner as much as the matter of correspondence. That letter writ- ing is a past art is beyond controversy, and there is room in the language for a dirge over its loss. Easy transit, cheap postage and the typewriter have made superfluous the long delightful pen-talks which our ancestors exchanged with profit and delight. Trite, they cultivated the grand style and their compliments savoured of unreality, but the effort to write well often gave a dignity and b6auty which is painfully absent from modern correspondence. .</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Take in illustration a brief letter of Jahn Newton, famous as the friend of Cowper and author of some of our finest hymns. It was penned while he rested his horse at Warring- ton and Newton vThttiti- have been greatly surprised had anyone suggested that it would be quoted a century and a half after. It was a brief note to his, wife, suCh as any letter writer would then have penned. But let the modern letter sbribbler study its diction and note how far our taste has degenerated in the use of our mother tongue. Warrington, December 13th; 1758.</p>
        <p>My Dear*st, , Thus far it is well,and I trust shall be to the end. The weather is fine, the roads good, the , horse free, and easy. He has not started once, though be sometimes raises his ears. -</p>
        <p>=Now and then 1 feel some twinges at being forced from you, though- but for a season, but the cause makes amends. Three or four woeks will, I hope, re-unite us ; and then, one hour will repay the pains of absence. Let us mot wish away the interval, but make the most of it, for it will soop be over. The new scene of life which appears tb be opening before us is very important. We have need to pray earnestly, constantly for each other, and for ourselves. Make much of the means of grace, reserve seasons for retirement. Endeavour to avoid the company by which yon cannot improve, and to improve by that which you cannot avoid. Adieu. May the peace of God here, prepare you for his glory hereafter. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Your loving husband,</p>
        <p>J4iTIN NEM TOY.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Sorri documents of Dr. John Aikin of Warrington, remembered as the friend of Howard and an able literary man and scholar, have just come to light in Manchester, and the Corporation of that city has done honour to the memory of the Warrington •doctor by purchasing them. One of the papers is an agreement for the preparation of Dr. Aikin's work "A description Of country around Manchester," one of the earliest and most important books of local topography. By the courtesy of the official custodian we have been able to copy the agreement. Memorandum of an Agreement hetween Dr. J. A ikin and John Stoadale. It is hereby mutually agreed between the above parties that Dr. Aikin shall compose a work to be entitled" A descrip,-- tiOn of the Country thirty miles round Manchester, ' or any other title which the two .parties may hereafter agree upon, for which he ito receive Two Hundred Pounds at different Instalments ; viz., Fifty Pounds to be paid on the YOth sheet being printed; snd Fifty Pounds' on the .fortieth shert being printed; Fifty Pounds on the Sixtieth, and the remaining Fifty when the Whole is printed. The work to • make one large volume quarto consisting of from 330 to 630 pages of a sized Letter , and - Page nearly similar to Hunter's Voyage, but if the book erther succeeds or falls short of that limit, the difference shall be accounted for at Two Guineas altd a half per sheet. Xs witness our hands this second day day of August, 1793. JOHN STOCKDAI.E. J. AIKIN.</p>
        <p>The book was produced at Eyre's press and duly issued in 1795. The scope of the work was enlarged to a radius of 40 miles around Manchester, and the large volume is a, monu- ment of labour and research. John Stockdale was a publisher of Piccadill3i, Manchester,and several of the M.S. appear to have come from his business papers, as there are receipts in Dr. Aikin's hand, for payment under the agreement. Dr. Aikin's Warrington house remains to us, though sadly disfigured, at the East corner of Orford-street and Butter- market-street. Here John Howard spent many evenings, while the Doctor gave literary form and finish to his great work on the "State of Prisons." -The imposing stone doorway of the old house has been preserved by Alderman Bennett,, and is to be shortly erected at his home at Paddington.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Our readers are not willing that we should leave the subject of the ancient word-forms preserved in the Lancashire dialect without giving some more examples. This, we will gladly do when space permits, and enquiries shall be answered when the subjects have general interest. For the present we Make a quotation illustrating persistence of the dialect of this district. It is from a deed preserved among the papers of the Blundell family and dated 1445, Most documents of that period were in Latin. This is in the vernacular. Selecting one sentence we read " If he dee bowte haye of hys bode geton i weddyd " etc. There are here four Words at least which are familiar in Lancashire: " dee " for die, " bowte " for without,- " geton- " for got or begotton, and " i " for in. And after five hundred years hard wear they still do duty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>HOW TO SAVE DOCTORS' FEES.</head>
        <p>HOW TO SAVE DOCTORS' FEES.</p>
        <p>Save doetOrs lees; cure yourself at a moderate</p>
        <p>cost. Munyon's Home Doctor Book will tell you how to treat yOurself at home with shilling remedies, to be obtained from any chemist. A. copy of this book can be obtained free (if the " Examiner " is mentioned) by anyone writing to Munyon, GI, New Oxford-street, London, W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>S MY LIFE STORY.</head>
        <p>S MY LIFE STORY.</p>
        <p>-• • ,1114*vk,- 10t4f.-: . . • , , • .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MR. ANDREW. RATHBONE.</head>
        <p>MR. ANDREW. RATHBONE.</p>
        <p>The story of my life is so interwoven with the progress of my native town, that I find the narration of the one without reference to the other incomplete. This must be my apology to your readers for any references that I shall find it necessary to make. I also find it very difficult to condense what I have to say within the limits of the space allotted to me, but " I will a round unvar- nished tale deliver." I was born in Latchford in the year 1851. My father was a file cutter at Messrs. P. Stubbs in Scotland-road. My earliest recol- lections date back to a time when my parents had removed to a house in Bewsey. At that time it was considered a very healthy district. Nearly all the houses there had a garden attached, and the one we lived in was bounded on three sides by fields and gardens. Roses and all kinds of flowering plants grew there in profusion. It was very open and ifleasant, but that had its drawbacks. I remember we had not been there long when a strong hurricane blew off a portion of our roof, stripped the lead flashing and a portion of the roof off St. Paul's Church, destroyed a large building which was in process of erection for what was then known as Bewsey Forge, now Pearson and Knowles' Ironworks, and did very serious damage to many places in the town. I also remember about that time a terrible hailstorm, some of the stones of which measured three-quarters of an inch diameter. In front of our house was a field in which a grey horSe was graz- ing, and I can see now in imagination that poor horse scampering hither and thither in the vain attempt to escape the punishment the elements were inflicting upon it. That storm did a terrible amount of damage in the country, and worked fearful havoc arbongst the hdt houses and green houses of the land. My earliest recollection of Warrington, is of a very much more primitive town than it is to-day, especially its sanitary conditions. Then, an unusually heavy fall of rain, or a sudden melting of snow, caused the floods to come down, and the rivei\ to overflow its banks, turning the meadow's into miniature seas. It is not so now, and the Ship Canal, however it may have failed to benefit the tpwn in otbar respects, has conferred untold benefits in this. The drainage of the town was in a very faulty condition. It was part of my duty as a boy, to take my father's meals to the works, and _I well remember on one occasion I was. taking his breakfast just after a heavy rainstorm, but found it impossible to get to the works in the ordinary way. Scotland- road was flooded nearly half its length, and I was carried to the shop gates,on the back of a man who waded knee. deep in the water. IThe paving, too, was done with the cobble or boulder stones of the district, and the Square granite setts or wood blocks were unknown to the town for that purpose then. The credit for ridding the town of these unhealthy and uncomfortable conditions is due to the late Alderman John Burgess more than to any other man, and the town owes him an everlasting debt of gratitude. ' My education was received at Heath Side National School of which at that time, Mr. John Brown was the headmaster. He was one of the best men I have ever known,full'of fine precept 'and lofty example. His teach- ing was religious and moral, as well as scholastic. The moral put might be summed up aS "Do the right thing, because it is the right thing to do." He was very severe against wrong doing, and used to make an example of any delinquents before the whole school. I vemember one period when there was a craze amongst the lads for making watch guards of horse hair. Many of them were really neat ,and Keay through the arrangement of colour. At that time Peter Walker and Sons carried on their principal business if not the whole of it at the King- street Brewery, for it was before Dallam- lane Brewery was built. Every morning there was a large number of drays drawn by horses of various colours. While these were waitin their turn to be served the lads would steal under the, dray and pluck a handful of hair out of the horse's tail. But this type of cruelty by the lads was strictly forbidden by the schoolmaster. The craze had laid as strong a hold on me as anyone else, and seeing a nice chestnut horse one .morning was too much for my moral calibre. I coveted some of the long chestnut hairs of its tail, and yielding to the -temptation, I seized the opportunity to steal. under the dray and to pluck some of them out. But I was caught by the drayman with my booty, and taking hold of my coat collar he hauled me up before The schoolmaster. The latter in turn, hauled me up before the whole school and gave me a severe punishment, It did me good, for I never repeated the (fence. The physical punishment I did not mind so much, but the example for wrong-doing I did. The sense of shame was very keenly felt, and that was what cured me.</p>
        <p>The first kind of employment I had wa while. I was at school., On Satirircl?:„-s sQI, the "Warrington Advertiser" which wa published by Mr. Geo. Powlson, at Marke Gate. FOr this work I was paid at the rat+ of 3d. in the shilling, and on one occasion th, person who paid me gave me too much which I didikt discover till I counted it ove after leaving the shop, but as soon as I dis- covered the mistake I took the money back, and the lady who had paid me was so pleased that she gave me 3d. for my honOsty,but the pleasure that it gave my parents was a much more ample reward to me. My next occupation was opening and closing Mr. Oakden's music shop. I was very fond of music, and was many a time enraptured by the performances of the shop lady on the piano. Up to this time I had continued my attendance at school, but I left. at the age of 12 years for my first regular place of employment, which was</p>
        <p>selling newspapers on the Old Bank Quay Station. From there I went to work at the Whitecross works at small wiredrawing, commonly known as jigger work. After remaining at this about two years, I was put under the late Mr. Thomas Birchall as an__ apprentice to thick wiredrawing. Soon after this Mr. Oakden began .his subscription con Certs for which the best talent of the day was secured. Being very fond of music and not being able to afford the price charged for admission to these concerts, I applied for and secured one of the positions for the sale, of programmes at each concert. By this means I was enabled to attend them all, and heard some of the best talent, both vocal and instru- mental, of the day. I went through my apprenticeship successfully, but before I part from this, let me say that never was a lad surrounded by better influences. The White- cross works had been founded by Mr. F. Monks, and though he may not have been cognisant of it, it will be some. satisfaction for him to learn now that his career has been watched in the spirit of emulation, not by one only, but by many who have moulded their lives on his pattern. They may not have attained to his high standard, but the good that he has done during part of his life will live after him. I wish also to testify to the worth of Mr. Thomas Birchall, whom I knew more intimately, and I shall always feel greatly indebted to him. Soon after I had served my apprenticeship, I went to work for Messrs. Smith and Houghton, Dallam Wire Works, and from there I went to Sheffield, where I worked in turn for three different firms. It was while there, that I fortunately married Eliza„the fourth daughter of Thomas Daintith, of Padgate, a fact of which I have every reason to be thankful. This was in the year 1873. Up to the time of getting married, I had not known since_ my apprenticeship what it was to want a day's work. From that time,however, work fell rapidly off' and it will be remembered by those who are old enough, that those times were very much like these. Up to 1873 trade had increased, as a prominent politician once said, "by leaps and bounds." There was a great inflation followed by as great a slump, which lasted several years, and after suffer- ing it for about two years I came back to Whitecross. After this, I -worked at the blocks about six years, when I was appointed sub-manager under Mr. Thomas Birchall, who was then works manager. I filled this position for about 12 years, when I received the appointment of general manager to Wm. Smith and Son, Dalian/ Wire Works, formerly Smith and Houghton. It was while there, that an amusing event took place. I had frequently to see-a gentle- man on business, and -When business was finished, we generally had a few minutes general conversation. The time I have in mind was about the yea l• 1895, when the question of fair trade was before the country. Without detailing all the pros and cons of the conversation, I will give the gist of it. He favoured the development of Canadian resources with English capital, and spoke of the advantages it would be to our sons. I combated some of his -contentions, and he then asked me what I would do if I had as many sons as he. "Oh ! " I replied, "I've more 'sons than you." " How do you know you have ? " said he. "I don't know" said I, "but I feel quite safe in asserting it." " Well," said he, " I'll bet you twopence you hav'nt." 'All right," I said,. "I'll take your bet." Thereupon, with the air of a man who was sure of his ground, he said, "Why man, I've got eight sons." "Why man,"l replied "I've got ten." At that he bt‘rst into a hearty laugh and said,"Here's threepence for you," and he afterwards intro- duced me as the man who won the threepence_ I remained with William Smith and Son for about 13 years, and towards the close of this time my health broke down so that I had to take a long holiday, and thoukh pro- gress has been slow, I am pleased to say it has been sure.</p>
        <p>During my connection with the Whitecross Company, the Firth Company was formed. Operations were commenced at Firth Place, from which it derives its name, but as the business grew, the accommodation there was found to be inadequate, and we moved to Friar's Green., This too, in process of time, and with the increase of business, was found to be too small, and more extensive and commodious .premises were sought. These were found at Florence Mills, which we still occupy. About two years ago, I was requested to take over the management of the works, and I find my time fully occupied with this and my own business of merchant.</p>
        <p>A. RAT HBONE,</p>
        <p>The Mount, Grappenball.</p>
        <p>,s Ls ;e Le</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Cairo street Sunday School Band of Hope.</head>
        <p>Cairo street Sunday School Band of Hope.</p>
        <p>The second annual concert was held in the above schoolroom on Mofiday. Miss Honghton was;' in the, chair and the undermentioned artists acquitted them- selves admirably , Master Bates, F. Williams and Miss Court opening selection ; Master :1. Bates violin solo, " Bohemian Girl " ; Master F. Williams' banjo solo, " The Park Crescent " ; Miss M. Isherwood irs her solos " The, Swallows " and" 'fit for. Tat." was much appreciated.. Miss Clatworthy Mandoline, in her solos. " The Grand March," and Lydia " proved. herself very efficient. Members .of Cairo-street Sun- day School in their Fairy Play, " A real Fairy," also earned well 'merited applause. The 'humorous part of the programme was well maintained by Master Richard Lawton.</p>
        <p>Edwin Braley, a mechanic, was committed at Grays Essex, on Saturday for trial for stealing 32Ibs. of tete.. phone wire. The u ire formed part of the War Mlles telephone line connected wrth Tilbury Port, and cutting it enditngered the coast defences.</p>
