      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>wis, lA-- ,11-itzt4.:l)=-IT,II tri ,-:--'-4 • , ' '''' NI – -i .'4 00,,,,;• ,11,--'''it,4-1.:41-;', ( --;73, .1.: : j i [ '\ \ .14 . • A 4 k ...'-- ~. "..4.:‘;•,.: .'. n ) , , ,-,co _ ,-,--- u – _,----_---- —... ..- '-ati.,17.-.0.-. •-i.f-;. 'v,...--,:-. -tot. • ....,1 kf-A,i= • --1. \,, ,„_, --.------_,,, t ,</head>
        <p>wis, lA-- ,11-itzt4.:l)=-IT,II tri ,-:--'-4 • , ' '''' NI - -i .'4 00,,,,;• ,11,--'''it,4-1.:41-;', ( --;73, .1.: : j i [ '\ \ .14 . • A 4 k ...'-- ~. "..4.:‘;•,.: .'. n ) , , ,-,co _ ,-,--- u - _,----_---- —... ..- '-ati.,17.-.0.-. •-i.f-;. 'v,...--,:-. -tot. • ....,1 kf-A,i= • --1. \,, ,„_, --.------_,,, t ,</p>
        <p>'I</p>
        <p>RINGWORM, HAIR-EATING SCABS</p>
        <p>AND FACE SORES.</p>
        <p>Four in One Family Cured by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>The wonderful germicidal and healing qualities of Zam-Buk have ;just freed four members of another family from loathsome skin-diseases of germ origin which the doctors failed to combat. This is the family of Mr. B. Grant,- an electric tram driver, of 409, Ley- street, Ilford, Essex, "We have three children," said Mr. Grant. " Our - second boy, aged four years old, commenced with a ringworm sore in the middle of the forehead. The doctor told us measles had left in the boy's flesh a .germ that fed on the r'oots of the hair. The eruption shifted to the sides of the head, and spread round, meeting at the back and going up to the crown, where it became most severe. The -hair all fell out at the roots and left a great loathsome mattery scab all over the parts. " The doctor ).minted the scabs with iodine and covered them with ointments, but the sores • only became .more 'loathsome and more widespread. At the end of four months we took the boy to the hospital. They told us the only hope' was to apply X -Rays, but we would have to bring the boy again a ,month later ' for that, " Having heard of the great fame of Zam-Buk for cases of this kind, began in the meantime to experi- ment with the balm ourselves. The first' application on the 'child's head cooled the 4ores and soothed the scalp so much that he began to sleep better. We had sufficient confidence td continue the use of Zam-Buk, which killed the germs, caused the scabs to fall off, and formed' a clear new skin In three weeks—a week before we were due at the hospitaL for the X-Rays treatment—the boy was quite cured, and his hair growing again. ."'Our: eldest child, Lawrence, five years old, and the baby, Lottie, twelve months old, also began with the sores. They were spreading over the boys head, and had covered the lower part of the • baby' face, the mouth and chin and down -t the neck. .We treated both these children with Zam-Buk, and again a wonder- ful clean cure was effected. - "The lower part of my face „and round under the chin became covered with barber's rash, making me a sickening sigh. Zam-Buk in a fortnight completely rid me of that unsightly skin disease."' . Zam-Buk is prepared only by' the Sole Proprietors at their private laboratories, and is sold for them by . all chemists, in closed is. 1 Id., 2s. Oil, and 4s. Gd. boxes, sealed up by the Government stamp. -Beware of cheap and useless ointments. Only with Zam-Buk (by reason of its. unique composition) is it possible. to effect Cures like the. above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>HOW TO ROOST POULTRY.</head>
        <p>HOW TO ROOST POULTRY.</p>
        <p>Perches are best made loose, And fitted into a slot, sass "Farm and Home." 'thus they are easily taken out and cleaned ; in this way the poultry-house itself can be quickly and more easily whitewashed, and the perches, too often the nesting-place of insects and the cause of scaly-leg, can Le thoroughly and efficiently cleansed. They Ehou Id not be too small or too large, but just of the right size for a bird to balance itself upon with ease, avoid on the top and flat below, to that it can rest its feet and claws without having to hold on underneath. Care should be taken that the wood is free from notches and quite smooth, a rough piece of wood often being the cause of bumble-root and scrofulous .sores.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MR. LOUIS HENSHALL.</head>
        <p>MR. LOUIS HENSHALL.</p>
        <p>SENTENCE OF FOUR YEARS PENAL</p>
        <p>SERVITUDE. -- Trial at the Assizes.</p>
        <p>The la 2-1. s,•ene in a painful ease, which widespread interest, was enacted at the I.:verpool .\ s-,izes on Saturday, before the Lord Chief Justice (Lord Alverstone), when Mr. Louis Henshall 111), director of H. A. Naylor, Ltd„ timber merchants, Howley, and one of Warrington's 'best known . com- mercial men, appeared in the dock to answer limner- ous charges of forgery and. theft. For upwards of a Month the prisoner had been in Walton Gaol awaiting his_ trial. By consent, ;._aturday had been specially set apart for the hearing and the large attendance of the public in the ,Crown Court in the noble St. George's Hall included a fair sprinkling of Warringtonians. A well-known Town Councillor-- • and other prominent men were there as spectators, whilst amongst those directly concerned as witnesses the Chief -Constable was conspicuous._ An early arr:ival. in the well of the</p>
        <p>_ Court wa's Mr. John Naylor who took hi S seat neat the solicitors. An added Interest centred in the case owing to the -fact that Mr. Rufus Isaacs, KC.; M.P had been retained as leading counsel for the defence This. brilliant advocate, of world-wide reputation the victor in many a notable • cause,</p>
        <p>was</p>
        <p>the cynosure 'of- all eyes on entering the Court. Tall and keen-eyed, with a , strikingly intellectual face, there is something command- ing about his very. presence. Mr. Isaacs had with him Mr. Rigby Swift, one of the best_ known barristers on the Northern Circuit, who . did such strenuous work on! the prisoner's behaltdurhig the preliminary investigations at Warrington. The prosecution had secured the services of an eminent gentleman in the person of Mr. A. •M. Langdon, and he was assisted by Mr. Gordon Hewart who distinguished himself by his clear and concise present- ment of the case before the justices. It should be ment4oned that Messrs. Tatham, Worthington and Co., of Alancliester, instructed counsel for the prosecution and Mr. T. S. Steel, of Warrington, had charge of the defence.</p>
        <p>A fanfare of trumpets heralded the arrival of the :Edges at the hall and an expectant bush fell upon the . _ _ .</p>
        <p>t!ou'it. Then the High Sh-eriff and his chaplait took their places on the Bench and lastly.came the Lord Chief Justice, a dignified figure in his scarlet and ermine robes, and everybody rose to pay their respects. Then the Clerk of . Assize (Sir Herbert Stephen) called the name " Louis Henshall " and the prisoner was brought up from -below and stood in the</p>
        <p>dock whilst some- of -the charges were read over to him: He was 'wearing the long, heavy- overcoat in which he appeared at the 'Warrington Police Court and be 'leaned f6rward and li4ened intently to the irtdictments.</p>
        <p>The charges enumerated in the calendar were : W Forgery of a request, purporting to be an invoice front J. Tandy to IL A. Naylor, for the payment of money, at Warrington, during the • month of ,October, 1906. (2) Forgery of a request, purporting to be an 'invoice from Joseph S. Garland to It. A. NrCylor, Ltd., for the payment of money, at Warrington, during the month of April, 1907, and also-forgery of a request., purporting to bean invoice, from William Stubbs to R.. A. Naylor, Ltd, for The payment of money, at Warrington, during the month of August, 1908. 3 (a) Fraudulently taking £lOO, the property of R. A. Naylor, Ltd., to his own use, at Warrington, on 4th November. 1908. (b). Fraudulently taking £lOO, the property of R. A. Naylor. Ltd., to his own use at Warrington, on 18th November, 1908. () Fraudulently taking £79 75., the property of. R. A. Naylor, Ltd., to his own use, at Warrington, on 12th November, 1909. (4) Stealing £54, the property of Robert Ander- tcn Naylor, at Warrington, on 15th December, 1903. (5) (a) Stealing £5OO, the property of Robert Anderton Naylor, at Warrington, on 20th February, 1904. . (b) Stealing £4OO, the property of Robert Anderton Na34lor, at Warrington, on 11th February 1905.</p>
        <p>We understand that other indictments had been, lodged, but prisoner's plea of guilty to four charges was accepted, and the proceedings lasted last less than half-an-hour. The acoustics of the Court are not of the best and to those in the' Press gallery much of Milt transpired was almost inaudible, counsel generally not raising their voices above a conversational tone. Prisoner was accommodated with a chair whilst the arguments on his behalf were put forward by Mr. -Isaacs.</p>
        <p>At the outset MLR. G. Webster, barrister, who lived At Warrington in his earlier days, untie an application. on behalf of the Warrington Corporation that the costs of the prosecution should be borne by the prisoner. He callei his lordship's attention to section 6 (sub- section 1) of the Costs in Criminal Cases Act, 1904, which came into force on Jranuary Ist, and provides " That the Cotut by or before which any prisoner is convicted of al indictable offence may, if they think fit, in addition to .ny other lawful punishment, order the person convicted to pay ths whole or any part of the costs incurred in or about the prosecution, and con- viction, including any proceedings before the examining justices, as taxed by the proper officer of the Court." - His Lorthhip : What have the Corporation to do with it ?</p>
        <p>Mr. Webster: They have to pay the costs in the first instance,</p>
        <p>His I.( rdship : Are they the prosecutors? Mr. Webster: No. they are the paymasters. His Lordship: Who are the prosecutors? Mr. Langdon : The executors of the late R. A. Naylor in one and 'the company in the other. His Lordship, having perused the 4et quoted by Mr. Webster: Where is there any power for me to order the costs of the Corporation? Mr. Webster: An authority is contained in the case of Rex v. Stoddatrt in regard to football coupons. His Lordship: I know I have power to order the costs of the prosecution, but I gather you represent the people. before whom the prosecution has teen con- ducted. You are the Warrington justices? Mr. Webster: No; I am. the Warrington Corpora: tion. They are the people who have to pay the money. Mr. Rufus Isalcs : I would suboSi', that the Corpora- tion of Warrington have no locus standi. - His Lordship: I cannot see that they have.. Their's may be the put se out of which some of the expenses may ,come,if no order is ruade,because they have general con- ductand control of criminal business, but I don't suppose that a county authority would ask me to give them the costs of bringing up policemen and witnesses. I would require more authority than this Act of Parliament before I could give costs to the Warrington Corporation-. .Mr. Webster: My learned friend Mr. Isaacs, who was in the House of dommons when the Act was passed, might be able to explain it. .(Laughter)-. Mr. Isaacs, however, did not accept the invitation. His Lordship: You will have the honour of appear- in g here at your ow n experqe, Mr. 'Waster. (Laughter'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>MR. TSAACS' PI4E:I FOR THE PRISONER.</head>
        <p>MR. TSAACS' PI4E:I FOR THE PRISONER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rufus Isaacs, in calm yet earnest tones, then addressed his lordship on behalf of the accused. He said he did not propose to speak at any length, because his lordship had seen the depositions and had, he was sure, noticed the letter of December 16th, 1908, written by the prisoner to Mr. Naylor, which contained practically all that could be said in favour of the prisoner. Prisoner had consented NI, an order being made that an account should be taken with regard to proceedings pending against him, both by the executors of Mr. Naylor and the company. That account world be in respect of moneys which it was sought to recover against him. He had consented to that account being ' taken as a Step in the restitution which the prisoner must make. That having been done as a Well, and his lordship's attention being called to the letter, he would only add that throughout a long period—about twenty years—the. prisoner had been associated with that busi- ness, and had certainly devoted the utmost of his energy and industry to the business. His Lordship: Are you speaking of Naylor or the company? ' Mr. Rufus Isaacs replied that it was both. It was Naylor until 1905, when it was converted it into a company. Something_ like twenty years ago he became associated with the business of Mr. Naylor, and eventu- ally became a I Erector of the business of Naylor Limited, a business in which he invested his own money, and in respect of which he served as director and manager. 'Your lordship has before you the espies- sions costained in the letter to Mr. Naylor," continued Mr. Isaacs, " and. I must content myself by saying f am satisfied, having placed those facts before you, to leave the matter to-your lordship, knowing well that you will, in considering the sentence, take. into account every- thing yon think may legitimately be said in favour of a reduction of the sentence." _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>His Lordship : Thera are some things I would like to know. As to emus of these charges there were unquestionably entries in the books which would tend to concealment. Their appropriated the amounts to other accounts which might pass in the books of the company unnoticed. Is there any explanation-of that? haacs : The documents were undoubtedly before the company. The Lord Chief Justice said that he could quite understand that the prisoner had worked bard for the company, and put his own money into it, I ut his lord- ship had to deal with particular transactions ; and, if Mr. Isaacs could say anything in excuse of these facts mentioned, he would I.ke to hear it. Particular trans- actions bad been to a certain extent concealed by entries made by the man himself. There were directions put in certain places which would have the effect, in the first instance at any rate, of concealing where the money had gone to. Mr. Isaacs : All I can say with regard to that, is that the business in regard to the accounts has been carried on somewhat irregularly. His Lordship : That does not give me exactly the loophole I should like to have, if I could get it. I have</p>
        <p>no explanation as to how the • money was used. Had he otl-wr businesses ? Mr. Isaacs : I don't•think that it would be sug- gested that be wasetti say way living an imnroral life or anything of that kind. • .</p>
        <p>Lord. Ship No that did lilt occur to me. What I meant was, was it extravagance in home expend:ture ? Mr. haws : I don't think it cpuld be said that he was eNt,avagant. He was putting the money into build- ing estates and other ente-prises of tint kind. I think th It is where the money has gote. . .</p>
        <p>Efis Lordship : FroiU wba time do you ,4uggest that these defalcation.; and misappropriations were taking</p>
        <p>place? . Mr. Langdon : The indictment starts with the sear 1901: The account t 3 Le taken will, of course, go further back. _ _ .</p>
        <p>His lordship: What is the total amount you suggest he has had of the company's money? Mr., Landon was understood to reply that. this amounts were £l,OOO of the company's and about £1,500 cif R. 4. 'Naylor's. - His Lordship Have you any view as to the pu:.- poses for which this money was used? Mr. Isaacs says be put it into property, which may be rash speculation. Have you any other suggestion? • Mr.Langdon : We have no information as to where the money went. Of coarse, the prisoner has had the money, and for all we know be has it still, but now that he has recognised his guilt in the way he has done and offered to make restitution, on behilf of the prosecution and the firm with,which the prisoner was employed, I would, leave the question of punishment- entirety hi your lord- ship's hinds, with the observation that risuner's old employers do not wish to press the case. THE SENTENCE.</p>
        <p>The prisoner was—ti;n—asked to stand, and the Clerk of Ass!ze formally told him that he had been found guilty on his own confession of certain offences, and asked if, licha(l anything to say why he should not be sentenced according to law. Prisoner made-no reply. ' • The Lord Chief Justice, in passing sentence, said : There are DO cases that give so much pain to his Majesty's judges as having to pass sentence on a man in your position—a man of ability and integrity so far as the world is concerned, able to conduct an important- business, and undoubtedly acquiring the confidence of those by whom you were employed. It is one of the lamentable things that we have to pass sentence on people in your position, who have been guilty of these serious frauds. In the interest s of the administration of justice,. it is quite impossible to allow tile fact that yon are well educated,or have madeyour way with credit, to- weigh, because those who have your advantages ought to be able to resist temptations which a poor starving criminal can scarcely be expected to resist. As I have had occasion to say more than once in these painful cases in this very court,it is impossible to administer justice if it is to be supposed that those who are well educated, and fortunate as far as this world's goods are concerned, are-not to be seriou9ly punished for serious offences. I. take into consideration what has been very properly urged on the part of the prosecution—that they do not desire to press the case. There is no evidence, before rue, and I do not assume that this money has been spent in gambling or in any improper means; but, on the other hand, I suppose it has been used for some kind of. investment or speculation in which you were personally engaged. But I have to look at the fact that a man trusted by his employers has for a period of some years been defrauding them, and I regret to say I have no doubt from tbe depositions that means were taken by you to Conceal the fact that you-had misappropriated the money in the hope possibly that, it might not be discovered. It is my painful duty to sentence you to</p>
        <p>penal servitude for foil r years. - Prisoner, who appeared to feel his position acutely-, was moved to tears dnring his lordship's impressive words. When the sentence was pronouneel be stood for a moment as if dazed, but when a warder touched him he quickly descended to the cells. Ills Lordship then expressed his appreciation of the way in which information-regarding prisoners had beea bought before the Court. As far as possible everything that could be said in favour of a prisoner was mentioned as-well as whatcould be said against him, and there was not tbe slightest ground for the suggestion some times made and he believed as a rule unfairly—that the police authorities attempted to suppress what would be to the advantage of the prisoner. An application to his Lordship that prisoner should. be allowed to see his solicitor .was readily accedel to and Mr. Steel afterwards'had half an hour's interview with him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>• " How foolish I was to neglect Anaemia." A London girl's bitter regret.</head>
        <p>• " How foolish I was to neglect Anaemia." A London girl's bitter regret.</p>
        <p>The lesson of her cure by Dr, Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>In spite of the fact that heavy eyes, pallid cheeks and actual suffering so readily betray the presence of Bloodlessness (Anannia), it is; indeed, strange that so many women and girls neglect the appeal of the body for new, good blood. One young lady in Loudon, Miss Lilian Codling, who assists her brother in his business, the" Avondale" Confectionery Stores, at 30- Newington Butts, S.E., in conversation with a - Press representative, recently, bitterly lamented having been thus negligent. - " How foolish I was to neglect Anannia I know now only those who have suffered as I have, cm approciab this," she said,</p>
        <p>Miss LIE</p>
        <p>Lmil I was ,about fifteen I looked the picture of perfect health. But later My . face become pale, and I suffered with palpitations" and breathlessness ; if ever I-hurried,. or. became excited the attacks came on and my existence was wearisome. "I was treated by several docters, but my state became most distressing. My body. ached ; my joints were fearfully painful ; my ankles swelled and I had a horrid weakness across my back. 1 • grew terribly nervous and irritable. My eyes were sunken and I suffered incessantly from headache.. , " At night I. used to crawl into bed quite exhausted, yet what sleep I obtained did not rest me, and I was always tired out. " :‘,-4 for food, I tried to avoid eating, for fearful indigestion pains would seize me across the chest - soon after eatibg food. " About this time a married sister who lid suffered badly- with Rheumatism was completely cured by. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and she recommended me to try the same remedy. " After I had taken about six boxes of these Pills- my heart, which hitherto had felt se over-worked, did not trouble me at all, and I could breathe freely. As I persevered, the pills built up. my strength, and in time I was restored to health. I enjoyed sound and refreshing sleep at nights, and could take my meals quite regularly without any unpleasant after-effects- My foods nourished me and I quickly regained my lost weight. "What plehsed me most was the cheerfulness that came to me, and the ease I enjoyed from headaches and backaches. I had never felt so well in all my life; my complexion had become soft and smooth, and. every trace of Anaemia was banished." Dr. Williams' Pink Pills enrich the Blood and give tone to the Nerves. Thus they have cured Ancerniar, Influenza's After-Effects, Indigestion, Neuralgia, Spinal Weakness, Rheumatism, Sciatica, St. Vitus' Dance, • Paralysis, and Eczema. The genuine pills bear the full name on each package, in seven words: Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Of dealers,- or direct from the Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, post free, 2a Vd. for one box, or I 3s. t.M. for six.</p>
