        <p>Culchethi</p>
        <p>S tzts -ATtLET DINER EPIDEMIC.—Owing to several fresh da es having broken, out during the past fornight the / schools have been closed until Monday the 29th inst.</p>
        <p>-uDING AT CUICHETIL—The , wedding was 4—°lllllized at the Newchurch Parish Church on Satur- Zlast of 'Mr. John Johnson, of Newchurch and. Al rs. bn Ilankera, of St. Helens. The Rev. E. W. Whitten, Va-1? Kaye being the officiating ministe-. The bride to;',4,ttited in a dress of brown material with hat to s,:sh'en, and was attended by Miss Henshall, of Kenyon, 1)-il1desataid. Mr. Herbert Johnson brother of the wte;dlFrootu acted as hest man. After the cereniony the 14;,,mink breakfast *was partaken of at the home of the as'megroorn where numerous friends and relatives sentbled to tender their wishes to the happy couple.</p>
        <p>Risley. miss, con ON SERVICES.—A week's mission services were uded at the Independent Methodist Chapel, on tta • el a,' mt. Theservices were well attended, although one Weather was very inclement, snow and sleet falling c'ne or two evenings. But those who braved the dirlerits were well repaid lsiy listening to the inspiring onec°arnes given by Mr. F. E. Cook, who pointed out in obviaf his sermons it was not solely the great and spoil.'" sins such as drunkenness, gambling, etc., that th t men and women's lives, hut the apparently small ‘,Tiags• The insidious evils of envy and jealousy were wet: small foxes who spoilt the vine." Solos and duets Inuch rendered by members of the chpy and were road. appreciated. A very good impression has been Bgr,' and much good been done in the church and ounding district.</p>
        <p>Moore. • SOCr„ r IMPROVEMENT SOC I ETY.—Tp connection la_ this Society, on Wednesday evening there was a eciaz lecture, more especially for the children, but , 9 interesting to the adults who were present. recturi-r was Mr. O'Callaghan, who lectured,for the Anna' Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to "s'ais, the subject of his address being "Animal life," the- very interesting and sometimes amusing manner i lecturer taught his hearers the gre‘t necessity of winslness to all forms of animal and bird life, if .we bs • bed to make friends of them. He also illustrated by 4aEse s of his very good slides how theanimals possessed 441 remarkable degree the eleThents of commonsense jer,, their love and sympathy for their own kind. The e "urer's aim REIS to teach his audience that it was the erre".t,or's intention that all the forms of life in His aeccni should live happily and peacefully together, rieti.'ull'state can only be arrived at by continued kind- ere find gentlenef,s on our part to all our fellow atnres both animal and human. A very hearty vote thaelr's to Mr. O'Callaghan was proposed by Miss uttou and seconded by Mr. Lawton.</p>
      </div>
