        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>BTOCRT° s HEATH CHORAL UNION.—This young ittgnisation is making rapid progress, which was t.,;Tc-gly evident at the first yearly concert, which ;';'` Place last Thursday evening. The orchestra igha marked success in " Morning, Noon and (Salve), " Clock Symphony" (Haydn) and larionetten." (nndaered very The Stockton Heath Choral Unit') esc )(R(l).hurn), effectively "How sweet the calm " Sweet and Los " (Barnby)," 0 Happy -Lylasso!. ~ "Hark the echo falling" (Orlando di Meade't " "Jernory " (Smart) and "1 hanks be to God" taste ssohn). They sag sAith precision and good Su Which evidenced painstaking practice. were ngNlgyht " (Thomas), apd "Idle Words " PPlanded, miss Miss Chalmers, and she was, loudly renderedlthcl Vair and IMr. tastpt tilly " The Keys of Heaven," Mr. Harry Berrey ,ais.heard to ad Vantage in his inteiPretation of " The tors Gray_ eetly sang " (Sailiran), Miss Gertrude Povey " Violets " (Wright), and " The song my 14,?Ither (113°. (Gr• *haw) Mr. H. Berrey and Mr. I.ne rende7ed " The Rivals'" to the enjoyment of their Znarels• Misses Dutton, PP'vey, Grounds, Armstrong, Messrs. I3errey, Wood, Noden, Plumpton and °Lunds took rart in the madrigal " Love is meant. to (117:1,us glad " (Merrie England), whieh went with a svvin., Several ladies of the choir joined i:Y sweetly 411 Come away elves." Mim Ethel Parr inerterpreted " When the tide come," in a Nay which did e racredit. Messrs. E. Noden and B. Plumpton caused (et' h laughter when they sang "Two .proper men " Pn"Than)• The quartette "Poor old man "I by Misses Parr, Messrs. BeiTey and Milne created much an. tent. Mr. Bamford 's cello solo was very much talieV„,.cd• IVluch praise is dnA .to Mr. Berrey, the u'ei conductor of the choir, who was largely Pensible for the concert.</p>
        <p>Penketh and Sankey. .114ANTRRIc LECTURE.—An interesting lecture) 'on Sch°l-",o'' was given on Tuesday night in, the rt W, O 1 by Mr. George Thompson (of Liverpool). Mr. teh-l• 13rown presided over a good attendance of 'tb ("ars and ladies and gentlemen from the village. Mr. int:taPson in the courae of his lectufe gave .tome very reijesting accounts of the ancient city, its churches, I,rells and customs which were greatly enjoyed by all !e, at- The subject was well illustrated by limelight INteB"..a. A vote of thanks to the lecturer,- proposed i ) ‘er we , send supported by Miss Davenpoit,b ro ugbt late,as,ant meeting to a close. The lentera was MAU. Mr. Robinson. LP m eett—RE Ry MRS. PARKER.—At a well attended qtilf.,l"g of the-Literary Section of the Peeketh Wesley tie1i,,"1,20 Wednesday 'evening, Mrs. Parker gave.. a ti,llll little lecture on Mendshsohn, commenting e the e tendency of some modern critics to depyt-cat kr, 1,„r:,%t ruuscc4in's work on account of its simple, fe a f,'""P,torward nature. She thought however that the the e;e"ts being easily understood by the majority and toratercms emotions it . aroused, were intim ely pre- korai? to the simulated enthusiasm for the obscure and NueierY empty' music of-certain later composers.— r tla •n°"B...t.nusical illustrations were given, Mrs. rffße Y 1 .ftttur o m " 'from the " Midsummer Night's kries;ll;" the " Funeral March," and the" Spring Song." h Will' • Marjorie Dodds Miss Parker, Mr. and Mrs. _ , erb ' o aW, Mr. R. E. Garnett of Liverpool? M r. seier4„e,rt Gu . est and Mr. Blytb assisted in Mn ‘l°lis from "Elijah" And "St. Paul. 'he f"YA-NSUiDy SCHOOL.—Under the auspices of %teri",°g Leagues Union, a grand cinernatograph W 55 - given on Friday, in aid ot the waoonai t Children's Home and Orphanage. The chair °Pesrnhen by at '7.30 by Mrs. Dodds. Tne concert was stiesz?-. with a most suitable hymm " Scatter seeds .of ce.„..fit,tnriesys H which was thrown on the sheet and was 1:11 ~.sang. Miss Bell followed with a recitation the 6t, irernan's Wedding" which was illustrated on E'arta:-'oet.Lantern pictures which were shown by Mr.VV. iiietclascn, of Warrington, and were both humorous and hattructive. A shirt address was de!ivered by Mrs. tilt)..a of Widnes. Mrs Dodds.alse spoke and asked Ctliil re Present to ' help An illustrated sons " The 'attnn-c,l'a Home " was rendered in a Most effective Waitrr 1337 Miss Johnson. Miss Bell recit, , d The triPg. li6t " a pleasing manner. The hyrnm "rhere's had lid for little children" was thrown oft the sheet, clo,Ver this had been sung, the proceedings were u with the Benediction. 0 . PARISEI COUNCIL MEETING, l'aldn:AknaaY evening a Parish Council meeting was Dreaid the New School, Stock's-lane. Dr. Alurray,J.P., kess"`' and the following Councillors were present : tv a. Witbinshaw, R. Rontledge, J. Ditchfield, clerkl.adward, Miss Tinsley and Mr. E. Foster ( parish reitted 911 the minutes of the last meeting being sub- tier ' utsotission arose as to the road widening at the tiloentirl:l : tfbeGy illrwoxstone-road. It was resolved that the • !should write •to the trustees (Messrs. Nall's), C give the land required for such all era - Chairman pointed out that the District ;s were .not in a position to buy laud. tricokituollowing letter was read from Superintendent Widnes :—Sir, I be to acknowledge the (c)frYs)" rw letter of the 19th inst, and to inform ~ fm,)`,.!at a the matter ref erred to as regards speed limits "l es receive attention. ree,te'7titteßr .Nivas read from the London and North: ceiPt • waY Company as follows : Sir,— i am n 'lug ettp,, 0.. Your letter of 19th inst., but. much regret ot see the vi'ziy to recommend the expense in light- oad to the station with gas instead of oil. . Of</p>
        <p>course on the days you mention, the weather was exceptional and gas lamps blew out as well as oil.—W. TuRNBULL, Superintendent. It was resolved that the matter he left in the hands of those who had. cont acts with the company to see if they can influence them to put gas in place of the oil lamps. It was usolved that" the next Parish Council meeting he held on the sth of April at 7.30- and the parish meeting at eight o'clock. •</p>
