      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 20, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 20, 1909.</p>
        <p>by Mr. Williams, it was deciod to elect a new com- mittee who would, if sullicieut financial support was discovered after canvas,iug the district. proceed with the arrangements for holding the festival this year, otherwise the scheme would have-to be.dropped. Mr. J. D. B Smith was elected chairman and Mr. Fray vice-chairman, .with Messrs. F. W. Ashton' and I-13rry Saville as joint hon. secretaries. A vote of hanks to the chairman concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>PADG ATE DISTRWT N usi.—Nurs Kane - who for severSil years haS.,heen the District nurse for Padgale, is leaving to take no doilies in Douglas, Isle of Mau.</p>
        <p>DEATFI OF MR. G. LITTON.—WO regret to announce the death which took place on Tuesday at his residence, The Hot lies, Fearnhead, of Mr. George Litton, who vas well known in the corn trade in Warr ineton and the district. He had been an invalid for s,me years. Mr. Litton was 72 years of age and a son of the late Mr., Thomas Greenall Litton, of Sankey Hall. The funeral takes place to-day at Christ Church, l'adgate. JUMBLE SALE.—A jumble sale.in aid of the Wesleyan Sunday school building fund was held in the school- loom on Friday last, the 12111 lust, The sale was opened at 7.30 by the Rev. Thom.s Cladding.. A large assortment of articles were offered for sale on the various stalls, and the stallholders and friends were amply rewarded for their efforts by the excellent financial success of the sale. The total proceeds realized over £9. The stallholders were as follows : Dapery, Millinery &amp;c. Stall : Miss Richardson, miss E. Mason, Miss N. Bennett, Mrs. Newton, Mrs. Wright and Mrs.Plinston. Crockery Stall : Mrs. Duckwort h and Mrs. nu, ton. Refreshment Stall : Mrs. Dr. Bennett, Miss Andrews. Miss Eaglesfleld, Miss &amp;Deli, Miss May Gandy. Men's Clothing Stall: Mrs. Tunstall and Mm. E. Andrews.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—Tbe monthly devotional" meeting of the Guild was held . On Monday evening, Mr. Duckworth occupying the chair. Mr. Gipsy Evans or the Liverpool Mission was expectSd to address the meeting on the subject " Contrasts in Conversion "but on ing to an attack of influenza the well-known missioner was unable to attend, but an excellent substitute was' found in the Rev. Arthur Phillips. In a well delivered speech he gave a short account of the work done by Mr. and Mrs. Gipsy Evans in the Liverpool Mission. He spoke of the many curious, types of people with whom they came in contact. but said that when once they realized fully what Christianity meant, religion became a very real thing with them. One of the means they had of attracting the people was the holding of conceits every Saturday evening. These concerts were very popular anthproved the means of keeping many of the young folks off the streets and out of the pub! ic- houses. He also spoke of the importance of open-air work which was carried on with great zeal by Mr. Evans and friends, and said that the needs of the poor were most aPpalling, though owing to a certain amount of pride it was very difficult sometimes to get them to disclose their extreme poverty. He said they opened soup kitchens during the winter months which had proved a great blessing to many and iurged all to assist them as far as possible in the importantwork in which they were engaged. Mrs. Gipsy Evans Wen spoke a few words in which she said that drink was the curse of everything in the slums and she would like to see all the distilleries burnt to the gronnd. (Applause). Solos were sung by Mrs. Gipsy Evans and votes of thanks were proposed by Mr. Wright, sem.nded by Mr. Newton and supported by the Rev. W. Mellor,the late pastor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Lymm</head>
        <p>Lymm</p>
        <p>LyMM WOMEN'S LI3IERAL ASSOCIATION.—Wbat ouaht to prove a valuable adjunct to Liberalism in the &amp;strict came into being on Thursday afternoon atiaa meeting' of ladies interested in the cause, and held \t, Hirst's pavilion. The meeting. had been palled by Mrs. W. J. Crossley, who addressed those present on the aims and objects of a Women's Liberal Association. As a result of the meeting a committee was formed with Mrs. Crossiey as president, Mrs. R. H. Gotobed, hon. secretary, end Mrs. John Brooks, hon. treasurer. LYMM DISTRICT COUNCIL—There is to be only ono contest this year. This is in the Booth's Hill Ward where Mr. F. 0. Moston, the retiring Councillor, is being opposed by Mr. C. E. Cowper. The election takes place on the 3rd April. In the Heatley ward Mr. T. H. Warburton is retftrned unopposed and in the Lymm ward Mr. R. H. Gotobed, who was proposed by Mr. E. 11. Smith of Lyman Hall, and Mr. S. W. Goulo, of Foxley Hall, is also returned unopposed. Mr. Gotobed is essentially a man of the district, taking a keen interest in all that pertains to The good of the place and the people, and is therefore in the right place as a councillor. He is a fluent speaker with a good voice, chairman of the Liberal Association, member of the Sub-administrative Education Committee for Lyman, and also finds time for various other useful public Vices. lHe is broad-minded enough to he strongly Ibpposed to politics being used in any way in connection with Council work. Mr. Goto bed is also a Free Mason, taking a great .interest in the craft and especially in the Earl of Chester Lodge under Whose banner he is enrolled.</p>
        <p>LYMM CHORAL SOCIETY.—The annual _ business meeting of members of this societY was held on Tuesday evening in the District Council Offices. Mr. E. Taylor presided.—Mr. T. M. Eastham, the honorary conductor, reviewed the work of the session and spoke of the steady progress the society had made both in numbers and in efficiency, since its inauguration and the hon. secretary, Mr. F. Champ gave a brief report referring to the very successful social held at the beginning of the session and the equally successful concert held last month. The • following officers for the ensuing year were elected :—Hon. conductor' Mr. T. M. Eastharn ; chairman of committee, Mr. B. Taylor; hon. treasurer, Mr. F. Champ; hon. secretary, Mr. F.P. Brown. During the evening, Mr. James D. B. Smith, on behalf of the society, presented .to Mr. Frank Brown (who for the past three years has acted as co-secretary with Mr. F. Champ) a very handson-se clock,, as a token of their appreciation and regard, on the occasion of his forth- coming man iage.—Mr. Brown acknowledged Hie gift in a few well-chosen and sincere words of thanks. CON G S EGATIONAL CHURCH BAZAAR.—This bazaar am brought to a close on Saturday evening last and it has proved a very successful effort, a sum of &lt;£2os Leing realised. as the result. The bazaar was opened on Saturday by Mr. Alfred Arnold, who in a few well choir words urged the niembers of the church and congregation not to be &amp;intent with this special effort but to make a sustained effort throughout the year, not only as regards financial natters but in the true work of the church. Mr. Ernest Mumford, a former deacon of the church, occupied the chair, and in introducing Mr. Arnold, dwelt upon the debt which congregarionalists 'throughout Manchester and district owe to Mr. Arnold. Mr. Mumford also expressed the great pleasure he felt at once more being amongst those with whom he had worked so _cordially in time past.— On the motion of Mr. J. E. Ball, treasurer of the Bazaar Fund, seconded by J. Hardy Holt, a very cordial vote of thanks was extended to Mr. -Arnold and Mr. Mumford. The entertainments held on each nieht of the b azaar were decidedly successful and added con- siderably to the enjoyment of visitors and to the revenue of the bazaar. The following ladies and gentle- men very kindly gave their services in this work : Thursday, Mrs. F. P. 'Cooke, the Misses Pattison, and -Miss Ouston, who gave two short plays entitled "Our at 'o n.e " and " Lady Hatightington " ; Friday, Misses F. L. ,Brearley, C. Boond, L. Evans, M. Hesktth, E. Hesketh, Messrs. Halliday,Hesketh and Deeks, and took the form of a musical entertainment. Saturday's pro- gramme .was provided by the elder scholars who gave a short farce entitled "'[he White Hat,".in which took part: Misses L.Eaton,E.Turner, E. Moores,and Messrs. J. Webb, J. Rose and H. Radcliffe. Miss Mary Brand reLt very Rindly rendered 's,3veral solos" on Friday and Saturday evening in the bazaar room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LYAINI PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LYAINI PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY :—Before Mr. A. Watkins (chairman) Major Groves, the Hon. J. E. Cioss, Messrs. J. Pov.vis Dewhurst, and F. 0. Moston. ALLE GED THEFT.—Catherine Hinton,spinster, resid- ing at Rock House, Lymm, was charged with stealing a quantity of clothing, A silver watch and a small clock the property of Helen Taylor; Fletchers Lane Lymrn. Defendant pleaded not guilty. It appears tint the defendant uent to live with c9tnplalnant for 6 days and when she left, the clothes were missed. Sergeant Wash- ington sBid he bad searched defendant's house and could not find any of the goods.—Defendant said she had never seen any of the articles, and knew nothing what- ever of them.—The bench dismissed the case.</p>
        <p>ROAD OBSTRUCTION.—John Davies, KnutSford; wss summoned or allowing his horse and cart to remain unattended on the High Legh-roau on March 4th. He was fined 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—James Flinn, Barford-lane, was fined 2s. 6d. for using obscene language on February 20th.</p>
        <p>TIIE MAY QUEEN FESTIVAL, A STEP FORWARD.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the adjourned public meeting held in Hirst's pavilion on Tuesday eight to receive the report of the sub-committee appointed at the previous meeting to interview the Tradesmen's Association as to the question of ways and means. Mr. Harry Fray was voted to the chair and after explaining the business to- be done asked Mr. 'Cooke, - in the absence of Mr. J. D. B. Smith, to give his report .of the meeting with the Lymm Tradesmen's Associa- tion the previous evening. Mr. Cooke stated that as instructed, the deputatil pointed out to the Assbciation that the May Qneen Committeeidid not see their way to itqcppt the financial, responsibility involved in running the Festival this year without some definite promise of support either in the way of guarantors, or Subscribers. The deputation then withdrew to enable the Tradesmen's Associa- tion to discuss the question., On being invited again into the room the Chairman informed theta that the AFsociati.on had passed a rcsolti- tiOn to the effect that as, a i.ody they curd(' inJi; guarantee any financial support. Considerable discussion and criticism of the management of the•finances ry the May Queen Committee then ensiled whilst at the same. time surne,measure Of.encouragetnent of financial assist- ants was held ittitty various Winters of the assoeia, There was a loot; discussion •in which Mess s. Smith, Savil II isms and (Abel', took part and eventually, on the proposition of Mr. Smith, seconded</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Stretton and Antrobus.</head>
        <p>Stretton and Antrobus.</p>
        <p>Our readers will learn with regret of the death, which Look place last Friday, at The Grange, Autriibus, of Mrs. Ellen Pirnhitt, aged 61 years, Widow of Mr. John Pirulott.. The deceased was highly restuct,ed. having iteen a resident in the neighbou• hood all her life. She took a deep interest, in the Pi Uprose League (Whitley Habitation) served first as a warden, and at the time of her death war; on the Rxecut xe Council. She was much in'erested in the work at Whitley Parish Church. She leaves four sons and six daughters. The funeral took place at St. Matthew's Church, Stret ton, on MondaY af-erhoon. The official.- ing clergyman was the Hon. and Vey. C. F. Cross. The chief miinirners were Mr. A. Pi mliitt, Mr. W. F,Pimbitt. Mr. R. G. Pirulott (sons), Mr. William Fletcher (biother-in-law), Mr. F. Big2s. Mr. J. Knowles. Mr. Percy Wright, (sons-in-law). There was a large attend- ance or friends of the der eased lad 7. Wreaths were sent, by Mr. and Mrs. A. Piniluit, aid Mr. William Pimloit. Miss Nellie Pinilott. Mrs. Garner; Mr. arid Mrs. John Knowles, Messrs. R. G. and H. C. Pitnlott. Mr. and Mrs. Piank Bigt,s. Mr.-and Mrs. P. Wright, Master Geoffr.--y Garner, Mr. and M !let cher, • Mr. Thomas Halland family, Mr. and Mrs. Alathe's. Mr. and Mrs. Baxter and family, Miss Janet' Jones, Mr. and Mrs. Dean and family, Miss Lucy Fair- hurst, the Primrose League, Whitley Habitation ; Mr. and M rs:, Bnrnage, Mr. G. 31 ourifleld, -St mei on ; Mrs. Hewitt and family, Stockton Heath; Mrs. Knowles and family, and the Clovely nursing staff. Messrs. J. -Al: Gieenhalgh and Company, of Warrington, carried oat the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>MR. J. A. SEDDON, M.P., AND FARMERS'</p>
        <p>GRIEVANCES,</p>
        <p>Speakidg at the annual Ilinner or the 'Liverpool and District Farmers' Association on Monday, Mr. Seddon. M.P., frankly confessed that Parliament had neglected the tamers, hut to some extent. the farmers themselves were responsible : (Hear, hear.) Every member of his own party was willing to stand up and defend the just claims or the farmer, because they knew very well that upon the agricultural prosperity of this country 'depended to a great extent the prosperity of the industrial community. On the Agricultural Rating Act, he said, the farmers would have to make out a case at once if it was.to be, continued. His•presence was an indication of his own willingness to learn their .grievances and give them careful ccnsideration. (Applause:)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS SOCIALISTS.</p>
        <p>The concluding Sunday demonstration of the season was held on Sunday afternoon by the St. Helens Socialist Society at the Hippodrome. Mr. J. H. Standring, prpsided, and spoke .to a resolution affirming continued adherence to the principles of Socialism, and an address was also given by Mrs. Bridges Adams, of London, who spoke at length on the ditty of the State to the children. vlr. T. Russell L:thottr candidate for Spelt Valley also spoke on the Socialistic propaganda, and the resolution was unanimously carried. GIRL'S FATAL BURNS.</p>
        <p>Alice Unsworth, aged seven, .if 8, Booth-street, 'Matto Heath, died in the St. Helens Hospital on Saturday from extensive burns received under distress- ing circumstances. On Friday morning the girl was r o ed by her mother and went downstairs to chess in front of the fire. The mother,was attending to the baby wheh she heard a scream. She ran downslairk with, the child in her arms, and found Alice partly dressed with her clothing on fire. She attempt, d to extinguish the flames, hut the child made for the street door, iind on following her the mother fainted. Airs. Jones, the next door neighbour, ran to assist, and every- thing possible was done for the child. but from the out- set there as very little hope of recover).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above council was held at the Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. G. Pilling presided and.there were also present:— Messrg. J. Spruce, T. Wright, S. Woodward, C. L. Linaker, P. Collins, W. N. Morris, • A. Haining, C. Roylance, J. Hallows. G. Egerton-Warburton, H. Mawdsley' T. Guest, Turner, T. Antwiss, T. Wood, .1. Stokes, J.ll. Dairies J. L. Jones. C. Horton, G, Watson R. Nixon, J. Renshaw, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer of health), J. Swinton Lind W. Diggle (surveyors), and J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ESTIMATES.</head>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>The clerk reported the estimates for the half-year, ending 30th September next. General expenses (public health) were set down at £1,313; highway expenses £2,637 ; balance and contingency,. £2,462; total expenditure £6,612. Deducting the probable balance on March 31st of £3,122, left the total amount required from the oArseers at £3,490. The a4essable Nal ue of the district is £181,534, a rate of 40. in the .£, would produce £3,404. It was recommended that a call for £3,490 be made on the overseers payable on May 7th, and August 7th. The estimate was adopted. MEASLES EPIDEMIC. -</p>
        <p>Dr % Adams reported severitl cases of infectious diseases :—Scarlet fever, Stockton ljeatb, 1 ; typhoid fever, Halton 1 ; dyptheria Weston village 1. He also reported that there was an epidemic of measles at Stockton Heath. The infants school had 37 scholars absent chiefly through the disease. At the Boys School there was 27 away of which measles was again the, principal cause. The question of closing the schools would be considered before the next meeting. The Council recommended the closing of the schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF 'GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF 'GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Mr. T. Baxter pre- sided and there were also present :—Messrs. J. Spruce, T. Wright, S. Woodward. C. E. Linaker, P. Collins, W. M. Morris, A. flaming,- C. Roylance, J. Hallows, G. Egerton-Warburton, E. Ihich, E. Watson, H Mawdsley, T. Guest, Turner T. Antwis, G. Pilliuj, T. Wood. A. R. Norman, J. Stokes, J. H. Davies, -J. L. Jones, C. Horton, G. Watson, R. Nixon, J. Renshaw, W. Win- stanley, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), T. Cooke J. Pearson and W. R. de Prez (reliev- ing officers), and T. D. Rollinson (master). SIR JOHN BRUNNER'S' OFFER. The Clerk read a letter from Mr. Charles G. Evans, private secretory to Sir John Brunner. offering to pre- sent a copy of the summary of the report of the Poor Law Commission to each member of the Board. The Clerk said that he had acCepted the offer on behalf of the Board, also giving a list of the members.</p>
        <p>NURSE RESIGNS</p>
        <p>Nurse Lewis tendered her resignation, as she had been elected superintendent nurse at CongletonUnion, Sand- huh. The Clerk said that he had advert'sed for a 'Successor, and a sub-committee as empowered to make the appointment. NUMBER OF INMATES. Mr. Spruce reported that the number of inmates in the House was still high, there being 192 at present, as against 164 at the corresponding date last year. CHILDREN'S ACT. The Palliamenary Committee reported that the Clerk had read a lettet,from the Elome Office with res- pect to this Act, and it was resolved that it be an instruction to thc Clerk to take such steps as he may dem necessary (including the advertising by posters thioughout the Union, to make the provisions of that portion of the Act which related to the reception of infants reward known to all persons interested in such provision's.</p>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES. The Clerk presented the estimate and expenditure of the half year ending September 30th next. Common charges were set down at £3,200; county rate, £9,187 ; balance and contingency, £3,397 ; total £17,784. Deduct- ing the probable balance at Lady Day of £4,344, left the amount requrred from the overseers £13,440. The assessable value of the Union is £238,652. A rate of is. lid. in the £ would produce 113,424 and it was recommended that a call for £13,440 be made on the overseers, being a decrease of id. in the £ on the call for last half year.</p>
        <p>The estimate was adopted. TFNDER S.</p>
        <p>The following tenders for -supplies to the Workhouse were accepted :—Groceries :—J. G. Kidd and Co., Ltd., Frodsham ; bread, t)tJ. G. Kidd and Co., Frodsham ; tobacco and snu , Joh, Thomas, Runcorn; butchers meat, S. Hancock, Frodslam • wine, spirits, R. Rowson and Son, Warrington fuel', R. Evans and no , Hay- dock ; coke, Runcorn Gas Co. ; milk, S. S. Basnett, on Brook ; funerals and carrying for Runcorn arci or Whitly, Weston Point ; and for the workhouse, l. Scrog Preston Brok : oils and colours, A. De Prez, NI ()ore ; drancr, P. H. 'Wright, Hal ton,; clothing J. Hunter and Sous, Liverpool ; hoots, W. Bayley, liii- corn ; artisan lA. or joinery F. IZowe ; pininnimz, Rit!')itrd Prodslrini ; h, _T. Polh.rd torAsham ; bi icklayim, R. Gleave and Sous Krodsham ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. J. J. BILSBROUGH.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. J. J. BILSBROUGH.</p>
        <p>The news will be received with regret, especially in railway and commercial circles, of the death of Al L J. J. Bilsbrough, who passed away on Sunday morning at hiS residenee, Hill Top, Latchford, at the age of sixty-one years, after a Ivy brief illness. Mr. Bilsbrough commenced his railwoy career 49 years ago in the employ of the Laneashire and Yorkshire Railway Company at •Preston. He transferred his services to the London and North-Western Railway Company in 1860' and in September 1879 he went to Widnes where he held the position of goods agent and dockmaster until October, 1907. He was deeply respected by a large circle of friends and his retire- ment was mark&amp;l by the public presentation of an illuminated address and a cheque for FiLlO3 .from the manufacturers and traders of Widnes,and a handsome gold watch_from the Goods Department of the railway company. He leaves a widow and two daughters with whom' much sympathy is felt in their bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Wednesday afternoon at the Warrington Cemetery, there a very large number of friends were present to pay their last tribute of respect. .An impressive ervice was conducted at the kraveside by Mr. J. Hemery, of London, of the. British Branch of the Watchtower Bible iind Tract Society. The, chief marners were : Miss Bilsbrough ..(daughter), Mr. R. Bodle (son-in-law), Master Stanley Bodlei- (grandson), Captain Yates, of Runcorn (brother- in-law), Messrs. Robert and William Yates (nephews), Mr. and Mrs. Hill - (Nottingham), Mr. .and Mrs Palin (Warrington), Messrs. Straw, Smallman, Yardley, and Chamberlain,' Mrs. Harnpson, Mrs. Bowers, Miss ' Lloyd, and Miss Cares Well (Leigh). Others present included : Mr. A. Treacey (United Alkali Company Limited, W idn es), Mr, T. Glover: (representing Mr S. H. Hunt, London and North Western Railway, Garston), Mr. W. J. Powell (Gayston Dock), and Mr. J. Harrison (Stationmaster, Edge Hill, Liverpool). There were about thirty . representatives of the Watch Tower Bible and Tract Society, and many frientis journeyed from Liverpool and Manchester to be 'present at the funeral.. Floral tributes were sent by the following Mother and Flo," "Richard, Maggie, " Walter and Kittie," "Brother and Sister," members of the . church at Warrington, London and- North-Western Railway Goods and 'Canal Staff, Widnes, S. Whitlow, Mr. and Mrs. Leigh and family, and Mrs. C. R. Burton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>CHEAPER ELECTRIC LIGHT.</head>
        <p>CHEAPER ELECTRIC LIGHT.</p>
        <p>Wonders of a New Device.</p>
        <p>Saving of 70 Per Cent.</p>
        <p>A device to reduce electzic light bills by 70 per cent by/converiing high voltage electricity into low where there, is an filternating supply and substituting for lamps with a carbonised cotton thread neiv ones baying a metallic filament, was desbribed in these columnsiast Jung by Mr. Duncan Watson, M.I.E.E.</p>
        <p>Although then in its infancy and scarcely known to the public the systern was creating an immense stir in ,the electrical world. But statistics, save those of no experimental kind, wele difficult to obtain. Now, how- ever, Mr. Watson and other experts interested have obtained comparisons between the actual bills of con- sumers for the qnarter ending December last, during which they were making use of the new system, and the corresponding quarter in 1967, when they were not. "The figures," said Mr: Watson on Saturday "are revolutionary. They not only prove beyond question that an almost miraculous saying is Possible, but that electric light in everyday use can now be made far cheaper than incandescent gas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>OVER 70 PER CENT. SAVING.</head>
        <p>OVER 70 PER CENT. SAVING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Watson quoted the bills of three consanraers who had adopted the new system : Bills Sept.- Bills Sept.- Dec. 1907. Dec. 1908.</p>
        <p>Saving.</p>
        <p>Over 70 p.c.</p>
        <p>£3B 5 0 .£l 5 6 0 £lO 14 2 " In the first of these cases," said Mr. Watson," the cost of the transformer and new lamps which are neces_ sary was .E4O, in the second £l2 IGs., and in the third £l2. The lamps have a life of 2,000 burning hours. The transformers last practically for ever." The essentinls of the new system were briefly out- lined by Mr. Watson :---</p>
        <p>Transformer: A coil of insulated copper wire with a core and shell of special 'iron. The two ends are mclosed in cast-iron cases 'bolted together. A current coming from the mains, say at a pressure of 200 volts, is reduced by it to a voltage of 25.</p>
        <p>Metallic Filament Lamps: These consume exactly, without any wastage. thelow-pressurecurreiit delivered to them by the transformer.</p>
        <p>They do not waste heat—which means power—before givirig off light, as do those with a carbonised cotton thread. , Ewe remarkable calculations of the position in which the new system places electricity as compared with gas were made by Mr. Watson. The first concerns a country house :</p>
        <p>No. of Cost of Year's Lights. Supply. Bill. 100 4/- per 1,000ft.£36 Efs</p>
        <p>Illuminant.</p>
        <p>Electricity (old style) - 100...6-d. per unit... 265 12s Electricity (new style) ... 100...6d. per unit... 216 8s The second calculation concerned a great business house hr London.</p>
        <p>No. of Cost of Year's</p>
        <p>Lights. Supply. - Bill. Gas - per I,oooft. .22,351 Electricity (old style) 3,000 4d. per unit £4,200 Electricity (new style) "The attitude of the supply companies," said Sir. Watson, "has been remarkable. At first many main- tained that the swing of the new system was fictitious. To this the advisers of the consumers took strong exception. The result was the conference between the companies and thee advisers. Now the companies are actually offering to hire out transformers to their customers. They evidedtly realise that flieir only hope is to recoup themselves for big losses upon the bills of present consumers by vastly augmenting th&amp; list of their new ones.</p>
        <p>Illuminant.</p>
