      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</head>
        <p>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</p>
        <p>XXII.— If a 2s. per quarter duty on wheat is to benefit' the wheat farmer, how can it do so uniess it raises the price of wheat?</p>
        <p>Farmers want a corn duty because it enables them to get more for their corn. But if they get more for it the consumer must pay more for it. As Mr. Balfour put it at Edinburgh, on October 4th, 1904: "A protective policy, as I understand it, is a policy which aims at supporting or creating home industries BY RAISING HOME PRICES. The raising of prices is a necessary step towards the encouragement of an industry under a Protective system. The object of Protection is to encourage home industries. The means by which it attains that object is BY THE MANIPULATION OF A FISCAL SYSTEM TO RAISE HOME PRICES. If the home • prices are raised the industry is not encouraged. If the industry is encouraged, it is by the raising of prices. That is, in a nutshell, Protection properly understood."_ L. G. C. M.</p>
        <p>, 118511/... fo:if'4''. 1,1,1%1 !Sit e. __ ~,,...,i •1 \ to the ':,.• IR. if. . t EDITOR 4 ' i . , ~„.4.„._ ____----7.7-----.,__---4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Bridge-street Fifty Years Ago.</head>
        <p>Bridge-street Fifty Years Ago.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l would like to know how many shop-keepers there are in Bridge-street (on the side that has been re-built) at the present time that kept shops fifty years ago. There are very few, I think. I would like some old resident to recall the names (if he can). Or if we could have a list of the shop-keepers' names from the rate book of fifty years ago and compare them with i the names of the present shop-keepers, t would be very interesting to the young and old residents of the town.—Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>RESIDENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Some Curious Items.</head>
        <p>Some Curious Items.</p>
        <p>Sir,—During the last six or seven months some very curious events have occurred in connection with the Trade. • The closing days of September saw a grand effort in a Beer and Sandwich expedition to Hyde Park, London, and on the opening days of November we saw a contest for Municipal honours ' when the defeated candidate was a Publican, who unwilling to take his defeat philosophically, petitioned against his successful opponent. I have no interest in either candidate further than the interests of temperence and sobriety and I must say that a more undignified sordid story of treating and petty-cash I never heard in any court before, and now, the rate- payers at large will have to pay the cost. Surely the story unfolded at the election inquiry demonstrates once more the wisdom and necessity of closing,alllicensed premises during polling days at both Parliamentary and Municipal elections. .Still another curious item at the recent Licensing Sessions is the transfer of the Bridge Inn licence- to Orford while at the same time an objectioiiis raised to the only free house in the locality on the ground that they are too 'numerous. If Orford people like to have Greenall's or Walkers's ales in their midst I see no objection, but why should-a house be pbjected to because it is free and has been so Well Wnducted that the same tenant- has held more than tvienty licences for the same house. If licensed houses are redundant in the town which I believe they are, why not take the bull by the horns und reduce them where they are most numerous? Why was the recent transfer in lutter- market»street made when they might have refused it on the ground of. redundancy seeing that next door is another licensed house? In conclusion I</p>
        <p>would recommend our licensing_committee, instead of throwing the onus on the Chief Constable of making out a list of objections, to boldly decide to refuse licenses to all houses .where the tenant has not the right to purchase his stock where he likes—in fact they should all be free houses.—l remain B,:c. PITIL SQI.7OD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Tariff Reform and Intemperance in the Same Boat.</head>
        <p>Tariff Reform and Intemperance in the Same Boat.</p>
        <p>Sir,-r--Your correspondent Labour Omnia Vincit still seems to stand by his deduction from Lord Brassey's experiment which showed that teetotal gangs of work- men did fifteen per cent. More work than alcoholic drinkers. His words were "-that proves clearly then that if teetotalism be adopted we .shall have another fifteen per cent. added to the nine per cent. unemployed:" If this be so it is a perfectly legitimate inference, as I pointed out last week, to say that by increasing the number of alcoholic drinkers those at present unemployed could be found work. Last week I showed how erronaus was our friend's deduction by the method so dear to old Euclid of reductio ad absurdum. This week I propose to try and show that the belief which he shares with Ramsey Macdonald is erroneous by direct argument. The point is of con- siderable importance because, as I implied last week, , those who hold the view of our friend are at one with the argument constantly put forward by the President of the Warrington Tariff Reform League And his associates. The argument is based on the assumption that the call upon labour is a fixed limited quantity, and hence our friend's contention is that it is well to spread this limited amount of Work as much as possible by inefficiency, intemperance, that all may be employed. The Tariff Reformer, proceeding on the same assumption, sets to work to inrease the call on labour by making work" by the introduction of new industries even although these industries can only be made profitable by raising the selling prices by the help of a tariff. The after effect of economical production in the production of wealth, and wasteful production in the lessening of the same, is ignored by both parties. Your correspondent appears to consider that any extra efficiency increases profit and, this only, and that the increased wealth does not come back to the worker as consumer. The demand upon labour depends primarily upon our wants, but this is limited by our ability to satisfy these wants. The ability to satisfy wants depends upon our having the wherewithal to give in exchange to those who have the things we want. The demand therefore to make these things depends upon the posi- tion of those in want to 'give something in exchange. No doubt up to this point your correspondent will agree, but 'if I gather his meaning aright his idea - is that any increase of wealth through efficiency goes only to one class of the community, namely the capitalist, and being retained by him, and not shared by the workers, no extra demand is created by those who want, and whose means are limited If I have interpreted his meaning right I join issue with him on this point, Take the case of improved machinery, the immediate effect of this is to enable a manufacturer to increase his output which he does by lowering his price. His competitors, ever on the outlook to cheapen production, get the same machine or adopt some other means of increasing efficiency,and are able to sell at the same reduced price, and find it to their interest to do so. The effect of.this is that go far as profits are concerned in a very short fime they revert to the normal, the same as they were before the introduction of the machine. In a word. the result of any improvement, any economy in pro- duction through competition is handed on to the con- sumer. The result of this is that to the extent any article is Cheapened to the consumer by this amount is released just so much spending power or exchange value. Suppose by the introduction of machinery a man's pair of boots is reduced from eleven shillings to ten, the buyer able to give eleven shillings for his boots and, getting them at ten has a shilling at liberty to satisfy some other want which he spends upon some other article, and thus increases the call upon labour. If on the other hand by inefficiency in production, or say by the introduction of a tariff on leather and boots, the price he has to pay for his boots be twelve shillings he has either to go without, or -withdraw the extra shilling from some article he would otherwise buy, and thus lessen the demand on labour for the article he has to go without. In the first instance the introduction of machinery would displace a certain amount of labour in making boots, but this would be made up by the additional call upon labour through increased spend- ing power of the consumer. The two would balance each other if the amount of displacement were the measure of the gain in wealth production. But this is not so. If by the introduction of a machine the saving were only sufficient to balance the saving of labour the machine would not; be employed because there would be no benefit. It is because ,machines *aye more than the anicLunt paid to displace labour</p>
        <p>tilt they are installed. In the case of the boots in question sixpence may represent the labour displace- ment and sixpnce the gain. But the consumer has an extra shilling which he uses in satisfying some new want which employs labour in its satisfaction. The net result is therefore a labour displacement of sixpence and an additional call on labour of one shilling, and further the satisfaction of an additional want in the case of the consumer.</p>
        <p>It its not clear then that efficiency produces in the long run a greater demand for labour, and that efficiency the result of intemperance or any other thing reduces the call upon labour. In the same way, work created by introducing industries which are not naturally efficient by the aid of tariffs must in the long run ,(and not a very long .run either), lessen the call upon labour. The short sightdd view of the worker who favours inefficiency and that of the Tariff Reformer who thinks that by." making work" by a dear form of production, he will improve employment, is doing, and is likely to do, incalculable harm to the workers of this country. It is all import- ant that every effort should be made to get those (whose sincerity we do not, call in question) to look a little deeper and a little further than their vision has so far carried them.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Penketh, March 15th, 1909. The Territorial Force and " The South LancashiPes."</head>
        <p>Penketh, March 15th, 1909. The Territorial Force and " The South LancashiPes."</p>
        <p>Sir;—The Territorial Force has occupied so large a share of public consideration in the last 12 months, that I think the following figures may be. of interest. Warrington is the home, of the South Lancashires, their Depot is here, and the Memorial to their dead is here. At the time of stress, Warrington acknow- ledged and sealed the bond by its fund and help for the wives left behind, and by its help (still continued) for the widows ; and further, by its response to each call for Volunteers, Warrington .is permanently identified with the South Lancashires. _ _ • _</p>
        <p>In the Territorial Force the South Lancashire Regi- ment has two battalions, viz., the 4th and sth. They give their name to a Brigade. Two more battalions are necessary to complete the Brigade. The other two are the 9th and 10th (Scottish) Battalions, The King's (Liverpool Regiment) The full strength of each of the font- battalions should be :-29 officers, 980 N.C.O.'s aid men. ' The present strength of the South Lancashire Brigade is as follows : Shortage. • Offi'rs. Men.,offi'rs. Men.</p>
        <p>4th Bn. South Lancs. Regt. (Warrington and Newton) 22 779 7 201 sth Bn. South Lancs. Regt. (St. Helens and Widnes) 28 980 1 Nil. 9th Bn. Liverpool Regt (Liverpool) loth Bn. Liverpool Regt (Liverpool) Thus three battalions of the South Lancashire Brigade have already achieved tile feat of reaching to their full strength with the exception of a few vacancies for officers. It only remainsl for Warrington and Newton to attain its full complement in order to give the South Lancashire Brigade the unique position of a Territorial Brigade already arrived at full strength. The Warrington Corps Has recruited very fairly well during thd last few weeks, but I am sure that the facts and figures which I have just given will lead to a still further determined effort, if they are only generally known. I am sure the feeling will be as strongly held throughout Warrington and district,as it is by myself, that the 4th Battalion must not remain the only one that is not complete in the Brigade. The power of the Press in Londonf and more recently in Liverpool, has provW of such unparalleled assistance to the Territorial Force, that I venture to make a special appeal, Yr. Editor, for help from your pages, to raise immediately the 200 men that are necessary to bring the Warrington Corps up to its establishment, and up to the strength of its sister battalions, and so make the South Lancashire Brigade complete.—Yours obediently, J. A. EDELSTEN, Colonel. Secretary, West Lancashire Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>"THE REDEMPTION." Warrington Musical Society's Concert</head>
        <p>"THE REDEMPTION." Warrington Musical Society's Concert</p>
        <p>On Thursday night the Warrington iMusical Society gave their last concert of the season in the Parr Hall. They chose for their performance Gounod's "Redemption," which is particularly suitable to this time of the year. It consists of a prologue and three parts, and is rather long for ,the purposes of an ordinary concert. For this reason, probably, the music was hurried in parts. Beyond this there was little to criticise in the rendering of the theme. The concert was a particularly inspiring , one, and well worthy of the high reputation which this Society has won for itself. It was so good that one felt sorry that so few people were there to hear it, for, unfor- tunately, the attendance was poor. One special feature of the performance was the pre- sence of the boys /of the Warrington Church Choir, who formed the Celestial Choir, which introduces so effectively the first part of the Trilogy, . announcing Christ's divine purpose. "I leave My heavenly home, To atone for transgression."</p>
        <p>The Trilogy proceeds thus : Part I. begins with the story of tile Crucifixion, the character of Christ being admirably rendered by the principal bass, Mr. Robert Burnett. The music of the march to Cabiary, which recurs at intervals, contrasts strangely With one's modern notions of the solemnity of our Lord's last days. There is a buoyancy and worldliness about it which makes us realize, as only the genius of a Gounod can make us realize. how little ,the multitude understood the far-reaching significance and import of that occasion. Then follows the lamentation of the women, and Christ's remonstrance :--- '</p>
        <p>"Ye 4aughters of Israel weep not for me." Then come all too quickly changing movements, admirably interpreted, which represent the confusion of purposes amongst the assembled crowds, the mock- ing of enemies, the lamentations of friends, Mary's anguish expressed in a beautiful solo " While my watch I am keeping," magnificently rendered by Madame Lily Fairhurst, the attitude of the unpenitent and penitent thieves, Christ praying for his tormentors and then the solo expressing pardon to the penitent : "In very truth eay I to thee,to-day shalt thou be with Me in Paradise." Then comes Christ's death, and the orchestra depicts the natural phenomena of the scene which follows :—darkness, earthquake, the confusion. Part II deals with the Resurrection and Ascension, It opens with the chorus, supported by the organ and brass instruments. Four trumpets placed in pairs behind the orchestra are a novel feature of the music. The Narrator (Mr. Cynlais Gibbs, tenor), takes up the thread of the story and describes how the holy women visit, the Sepulchre. An angel proclaims the resurrection of Jesus. A gradually increasing volume of music, enriched by the strains of a harp, lead up to the culminating words "He is risen." Then Jesus appears and addresses the women and the two narrators (Mr. Cynlais Gibbs tenor, and Mr. Robert Burnett, bass) sing of the scene which follows. • A trio for women is beautifully rendered by Miss Marie Houghton and Miss Dorothy Truslove, sopranos, and Madame Lily Fairhurst, contralto. The finest part of the oratorio is reached towards the end of the second part in a sequence of choruses repre- senting the Terrestrial and Celestial choirs. Those lines beginning : " Unfold, ye portals everlasting," were rendered with remarkable effect, and the theme ends in a burst of song of unusual force and beauty. The music of Part 111 is different. It is hushed and calm, suggesting peace and steadfastness, patience and endurance. The drama is over, the blessings of holiness are to follow. The soprano (Miss Marie Houghton) voices a hymn of thanksgiving and rejoic- ing, and a voice from heaven (soprano) sends forth the apostles on their mission of glad tidings. Mr. Crossley is much to be congratulated on the success of this oratorio. His orchestra' seemed to enter into the theme with feeling and enthusiasm, and rendered it with a power unusual in a perform- ance of amateurs. Considerable credit is doubtless due to Mr. R. H. Pearson, the instrumental leader of the orchestra. The chorus, always good in the Warrington Musical Society, seemed to surpass itself, and gave the impression of having more voices in it than is usually the case.</p>
        <p>A certain old dame, who is well known to fame For preferring a shoe for a cottage, Her children would spank and to bye-bye would yank, 11 they ever complained of their pottage! Now this shows she was wise, and it's safe to surmise In a household so strict and housewifely, If a child had a cough it was treated right off, With Woods' Peppermint Cure—quick and livaly.-Adv</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>SEVEN NEW MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>SEVEN NEW MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>PORTRAITS AND BIOGRAPHIES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the County Sal-keeper, Preston, placed the following gentlemen upon the Commission of the Peace for the borough of Warrington: - ' John Arthur Edelsten: Norton Lodge, Halton, Cheshire, colonel in H.M. Territorial Forces..</p>
        <p>George William Joseph, 21, Bold-street, Warring- ton, doctor of medicine.</p>
        <p>James Thomas Locker, 12, Salisbury-street, wire manufacturer.</p>
        <p>John' Bassett Naden, 111, Liverpool-road, medical practitioner.</p>
        <p>Thomas Pemberton, 115, Bewsey-road, estate agent.</p>
        <p>John Upson, 8, Lodge-lane, schoolmaster. William Thomas Williams, 8, Arpley-street, draper</p>
        <p>Da. JOHN BASSE'TT Nan EN was born at Hartington, Derbyshire, in 1859. He was educated privately and after a fourteen ,years' commercial experience in. Manchester he took the medical course at. Owen's College, obtain- ing the diplomas, M.R.C.S. and 1..4.R.C.P. He came to Warrington</p>
        <p>in 1891, and settled</p>
        <p>down at his present address, 111, Liverpool-road, in 1904. Dr. Naden is a close student of English literature and ha S given papers on literary subjects before • the Warrington Society and the Warrington Literary and Philosophical Society. He is also a member of the Warrington Field Club. In politics he is a Liberal but has never taken an active part in public ,affairs. In 1891, he married Miss Pitman, of Shepton Mnllet, Somerset- shire.</p>
        <p>Col. JOHN ARTHUR ; EDELSTUN, who belongs to One of the oldest Warrington families,and is especially well known and popular in Terri- torial circles, was born in 1863 and is the son of Mr. J. Malin Edelsten, J.P. Joining the War- rington Volunteer Corps in 1887, he passed in ther less than the usual time from seccanl-lieu-</p>
        <p>tenant to Lieutenant- Colonel, having in the meantime also held twb posts on the staff of a Volunteer Brigade. He holds a number Of military certificates, many of them being " specials." He was twice local hon. secretary to the Royal Military Tournament, when held in Manchester, and served either singly or conjointly as hon. secretary- of the Volunteer Officers' Association of Manchester and District for about ten years. His colonelcy of the Warrington Corps began in 1903, but on the passing of the Territorial Forces Act (under which the Volunteers ceased to exist under their old name) he relinquished the command in order to take up the secretaryship of the West Lancashire Association. During Colonel Edelsten's command, the Volunteer buildings at Warrington were greatly increased and the corps has reached a state of efficiency which is a source of pride to the town. Before military matters occupied such a large share of his time, Colonel Edelsten held various hon. secretaryships,. including that of the Literary and Philosophic,al Society, and for eleven years he organ- ized the University Extension Lectures in Warrington. At the present time he is President of the Warringten Canine Society. In 1892 he married Miss J. H. Dun, daughter of the late Mr. John Dun, the eminent banker.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN UPSON, who has long been promin- ently associated with the teaching profession, was born at Fairham, near Portsmouth, in 1862. He received his early educa- tion at Southampton and became one of the first pupil teachers under the Southampton School Board. Alter his train- ing at Westminster College he was appointed ,master of the township school at Rainow, near Macclesfield. Mr. Upson came to Warrington in 1886 to take up the headmastership of Silver-street schools, which appointment he at present holds. Such is the confidence reposed in him by his fellow-teachers, that he has twice been elected president of the Warrington Branch of the N.U.T., and he acted as secretary for six years and treasurer for eight years. On resigning the secretaryship about three years ago, he wa4 the recipient of a token of regard in the shape of an illuminated address. Quite recently Mr. Upson succeeded to the high office of President of the Lancashire County Association of the National Union of Teachers. For some time he served in the Town Council' as a representative of Orford Ward, but when the Education ,Act of 1902 came into force he was debarred, as a member of the teaching profession, from continuing his membership. He has, however, represented the teachers on the Education Committee for the past six years and the Council retained his services as a co-opted member of the Museum and Gymnasium Committees. Mr. Upson is a strong Liberal and a convincing speaker on political questions. He is prominently identified with the Wesleyan, Methodist Church, being an ex-circuit steward, a local preacher, and the leader of a large men's Bible class at Silver-street. In 1886 he married Miss Elizabeth A. Cragg, youngest daughter of the late Mr: John Cragg, of Tower Hill, Rainow, Macclesfield.</p>
        <p>Ma. WILLIAM THOMAS WILLIAMS was born at the, village of Sam in South Carnarvonshire, in the year 1865. He was educated at the Bottwnog Grammar School and after serving an apprenticeship to the drapery business a t Pwllheli, came to Lanca- shire in 1885. For twelve years he was at Bolton</p>
        <p>and then he , came to</p>
        <p>Warrington where he opened a shop in Sankey-street. In 1904 he removed to the larger premises which he now occupies. Mr. Williams iS prominently connected .with the Welsh Church, Crosfield-street, and holds the office of deacon and trdasurer. He has been a, member of the execu- tive committee of the Warrington Tradesmen's Asso- ciation from its formation and this year the members honoured hint by electing him to the presidency. He is alp a director of the Warrington Tradesmen's Plat J Glass Insurance Co. Mr. Williams does not take an active part in political affairs, but his sympathies are understood to be on the Liberal side. In ISb7 he maried Miss Jones, of Knutsford-road, Warrington, a well-known vocalist.</p>
        <p>DR. GEORGE WILLIAM JosErn was, born in Liverpool 59 years ago, being the son,of the late Mr. Neild Joseph, chemist, of that city. He received his education at the Kirkbean Grammar School, Dumfries, and the Liverpool Institute, and was afterwards a medical student at the Royal Infirmary and</p>
        <p>lovergity College</p>
        <p>Liverpool. He obtained the qualifications of M.R.C.S. and L.R.C.P., later pro- ceeding to the M.D. of Durham University. After holding the appointment of junior house surgeon at Birkenhead Hospital, Dr. Joseph came to Warrington in 1874'as house surgeon at the Infirmary. He held that post for five years and was then appointed honorary surgeon, which important office he at present holds. It is interesting to note that Dr. Joseph was the last house surgeon at the old Infirmary in Buttermarket-street and the first at the present building in Kendrick- street. For seven yeaf4s he was medical 'officer of health for the borough and he was the first medical superintendent of the Fever Hospital and public analyst for Warrington. Dr. Joseph is a Conservative in politics and four years ago he was returned, to to the Town Council, after a contest, as a representative of St. Austin's Ward.</p>
        <p>colony last Boxing Day. Mr. Pemberton commenced work on August Bank Holiday, 1874,at Messrs. Monks Hall and Co., Ltd., and remained there for three years. He was with Mr Wm. Kinsey, estate agent, for the next four yearsd and in 1881 he went to Messrs T. and J. L. Tanstal and Co.'s, Bewsey Chambers, where he now holds a responsible position in the firm. Mr. Pemberton is a staunch .Liberal, and has taken an active interest in the party organizations. He became assistant secretary of the Liberal Club in 1880, and in the following year he was elected hon secretary. In March, 1908. he was appointed hon. treasurer of the Liberal Association. Mr. Pemberton is a • keen football enthusiast and has been treasurer of the Warrington Football, Club since 1882. Be married a daughter of the late Alderman John- Roberts, J.P., and they celebrated their silver wedding last year.</p>
        <p>G4,01.111C ill 0 r JAMES THOMAS LOCKER is the eldest son of the late Mr. Thos. Locker, wire cloth manufacturer and originator of the well- known firm of Messrs. Thos. Locker and Co., Ltd., and was born on September 2nd, 1863. He has been engaged in the wife-weaving trade all 'his life and is now Chairman of directors of</p>
        <p>Wthe Company. Coun- cillor Locker takes a great interest in politics, is an ardent Liberal and two years ago was appointed hon. secretary to the Warrington Liberal Association— an office which he fills with credit to himself and satisfaction to the members. He has for many years been closely identified with the Warrington Parish Church and is at present a sidesman and a manager of the National schools. He was elected to the Town Council unopposed as a representative of Fairfield Ward in November, 1907, and has done much quiet but useful work. Councillor Locker is a member of the Lodge of Lights of Freemasons. In 1890 be married Miss Harriet Coulson, daughter of Mr. John Coulson, superintendent of the Midland Locomotive Department, Carnforth. Councillor</p>
        <p>Locker is at present representing his firm's interests in South Africa.</p>
