      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>ORFORD WARD ELECTION.</head>
        <p>ORFORD WARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Halliwell Successful.</p>
        <p>By the unseating of Mr. Peter Peacock a vacancy was created on the Town. Council for Orford Ward. The contest to fill it took place on Tuesday, when the candidates' names on the voting paper were :—Mr James" Halliwell, boot and shoe dealer, Market-street (Liberal) ; Mr. James ilampson. general labourer, Garibaldi-street (Socialist) ; and Mr. W. McNeil Sloane (Independent). The latter gentleman's name appeared through a misunderstanding, for he did not seek election. The event was a very quiet one, but during the day the polling booth in Silver-street was visited by Aldermen Bennett and Smethurst and Messrs. C. E. Parker and Thomas Pemberton. Very little election literature was issued by either candi- date. Mr. Halliwell emphasized the need for half- penny tram-fares, and. Mr. Hanipson advocated municipal ownership and control, the direct employ- ment of labour by the Corporation, and the enforcing of a trade union rate of wages in contracts. In the counting room when it was known that Mr. Halliwell had won, the MAxott congratulated him, and sympathised with Mr. Hampson. Councillor Halliwell proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and the Town Clerk,and added he sympathised with Mr.Hampson and wished him better luck next time. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. HAMPSON in seconding the motion said every I consideration had been accorded to the candidates. He was sorry they hadlost, for he wanted to win.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>POLL DECLARED,</head>
        <p>THE POLL DECLARED,</p>
        <p>There was a considerable crowd in front of the Town Hall to hear the poll declared, The Mayor amid cheers proclaimed the result as follows : Mr. Jams Hallivvell (Lib.) Mr. James Hampson (Soc.) Mr. W. McNeil Sloane (Ind.)</p>
        <p>Majority</p>
        <p>Councillor HALLI WELL speaking from the Town Hall steps, under some emotion, said " I thank you for your votes and your assistance to-day, and I will do my duty." (Cheers). Mr. HOPSON, who had a good reception, said that was the second time be had had the opportunity of standing before them, and had had the misfortune to submit to defeat. Notwithstanding, he would have</p>
        <p>the workers of the town to understand that if their interests were to be served, they must look to their own party, to the candidate of their choice, and not to the Liberal Party. (Cheers). During the past week he had been through Orford Ward and had his principles submitted to the severest test and criticism, and he contended, with, all due respect to the man who had been declared their councillor, that if he could not defend the principles of Liberalism he could not defend the principles of the workers. So far as Mr. Halliwell as a Liberal candidate was concerned they had absolutely nothing to fear, but had to contend against other influences that were brought to bear upon the voters. Therefore it had not been a victory for Mr. Halliwell. (A. Voice : " A good 'un.") It had been a victory-for others. Therefore they were not satisfied with the result and they would go forward. He thanked all who had voted for him and worked for him that day. (Cheers.) The successful candidate then proceeded in a motor car to Silver-street, where he thanked all those who had ivorked for him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIAL OFFICERS V. SERGEANTS.—A billiard match between the officers and sergeants of the 4th South Lancashire Regiment was played last Monday at headquarters, and resulted in a victory for the sergeants by 87 points. The officers and sergeants sat down to a hot-pot about 10 o'clock, and a very pleasant evening was spent. Score :</p>
        <p>SERGEANTS. OFFICERS. Col.-Sergt. Jolley Qr.-Master Wilcock ... 100 Major B. Fair°lough 92 Sergt.-Major Ingram ... 100 Capt. P. N, Avery 99 Sergeant Ashton 9l Capt. R Roberts Sergeant Knight lOO Lieut. Monks 69 Sergeant J. Allardice... 100 Capt. C.L. Williamson 52 Sergeant D. Plinston... 100 Lieut. E. Fairclough 45 Col.-Sergt. Russell Colour-Sergeant CH&amp; 51 Lieut. E. Fa,irclough 100 Sergeant Roberts 29 Colonel P. Fairclough 100 Bandmaster Richardson 95 Catitain Picton -100 Sergeant Richardson ... 100 Lieutenant Woods 74 Sergeant Dagnall. Sergeant Moss lOO Lieutenant Henshall 51 Sergeant Mellor Cblour-Sergt. Thomas 100 Lieutenant Adams ... 59 - 1301</p>
        <p>1388</p>
        <p>For all Chronic Chest Complaints, take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>THE REV. E. C. E. CARLETON'S EDUCATION BILL CARRIED.</p>
        <p>After an exciting debate, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton's Education Bill, which seeks to maintain the denomina- tional system, was carried by 44 votes to 16 at the meeting of the Parliataentary Debating Society on Thursday evening.</p>
        <p>Mr. H: J. WESTBROOK (C.. Knut.iford) in a fighting speech, supported the Bill chiefly because of its generosity—(Opposition laughter)—generosity which it erred in conferring upon men who had the least right to it. (A Voice: ' Shame.") No serious objecton had been made to the Bill. The Member for Southamp- ton (Mr. Upson) had lost himself among the elements ot ordinary English Church history. They had often , heard of the schoolmaster abroad," but on this occasion they had an example of " the schoolmaster at sea." Admitting for the sake of argument that the hon. member's construction of the will of Sir Thomas Boteler was correct, for whom was he makiug a claim to the -endowments; for himself or for some other men? He (Mr. Westbrook) was prepared to admit that if the Church of England was not entitled to those endow- ments, the Roman Catholics were. Mr. Westbrook dealt at length with the question of trust deeds and contended that in the case of some chapels the doctrines taught were out of harmony with the trust deeds, and that gentlemen endowed with the propensities of the cuckoo were taking the nests built by other birds for quite different progeny. The member for Ashford (Rev. C. Harvey Cook) had trotted out that old "Johnny Walker" that Nonconformists bad been prevented from building schools by the action of the Church. What had the Nonconformists done since the year 1793 when all prohibitive Acts against Dissenters building schools were abolished? He suggested the answer was that they found in 1870, that they could have schools built for them at the general expense, in which they got all they desired. They had no difficulty in getting land for chapels and therefore they :-:ould have got it for schools. The Bin he contended, gave equal treatment and freedom; it robbed no-ono, and injured no-one. (Ministerial applause.)</p>
        <p>'Mr. E. ENGLAND (L., Warrington), said the Opposi- tion objected to the Bill because they believed it was a step backward. They were distinctly opposed to denominational teaching of any. kind in the schools. What they wanted was to establish national schools. He believed on fundamental principles, the Roman Catholic Church, the Anglican Cpurch and the Free Churches were practically one and the same, and it was an absolute scandal that in the twentieth century the people of this country could not agree upon a national system and settle once and for all this education bogey which was caused by introducing the religious element so much in the schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. G. TAYLOR (C., Petersileld) asked: Where would education have been in the past if it had not been for the Church of England? The Bill stood for impartiality and toleration, and there was not a single point on which it could be claimed that it gave the Church of England preferential treatment. Mr. D. WYN'NE JONES (Leader of the Opposition), in a stirring presentation of the case against the Govern- ment, said the Bill was incapable of amendment. They could pass it through the purgatorial fires as much as they liked, but they would never make a good Bill of it. The measure perpetuated theological tests and would establish sectarianism. It would make the common school the battle ground of sectarianism. The Opposition believed that before there could be any improvement, we must have a thoroughly national system of education.As to the non-compliance with the teaching in trust deeds,what about the High Church party in the Established Church? What about the Prayer Book and the subject of the Mass? The Bill was a striking revelation of the encroaching and aggressive character of the Church to which the Minister of Education belonged. The trend of events was driving men of all parties and creeds to say that the one solution of the question was the secular solution. "If you have so little faith in your religion, and in your Church and principles,"said the leader of the Opposition "that those principles cannot be inculcated without impeding the freedom of the day school teacher, the sooner it is svept away the better. Is it not a fact that you are afraid the adoption of the secular solution would mean that it would make the Disestablishment of the Anglican Church easier. Are you not less concerned About the maintenance of your schools than retaining the power you have over the teacher himself? I believe that if the National Union of Teachers would only make up their minds, they would settle the question once for all." (Applause). The Minister of Education (Rev. E. C. E. Carleton) maintained that the Bill was an honest and fair Bill. The Government dld not offer Roman Catholics any- thing they did not offer Free Churahmen., They made the same provision for denominational as for undenominational teaching. They gave all the same right of entry and they showed in all cases the same regard for the ownership of property and for the feelings of the parents. As a matter of history, such a trans- action as Mr. Upson supposed, with regard to trusts and endowments, never took place.</p>
        <p>, On a vote being taken the Bill was carried by 44 votes to 16.</p>
        <p>CAPTAIN CROSFIELD'S BILL FOR MILITARY</p>
        <p>TRAINING.</p>
        <p>Next Tuesday evening Captain G. R. Oros&amp;ld, mem- ber for Rochdale, will introduce a Bill to make com- pulsory the military training of the youth of Great Britain. It provides:—</p>
        <p>. - - That every Briton shall undergo a sufficient amount of military training._</p>
        <p>That ph-ysical drill of a military character shall be incorporated in the curriculum of all day schools That at the age af 18 every able-bodied youth\ who elects to join the Infantry shall undergo four months Military Training in Camp. Such Youths as elgct to join other branches of the Service shall,when necessary, receive a longer period of training, but in no case shall the time of such training exceed six months. That all young men so trained shall join their local Territorial Battalions as at present.organised, and shall remain members of the same for not less than three years thus, receiving further training under canva amounting to 14 days in each year. That all men so trained shall be liable to be 'called out for Home defence only. up to the age of 30 years in a time of grave emergency. That the foregoing conditions shall not apply to (a.) the only sons of widows (b) to such as are the only support of families (o) ministers of religion (d) mem- bers of the mercantile marine, royal navy and • regular army.</p>
        <p>That all employers o labour shall be required to keep open the places of such of their workpeople as are absent from their employment during, and in conse- quence of, such military training. That no Commis4ion shall be given to any man who has not served in the ranks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>RAGGED SCHOOL AND MISSION.</head>
        <p>RAGGED SCHOOL AND MISSION.</p>
        <p>Treat to Poor Children.</p>
        <p>A tea and entertainment to 500 children was given by Mr. James Dodds, president of the Warrington Ragged School and Mission on Monday evening, at the Mission school, Bank-street. After the children had been served with a substantial tea by a large staff of workers superintended by Mr. Skelland, they were entertained by a powerfnl gramaphone under the manipulation of Miss Dodds and Miss Marjorie Dodds. The selections were very happy as the children were able to join leartily in,the various choruses. The presence of Mr. Walter Moores who had generously consented to con- tribute some humorous items greatly added to the enjoyment of the young people, and he not only proved to be in good form, but his genuine pleasure in delighting the crowded audience gratified all concerned. Master Plumpton, of Penketh, and several of the children of the school also took part in the proceedings, and before the close Mr. Dodds addressed a few words of kindly advice and encouragement to the young people. He asked them to be serious, to be diligent in their early days, in what he called the golden time of their lives, while their minds were plastic and easily taught; to be attentive, assiduous, respectful to their teachers. Looking upon that denoely packed audience he saw for the youngest boy "—and girl opportunities for advancement, which did not exist when be was a lad. Without exaggeration there was open in these clays to the poor- est students the chance of attaining by scholarships and similar provisions, to high positions and foremost places in the battle of life. Let them not seek mere selfish prominence, but work from love of work, and of duty, and, more than all, be besought them to love and help their parents who in many cases, with heavy hearts and scanty means, were probably that very night struggling for their own and their little ones' existence,it might be in despair and hopelessness. After a vote of thanks, and bearey and prolonged cheers for the President and ,the Misses Dodds, an enjoyable evening, which i will not soon be forgotten, was brought to a close by the singing of the doxology and the pro- nouncement of the Benediction.</p>
        <p>Warrington Violinist at Hanley. Master Bradford Barton of Warrington gave violin solos at Hanley on the 11th inst., for the National Staffs District Choral Society, and his performances are favouiably commented upon by the Press. He was first heard along with Miss Evangeline Florence in Gounod's " Seranade," the violin obligato being rendered with excellent taste and skill, both singer and player being called and re-called. He also con- tributed the' last two movements of the Mendelssohn concerto for violin; "An Adagio" from the ninth concerto by Beriot ; 'and a "Bolero "by Daucla. In answer to an encore he played a delightful" Berceuse " of Goddard. His playing throughout was very fine, the.technique and feeling displayed being very remark- able in one so young.</p>
        <p>For Children's tacking Cough at night, Wood's Great Peppermint Cure. is. 11. 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-20_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
